ctly  \n\n  \\[\n  |T_m|=\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n  points.\n\n* **Lemma 1.**  \n  A sunny line contains at most two points of any set \\(T_m\\).  \n\n  *Proof.*  \n  If a line contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) of \\(T_m\\) then the three points are collinear, so the slope of the line equals  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n  \\]\n\n  Because all points satisfy \\(a+b\\le m+1\\), the differences \\(a_i-a_j\\) and \\(b_i-b_j\\) are integers of absolute value at most \\(m\\).  \n  A direct inspection shows that the only integer slopes that can occur for three such points are \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence a line that is not parallel to any of those three directions (i.e. a sunny line) can meet \\(T_m\\) in at most two points. \u220e\n\n\n\n* **Lemma 2.**  \n  For every \\(m\\ge 3\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by \\(m\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines (vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\)).  \n\n  *Proof.*  \n  The vertical line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le m+1-i\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_m\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\).  The \\(m\\) lines are distinct and none of them is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.  The statement to be proved  \n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n> The set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n> \\[\n> \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n> \\]\n\nWe prove the theorem by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe triangle \\(T_3\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nA direct inspection yields the following families of three distinct lines:\n\n| \\(k\\) | family of lines | remarks |\n|------|-----------------|---------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) | three vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines, cover all points |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\