o a sunny one  \n\nSuppose \\(\\ell\\) is a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions\n\\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nPick a point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that lies **only** on \\(\\ell\\) (such a point exists because otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be redundant and could be removed, contradicting the minimality of the family).  \n\nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by a line \\(\\ell'\\) that\n\n* passes through \\(P\\),\n* has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (hence is sunny),\n* is distinct from all other lines of the family.\n\nBecause \\(\\ell'\\) is no longer parallel to the forbidden directions, it may lose some points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously covered by \\(\\ell\\).  \nFor each lost point we add a **new** non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) that passes through that point and does not coincide with any existing line.  \nSince each lost point can be covered by a single such line, the total number of added lines is at most the number of lost points.\n\nCrucially, the operation increases the number of sunny lines by **exactly one** (the line \\(\\ell'\\)) while increasing the total number of lines by at most one (the extra non\u2011sunny line needed for the lost points).  \nTherefore, starting from a family of \\(n\\) lines with \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain a family of at most \\(n+1\\) lines with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines that still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nIf the number of added lines is zero we already have a family of \\(n\\) lines with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines; otherwise we have a family of \\(n+1\\) lines with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  In both cases we have produced a configuration for the same triangle \\(T_n\\) with one more sunny line.\n\nApplying this operation repeatedly we can raise the number of sunny lines by up to three (once for each forbidden direction) while never exceeding \\(n+1\\) total lines.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Construction for \\(T_{n+1}\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\) from (2) and the corresponding family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\).\n\n1. **Step A (preserve \\(k\\)).**  \n   Add the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\).  \n   We obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}\\) covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n+1\\) total lines.\n\n2. **Step B (increase the number of sunny lines).**  \n   Apply the \u201cturn a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one\u2019\u2019 operation to a non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}\\).  \n   This yields a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(1)}\\) covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines and at most \\(n+2\\) total lines.  \n   If the total number of lines becomes \\(n+2\\), we simply discard any line that is now redundant (there is always at least one redundant line because we have \\(n+2\\) lines covering only \\(|T_{n+1}|=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}\\) points, and \\(\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}<2(n+2)\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  After discarding we are left with exactly \\(n+1\\) lines.\n\n3. **Step C (repeat).**  \n   Repeating Step\u202fB up to three times we obtain families with \\(k,\\,k+1,\\,k+2,\\,k+3\\) sunny lines, each consisting of exactly \\(n+1\\) distinct lines and covering \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nSince the induction hypothesis supplies families for every \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\), after the three repetitions we obtain families for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk'\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,(n+1)-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the existence part of the theorem is proved for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nLet \\(m\\ge 3\\).  Suppose we have \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_m\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n*If \\(k=m\\).*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_m\\); therefore the total number of covered points is at most \\(2m\\).  From (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|T_m|=\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}>2m\\qquad (m\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  Hence \\(k=m\\) is impossible.\n\n*If \\(k=m-1