ts \\(T_m\\) in at most two points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_m\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nBecause the points are collinear, the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\n\nAll coordinates are positive integers and satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le m+1\\).  \nA short case analysis on the possible integer differences \\(a_j-a_i\\) and \\(b_j-b_i\\) shows that the only slopes that can occur for three such lattice points are \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Hence a line whose slope is none of these can contain at most two points of \\(T_m\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor every \\(m\\ge 3\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by the \\(m\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le m+1-i\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_m\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(i\\).  The \\(m\\) lines are distinct and none of them is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Statement to be proved  \n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\).  The set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying  \n> 1. every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n> 2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny,  \n> is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\nWe prove the theorem by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\).  All are non\u2011sunny and together they cover the three points.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the sunny line \\(\\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1\\) (slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \\(\\ell_1\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\).  \nAdd the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=2\\) and \\(y=2\\); they cover \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) respectively.  \nThus we have three distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny.\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2,3\\).*  \nIf two or three lines were sunny, Lemma\u202f1 would imply that at most \\(2\\cdot 3=6\\) points could be covered; this does not rule out the case \\(k=2\\).  However, with three points it is easy to check directly that any sunny line can contain at most one of the three points (the three points are not collinear with a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Hence at most one sunny line can be useful, and the remaining lines must be non\u2011sunny.  Consequently \\(k=2,3\\) are impossible.\n\nTherefore the admissible values for \\(n=3\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}= \\{0,\\dots ,3-2\\}\\).  The base case holds.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Induction hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some fixed \\(n\\ge 3\\) the theorem holds; i.e. for every  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,  \n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family.\n\nWe shall construct, for each  \n\n\\[\nk'\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,(n+1)-2\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\na family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k')\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Two elementary operations  \n\n#### Operation A (add a vertical line)  \nGiven any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) covering \\(T_n\\), define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\!*}= \\mathcal L\\cup\\{\\,x=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe added line is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny, and it covers the only new point \\((n+1,1)\\) of \\(T_{n+1}\\).  All other points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) already belong to \\(T_n\\).  Consequently \\(\\mathcal L^{\\!*}\\) covers \\(T_{n+1}\\) and has the same number of sunny lines as \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus from any admissible \\(k\\) for \\(n\\) we obtain the same \\(k\\) for \\(n+1\\).\n\n#### Operation B (turn a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one)  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and let \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) be a non\u2011sunny line.  \nBecause the family is minimal (otherwise we could delete a redundant line and obtain a smaller family), there exists a point \\(P\\in T_{n+1}\\) that lies **only** on \\(\\ell\\).\n\nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) that passes through \\(P\\) (choose any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \nSome points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that were on \\(\\ell\\) may now be uncovered; denote the set of uncovered points by \\(U\\).  \nFor each \\(Q\\in U\\) add a new line of a forbidden direction (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) that passes through \\(Q\\) and does not coincide with any existing line.  Because each such direction can be used, this is always possible.\n\nThe new family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) has  \n\n* one more sunny line (the line \\(\\ell'\\)),  \n* at most one additional line (the extra line needed for the set \\(U\\); if \\(|U|=0\\) no extra line is added).\n\nIf the total number of lines becomes \\(n+2\\), we delete any line that is now redundant (such a line exists because we have \\(n+2\\) lines covering only \\(|T_{n+1}|=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2\\) points, and \\(\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2<2(n+2)\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\)).  After the deletion we are left with exactly \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, and the number of sunny lines has increased by precisely one.\n\nHence Operation\u202fB transforms a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines into a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines, keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Completion of the induction step  \n\nTake any \\(k\\) satisfying (2) and the corresponding family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\).\n\n1. **Preserve \\(k\\).**  \n   Apply Operation\u202fA to obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. **Increase the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Apply Operation\u202fB to \\(\\mathcal F_0\\); we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal F_1\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Re\u2011apply Operation\u202fB to \\(\\mathcal F_1\\) (choosing a different non\u2011sunny line each time) to obtain families \\(\\mathcal F_2\\) and \\(\\mathcal F_3\\) with \\(k+2\\) and \\(k+3\\) sunny lines respectively.  \n   Each application adds at most one line and then we delete a redundant line, so the total number of lines stays \\(n+1\\).\n\nThus from a single admissible \\(k\\) we can produce configurations for the four consecutive values  \n\n\\[\nk,\\;k+1,\\;k+2,\\;k+3 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause the induction hypothesis provides configurations for every \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\), the union of the intervals (4) over all such \\(k\\) yields exactly the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,(n+1)-2\\}\\).  Consequently for every \\(k'\\) satisfying (3) there exists a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\nThis completes the inductive construction.\n\n---\n\n### 2.7  Non\u2011existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nLet \\(m\\ge 3\\) and suppose we have \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_m\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*If \\(k=m\\).*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_m\\); therefore the total number of covered points is at most \\(2m\\).  From (1),\n\n\\[\n|T_m|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2>2m\\qquad (m\\ge3),\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  Hence \\(k=m\\) is impossible.\n\n*If \\(k=m-1\\).*  \nThe single non\u2011sunny line can be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  In any of these three directions it contains at most \\(m\\) points of \\(T_m\\).  Consequently the whole family can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n2(m-1)+m = 3m-2\n\\]\n\npoints.  For \\(m\\ge4\\),\n\n\\[\n|T_m|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 > 3m-2,\n\\]\n\nso a configuration with \\(k=m-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  The case \\(m=3\\) has already been settled in the base step.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n---\n\n### 2.8  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* For each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem (induction construction).\n* No family can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines (counting argument using Lemma\u202f1).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). \u220e"
