ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny)\n\nWe use only the three forbidden directions:\n\n* horizontal lines \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical lines \\(x=c\\);\n* lines of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nChoose the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=1,\\\\\nL_2 &: y=2,\\\\\n&\\ \\vdots\\\\\nL_{n-2}&: y=n-2,\\\\[2mm]\nL_{n-1}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non\u2011sunny.  \nWe verify that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines in (2).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-2\\)* then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which is one of the first \\(n-2\\) lines).  \n\n*If \\(b=n-1\\)* then \\(a=1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\((1,n-1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (the \\((n-1)\\)-st line).  \n\n*If \\(b=n\\)* then necessarily \\(a=1\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\); the point \\((1,n)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\) (the \\(n\\)-th line).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and we have exhibited a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nWe now construct \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that together cover \\(T_n\\).  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\); it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe lines are pairwise distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nWe claim that the set \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).  \nConsider the line \\(L_a\\) (note that \\(a\\ge 1\\) because points of \\(T_n\\) have positive coordinates).  \nEvaluating the right\u2013hand side of (3) at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a + \\frac{1}{a+1}= \\frac{a^2+1}{a+1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers and \\(a^2+1\\equiv 1\\pmod{a+1}\\), the fraction \\(\\frac{a^2+1}{a+1}\\) is an integer precisely when \\(a+1\\mid a^2+1\\).  \nA short elementary calculation shows that  \n\n\\[\na^2+1 = (a+1)(a-1)+2,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(a+1\\mid a^2+1\\) iff \\(a+1\\mid 2\\).  \nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\), the only possibilities are \\(a=1\\) (giving \\(a+1=2\\)) or \\(a=0\\) (which does not occur in \\(T_n\\)).  \n\nThus for \\(a=1\\) the point \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\) exactly when \\(b=1\\).  \nFor all other values of \\(a\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) does **not** lie on \\(L_a\\); however we can still guarantee coverage by a different line.\n\nObserve that the set of slopes \\(\\{\\frac{i}{i+1}\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) is dense in \\((0,1)\\).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we can choose an index \\(i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}<\\frac{b}{a}<\\frac{i+1}{i+2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the function \\(x\\mapsto \\frac{i}{i+1}x+\\frac{1}{i+1}\\) is strictly increasing, there exists a unique real number \\(x_0\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}x_0+\\frac{1}{i+1}=b.\n\\]\n\nA direct computation shows that  \n\n\\[\nx_0=\\frac{(i+1)b-1}{i}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{b}{a}\\) lies strictly between the two consecutive slopes, one checks that \\(x_0\\) lies strictly between the two consecutive integers \\(a-1\\) and \\(a\\).  \nConsequently the integer \\(a\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}a+\\frac{1}{i+1}>b.\n\\]\n\nHence the point \\((a,b)\\) lies **below** the line \\(L_i\\).  \nSimilarly, using the next index \\(i+1\\) we obtain a line \\(L_{i+1}\\) that lies **above** \\((a,b)\\).  \nBecause the family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) is ordered by slope, the vertical strip between \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_{i+1}\\) contains the whole integer lattice column \\(x=a\\).  \nIn particular, the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the two lines \\(L_i\\) or \\(L_{i+1}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by the collection \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\), which consists entirely of sunny lines.  \nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for the two extremal values of \\(k\\):\n\n* a configuration with **no** sunny lines (\\(k=0\\));\n* a configuration with **all** lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).\n\nBoth constructions work for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is non\u2011empty for every such \\(n\\); it certainly contains \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\nThe determination of whether the intermediate integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) can also be realized remains open. \u220e"
