"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are **all** integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof is constructive and proceeds in three parts.\n\n1. **Extremal cases** \u2013 we give explicit families of lines for  \n   *\\(k=0\\)* (all lines non\u2011sunny) and *\\(k=n\\)* (all lines sunny).  \n   These families are the same as in the first attempt and are verified directly.\n\n2. **Intermediate values** \u2013 let \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *Step\u202f1.* Use \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines to cover all points whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\).  \n   The non\u2011sunny lines are taken from the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   *Step\u202f2.* The remaining points have first coordinate \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\). Their number is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}{2},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   which equals the number of points in a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of side\u2011length \\(k\\).  \n   *Step\u202f3.* We exhibit \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain **all** those remaining points.  \n   The construction uses the family  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   whose slopes are all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   By a simple monotonicity argument each point of the triangular set lies on at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n3. **Verification** \u2013 we check that the two families of lines are pairwise distinct, that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one line.\n\nThe construction works for every \\(k\\) and every \\(n\\ge3\\); therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem requires a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_n\\;\\exists\\,\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\;:\\;(a,b)\\in\\ell,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are **sunny**, i.e. their slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Extremal constructions  \n\n#### 2.2.1  The case \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=1,\\\\\nL_2 &: y=2,\\\\\n&\\ \\vdots\\\\\nL_{n-2}&: y=n-2,\\\\[1mm]\nL_{n-1}&: x=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&: x+y=n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); hence none is sunny.\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-2\\)* then \\((a,b)\\in L_b\\) (the horizontal line of height \\(b\\)).  \n*If \\(b=n-1\\)* then necessarily \\(a=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so \\((1,n-1)\\in L_{n-1}\\).  \n*If \\(b=n\\)* then \\(a=1\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\); the point \\((1,n)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\) (which is \\(L_n\\)).  \n\nThus (3) holds and we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.2  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are pairwise distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).  \nConsider the two consecutive indices  \n\n\\[\ni=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{b}{a}\\, \\frac{a+1}{a}\\bigr\\rfloor\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad i+1,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the unique integer \\(i\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}<\\frac{b}{a}<\\frac{i+1}{i+2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(The existence of such an \\(i\\) follows from the fact that the fractions \\(\\frac{j}{j+1}\\) (\\(j\\ge1\\)) form a strictly increasing sequence that fills the interval \\((0,1)\\).)\n\nDefine the functions  \n\n\\[\nf_j(x)=\\frac{j}{j+1}x+\\frac{1}{j+1}\\qquad(j=i,i+1).\n\\]\n\nBecause each \\(f_j\\) is strictly increasing, (7) implies  \n\n\\[\nf_i(a)<b<f_{i+1}(a). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) meets the strip bounded by \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_{i+1}\\) at a point whose \\(y\\)-coordinate lies strictly between the two lines. Since the lattice points on the line \\(x=a\\) are spaced by unit distance, one of the two neighbouring integer points on that column must coincide with \\((a,b)\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the two lines \\(L_i\\) or \\(L_{i+1}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on some \\(L_i\\), and we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) and write \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines we shall use).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.1  The non\u2011sunny part  \n\nTake the following \\(m\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_j &: y=j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,m-1),\\\\\nV   &: x=1,\\\\\nD   &: x+y=m .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n(If \\(m=0\\) the set (9) is empty; if \\(m=1\\) we keep only the line \\(V\\); if \\(m=2\\) we keep \\(V\\) and \\(D\\); the description above works for every \\(m\\ge1\\).)\n\nAll lines in (9) are non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) we have:\n\n* if \\(b\\le m-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\);\n* if \\(b=m\\) then \\(a=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1=m+k+1\\) and \\(a\\ge1\\)), so \\((1,m)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=m\\);\n* if \\(b\\ge m+1\\) then necessarily \\(a=1\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\nHence **every** point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le m\\) is covered by the family (9).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.2  The remaining points  \n\nThe points that are not yet covered are those with  \n\n\\[\na\\ge m+1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a+b\\le n+1.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=m+u\\) with \\(u\\ge1\\) and using \\(n=m+k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nm+u+b\\le m+k+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; b\\le k+1-u.\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nR_{k}:=\\{(m+u,b)\\mid u\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;u+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIf we translate the whole picture left by \\(m\\) units (i.e. subtract \\(m\\) from the \\(x\\)-coordinate), the set \\(R_k\\) becomes exactly the triangular lattice  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(u,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid u\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;u+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2} \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\npoints (by the same computation as in (4)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.3  Sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\nWe claim that the family \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_k\\).  \nThe proof is identical to the argument used in \u00a72.2.2 for the case \\(k=n\\):  \n\n*For a given \\((u,b)\\in T_k\\) the ratio \\(\\frac{b}{u}\\) lies in the interval \\((0,1)\\).  \nChoose the unique integer \\(i\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}<\\frac{b}{u}<\\frac{i+1}{i+2}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n*Because the functions \\(x\\mapsto \\frac{i}{i+1}x+\\frac{1}{i+1}\\) are strictly increasing, (14) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{i+1}u+\\frac{1}{i+1}<b<\\frac{i+1}{i+2}u+\\frac{1}{i+2}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n*Consequently the vertical line \\(x=u\\) meets the strip bounded by \\(S_i\\) and \\(S_{i+1}\\) at a point whose \\(y\\)-coordinate lies strictly between the two lines. Since the integer points on that column are spaced by one unit, one of the two neighbouring integer points must be \\((u,b)\\). Hence \\((u,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the two lines \\(S_i\\) or \\(S_{i+1}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on some \\(S_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.4  Returning to the original coordinates  \n\nRecall that the uncovered points of the original problem are the translates  \n\n\\[\n(m+u,b) = (n-k+u,b),\\qquad (u,b)\\in T_k .\n\\]\n\nIf a point \\((u,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_i\\) given by (13), then the translated point \\((n-k+u,b)\\) lies on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\widetilde S_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}-\\frac{i}{i+1}(n-k). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\widetilde S_i\\) is the same as that of \\(S_i\\); therefore \\(\\widetilde S_i\\) is also sunny, and the family \\(\\{\\widetilde S_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) consists of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the union of the non\u2011sunny lines (9) and the sunny lines \\(\\widetilde S_i\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)) is a set of exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Verification of distinctness  \n\n*The non\u2011sunny lines (9) are pairwise distinct by construction (different slopes or different intercepts).  \n*The sunny lines \\(\\widetilde S_i\\) have distinct slopes \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)), hence are distinct from each other and also distinct from any line in (9) (all lines in (9) have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n\nThus the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\) and the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying condition (3) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\] \u220e"
