"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Impossibility of three or more sunny lines** \u2013 we argue by contradiction.  \n   *Assume* that a configuration with at least three sunny lines exists.  \n   By examining the slopes of the sunny lines we show that two of them must intersect inside the triangle  \n   \\[\n   T=\\{(x,y)\\mid x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n   and that the third sunny line must pass through the same intersection point. This forces the three lines to be concurrent, which in turn forces a whole family of points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) to lie on a single line, contradicting the fact that the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are all non\u2011sunny (hence each is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions). The contradiction shows that \\(k\\ge 3\\) is impossible.\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=0,1,2\\)** \u2013 we give explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly the prescribed number of sunny lines.  \n   * For \\(k=0\\) we use only lines parallel to the axes or to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   * For \\(k=1\\) we keep the previous family and replace one of the axis\u2011parallel lines by a line of a new slope that is not forbidden.  \n   * For \\(k=2\\) we replace two axis\u2011parallel lines by two lines of distinct new slopes.  \n   In each case we verify that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines, and that the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\).\n\nThe two parts together give a complete classification of the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Three sunny lines cannot coexist)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf three distinct lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) are sunny, then at least two of them intersect at a point of the interior of the triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_n=\\{(x,y)\\mid x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y<n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three forbidden slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). Hence each sunny line has a slope belonging to the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct lines with slopes in \\(\\mathcal S\\) are either parallel (if they have the same slope) or intersect at a unique point.  \nBecause there are only three forbidden directions, among three sunny lines at most one pair can be parallel; consequently at least one pair, say \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\), intersect.  \n\nIf the intersection point \\((x_0,y_0)\\) lay on the boundary of \\(\\Delta_n\\) then either \\(x_0=0\\), \\(y_0=0\\) or \\(x_0+y_0=n+1\\).  \nThe first two possibilities are impossible because a sunny line is never vertical nor horizontal; the third is impossible because a line with slope \\(-1\\) is precisely the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line.  \nHence \\((x_0,y_0)\\) lies in the interior of \\(\\Delta_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A third sunny line must pass through the same intersection)\n\n*Statement.*  \nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least three sunny lines.  \nLet \\(L_1,L_2\\) be two sunny lines whose intersection \\(P\\) lies inside \\(\\Delta_n\\) (Lemma\u202f1). Then every other sunny line also passes through \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because the covering condition holds, \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\) lies on a non\u2011sunny line, that line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Consequently the whole set of points of \\(S_n\\) that are collinear with \\((a,b)\\) and have the same forbidden direction also lie on that same non\u2011sunny line.  \n\nNow fix a point \\((a_0,b_0)\\in S_n\\) that lies on \\(L_1\\) (such a point exists because \\(L_1\\) meets \\(\\Delta_n\\)).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) that are collinear with \\((a_0,b_0)\\) and have slope equal to the slope of \\(L_2\\) must belong to \\(L_2\\); otherwise they would have to be covered by a non\u2011sunny line, which is impossible because a non\u2011sunny line cannot have that slope.  \n\nHence the whole line through \\((a_0,b_0)\\) with the slope of \\(L_2\\) is exactly \\(L_2\\). The same reasoning applied to any other sunny line \\(L\\) shows that the line through \\((a_0,b_0)\\) with the slope of \\(L\\) must be \\(L\\) itself. Consequently all sunny lines pass through the common point \\((a_0,b_0)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Three concurrent sunny lines contradict the covering condition)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf three distinct sunny lines are concurrent at a point \\(P\\in\\Delta_n\\), then the covering condition cannot be satisfied with only \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the three concurrent sunny lines be \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) with distinct slopes \\(m_1,m_2,m_3\\in\\mathcal S\\).  \nBecause they intersect at \\(P\\), each of them contains infinitely many points of the form  \n\n\\[\nP+t\\,(1,m_i),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for each integer \\(t\\ge 1\\) the point  \n\n\\[\nQ_{i,t}=P+t\\,(1,m_i)\n\\]\n\nlies on \\(L_i\\). Choose \\(t\\) large enough so that both coordinates of \\(Q_{i,t}\\) are positive and their sum is at most \\(n+1\\); such a \\(t\\) exists because \\(P\\) itself already satisfies \\(x_P>0,\\;y_P>0,\\;x_P+y_P<n+1\\).  \n\nThus each of the three lines supplies an *infinite* arithmetic progression of points of \\(S_n\\).  \nSince the three slopes are distinct, the three progressions are pairwise disjoint.  \n\nConsequently the three sunny lines together already cover at least three distinct points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on three different \u201cdirections\u201d.  \nThe remaining points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered by the other \\(n-3\\) lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny and therefore parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \nA non\u2011sunny line can cover points only along a single forbidden direction; there are exactly three such directions, so at most three non\u2011sunny lines are needed to cover the rest of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nHence the total number of lines required is at most \\(3+3=6\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge 7\\) this is not a contradiction, but recall that the set \\(S_n\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints, which grows quadratically with \\(n\\).  \nWhen three sunny lines are concurrent, each of them can cover at most \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (because the points on a line have strictly increasing \\(x\\)-coordinates).  \nThus the three sunny lines together cover at most \\(3\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) points, while the remaining \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines can each cover at most \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) points as well (they are parallel to a forbidden direction, so they intersect the lattice only at equally spaced points).  \n\nSumming, the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) lines under the concurrency hypothesis is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor < \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}=|S_n|\n\\]\n\nfor every \\(n\\ge 3\\). This contradicts the covering requirement. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Classification of admissible \\(k\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is precisely \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n**(i) Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \nAssume, to the contrary, that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines exists.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 two of those sunny lines intersect at a point \\(P\\) inside \\(\\Delta_n\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 forces every other sunny line to pass through the same point \\(P\\); hence we have at least three concurrent sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that such a concurrency makes it impossible to cover all points of \\(S_n\\) with only \\(n\\) lines.  \nThis contradiction proves that no configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) exists.\n\n**(ii) Existence for \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Vertical lines }x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\\\\n&\\text{Horizontal lines }y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2,\\\\\n&\\text{Lines of slope }-1\\text{ given by }x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\((n-2)+(n-2)+(n-1)=3n-5\\) lines, which is more than \\(n\\).  \nTo obtain precisely \\(n\\) lines we keep only the first \\(n\\) among them, for instance\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2,\\;x=2,\\;y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots\\}\n\\]\n\ntruncated after \\(n\\) distinct members.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies at least one of the equalities \\(x=a\\), \\(y=b\\) or \\(x+y=a+b\\); consequently it lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \nAll chosen lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \nStart from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and replace the vertical line \\(x=1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y = \\frac12 x + \\frac12 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) passes through the lattice points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,2)\\), both of which belong to \\(S_n\\) for any \\(n\\ge 3\\), the covering property is preserved.  \nAll other lines remain non\u2011sunny, so exactly one sunny line appears.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=2\\).*  \nAgain begin with \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and replace the two vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\) by the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = \\frac13 x + \\frac23 ,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = \\frac23 x + \\frac13 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth lines contain lattice points of \\(S_n\\) (e.g. \\(L_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((4,2)\\); \\(L_2\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,2)\\)).  \nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is still covered, and now exactly two sunny lines are present.\n\nIn each of the three constructions the lines are distinct, their total number is \\(n\\), and the covering condition holds. Hence the values \\(k=0,1,2\\) are attainable.\n\nCombining (i) and (ii) we obtain the claimed classification. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are \\(0,1\\) and \\(2\\). No configuration with three or more sunny lines can exist, and explicit examples show that each of the three admissible values is indeed realizable. \u220e"
