\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the points are equally spaced along that direction). With only \\(n-3\\) such lines we can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-3)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   additional points.  \n\n   \u2013 Adding the two estimates gives an upper bound for the total number of covered points:\n\n   \\[\n   3\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+(n-3)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   =n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   <\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}=|S_n|\n   \\qquad (n\\ge3).\n   \\]\n\n   The strict inequality follows from the elementary fact that\n   \\(\\displaystyle n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}&n\\text{ even}\\\\[2pt]\n   \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}&n\\text{ odd},\n   \\end{cases}\\)\n   and the right\u2011hand side is exactly \\(|S_n|\\).  \n   Thus the assumed configuration cannot cover all points of \\(S_n\\), a contradiction.  \n   Hence **no configuration with three or more sunny lines exists**, i.e. \\(k\\le2\\).\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n   We give explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly the prescribed number of sunny lines.\n\n   *Notation.*  \n   For an integer \\(t\\ge2\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   V_t:\\;x=t,\\qquad\n   H_t:\\;y=t,\\qquad\n   D_t:\\;x+y=t\n   \\]\n\n   denote respectively a vertical, a horizontal and a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   All three families are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Choose the first \\(n\\) lines from the ordered list  \n\n   \\[\n   V_1,\\;H_1,\\;D_2,\\;V_2,\\;H_2,\\;D_3,\\;V_3,\\;H_3,\\;D_4,\\dots\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (the pattern repeats \u201cvertical \u2013 horizontal \u2013 diagonal\u201d).  \n   Because the list contains infinitely many lines, we can always stop after exactly \\(n\\) distinct members; denote the obtained set by \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at least one of the three equalities \\(a=1\\), \\(b=1\\) or \\(a+b=2\\) holds for the first three lines of (1); if not, increase the index until the corresponding line appears in the list. Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). All lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non\u2011sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) realises \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=1\\).*  \n   Start from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and replace the first vertical line \\(V_1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_1:\\;y=\\frac12x+\\frac12 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The line (2) passes through the lattice points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,2)\\); both belong to \\(S_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) that was previously covered by \\(V_1\\) (i.e. the points with \\(x=1\\)) is still covered, now by (2). All other lines are unchanged, so the covering condition remains satisfied and exactly one sunny line appears.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=2\\).*  \n   Begin again with \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and replace the two vertical lines \\(V_1\\) and \\(V_2\\) by the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   S_1 &: y=\\frac13x+\\frac23 ,\\\\[2pt]\n   S_2 &: y=\\frac23x+\\frac13 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Both lines contain the lattice point \\((1,1)\\); moreover \\(S_1\\) contains \\((4,2)\\) and \\(S_2\\) contains \\((3,2)\\), all of which belong to \\(S_n\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\). Consequently every point that was previously covered by \\(V_1\\) or \\(V_2\\) is still covered by one of the lines in (3). The remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are exactly those of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) that were not removed, so the covering condition holds and precisely two sunny lines are present.\n\n   In each of the three cases the family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, satisfies the required covering property, and contains the claimed number of sunny lines. Hence the values \\(k=0,1,2\\) are attainable.\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k