n   \\]\n\n   This proves that **no** configuration can have more than \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) we exhibit an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n   *Take \\(m=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines as follows*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x_{1},\\\\\n   &y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y_{1},\\\\\n   &x+y=n+1,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   where the numbers \\(x_{1},y_{1}\\) are chosen so that the total number of non\u2011sunny lines equals \\(m\\) (this is always possible because \\(m\\ge\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\)).  \n   These lines cover all points of \\(S\\) because:\n   * every point with \\(a\\le x_{1}\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\);\n   * every point with \\(b\\le y_{1}\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\);\n   * the remaining points satisfy \\(a> x_{1},\\;b> y_{1}\\) and therefore \\(a+b=n+1\\), so they lie on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   *Add \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct lines with slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (for instance, the lines \\(y=2x+1,\\;y=3x+2,\\dots\\)). They do not affect the covering property because all points are already covered by the non\u2011sunny lines. The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   This construction works for every admissible \\(k\\), proving that all integers in the interval (4) are attainable.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility result (4) with the explicit construction, we obtain the complete description of the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Number of required points)\n\n\\