ines, then  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently no configuration can have more than \\(\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\]\n\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad\\text{(the required number of non\u2011sunny lines).}\n\\]\n\nBecause of the bound (5) we have  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\ge n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(m\\) is at least the quantity demanded by Lemma\u202f3, so a covering by \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines is possible.\n\nChoose non\u2011negative integers \\(p,q\\) with  \n\n\\[\np+q+1=m\\qquad (p,q\\ge0).\n\\]\n\nTake the following \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=p}_{p\\text{ vertical lines}},\n\\qquad\n\\underbrace{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=q}_{q\\text{ horizontal lines}},\n\\qquad\nx+y=n+1\\quad\\text{(one diagonal line)}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(a\\le p\\), it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n* If \\(a>p\\) but \\(b\\le q\\), then \\(b\\le q\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n* If \\(a>p\\) and \\(b>q\\), then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a+b=n+1\\); consequently the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one of the lines in (7). The lines are distinct because the constants \\(1,\\dots ,p\\) and \\(1,\\dots ,q\\) are different, and the diagonal line is not parallel to the axes.\n\nFinally, add any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines (for instance \\(y=2x+1,\\;y=3x+2,\\dots\\)). They do not interfere with the covering already achieved, and they increase the total number of lines to \\(p+q+1+k=n\\). Exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\nHence a configuration exists for every admissible \\(k\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Complete answer)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor \\,\\}\\ } .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nCorollary\u202f4 shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\).  \nLemma\u202f5 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) in this range a suitable configuration can be constructed.  \nTherefore the described interval is both necessary and sufficient. \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**"
