rs every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 Conclusion.*  \nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are admissible.  \nNo other value of \\(k\\) can occur because the total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\).\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nDefine three families of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H:=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal V:=\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal D:=\\{x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEvery line in \\(\\mathcal H\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, every line in \\(\\mathcal V\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, and every line in \\(\\mathcal D\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence all lines in \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) are **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n*Covering property of the families.*  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n\\) the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) contains precisely the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,j)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\}\\subset\\mathcal P .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{y=j\\in\\mathcal H}\\{(a,j)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\}\n   =\\mathcal P .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe same verification holds for \\(\\mathcal V\\) and \\(\\mathcal D\\). Consequently each of the three families alone covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nHence, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) it suffices to start from the horizontal family \\(\\mathcal H\\) (which consists of exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines) and to replace \\(k\\) of its lines by sunny lines while preserving the covering of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Lemma (replacement of a horizontal line).*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal H\\) be the line \\(y=j\\) for some \\(1\\le j\\le n\\).  \nThen there exists a sunny lin