scribed number of sunny lines it suffices to start from the horizontal family \\(\\mathcal H\\) (which consists of exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines) and to replace some of its lines by sunny ones while preserving the covering of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma (replacement of a horizontal line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal H\\) be the line \\(y=j\\) for some \\(1\\le j\\le n\\).  \nThen there exists a sunny line \\(\\ell^{*}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\mathcal H\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell^{*}\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lying on \\(\\ell\\) are  \n\n\\[\nR_\\ell:=\\{(a,j)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nChoose two distinct integers \\(a_1,a_2\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a_1<a_2\\le n+1-j .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsider  \n\n\\[\nP_1:=(a_1,j),\\qquad P_2:=(a_2+1,j-1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a_2+1+j-1=a_2+j\\le n+1\\), both \\(P_1,P_2\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell^{*}\\) through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{j-(j-1)}{a_1-(a_2+1)}=\\frac{1}{a_1-a_2-1}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a_1-a_2-1\\le -2\\), we have \\(m\\neq0\\), \\(m\\neq\\infty\\) and \\(m\\neq-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is sunny.\n\n\\(P_1=(a_1,j)\\) lies on \\(\\ell^{*}\\) by construction, and the point \\((a_2,j)\\in R_\\ell\\) also lies on \\(\\ell^{*}\\) because the three points \\(P_1,(a_2,j),P_2\\) are collinear.  \nAll remaining points of \\(R_\\ell\\) are still covered by the unchanged horizontal line \\(y=j\\) (which we have removed) **except** for \\((a_2,j)\\); this point is now covered by \\(\\ell^{*}\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that was covered by \\(\\ell\\) remains covered after the replacement, and all other points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are untouched. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSelect any \\(k\\) distinct horizontals \\(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_k\\in\\mathcal H\\).  \nApply the Lemma to each \\(\\ell_i\\) independently, obtaining sunny lines \\(\\ell_i^{*}\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\bigl(\\mathcal H\\setminus\\{\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_1^{*},\\dots,\\ell_k^{*}\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines: \\(n-k\\) horizontals (non\u2011sunny) and \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy the Lemma, each replacement preserves the covering of \\(\\mathcal P\\); consequently \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Converse\n\nBecause the total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative. Hence no integer \\(k\\) outside the interval \\([0,n]\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible, and only these.}}\n\\]"
