from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nWrite a non\u2011vertical line in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+b,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) are two distinct lattice points on the line, subtracting the equations gives  \n\n\\[\ny_2-y_1=\\frac{p}{q}(x_2-x_1) .\n\\]\n\nSince the left\u2011hand side is an integer, \\(q\\mid (x_2-x_1)\\); write \\(x_2-x_1=q\\). Then \\(y_2-y_1=p\\). Hence any two lattice points differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\). Consequently the set of \\(x\\)-coordinates of lattice points on the line forms an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(q\\).\n\nIf the line is sunny, its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore \\(|p|\\neq0\\) and \\(|p|\\neq|q|\\). In particular \\(q\\ge2\\). The \\(x\\)-coordinates of points of \\(S_n\\) lie in the interval \\([1,n]\\); an arithmetic progression with step \\(q\\ge2\\) can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{q}\\Big\\rfloor+1\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nsuch integers. Hence a sunny line can cover at most the number in (2.3) of points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nFor a non\u2011sunny line we have three possibilities:\n\n* Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\) integer). The condition \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\) gives at most \\(n-c\\le n-1\\) admissible \\(a\\)\u2019s.\n* Vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\) integer). Symmetrically at most \\(n-1\\) admissible \\(b\\)\u2019s.\n* Slope \\(-1\\) line \\(x+y=c\\). Here \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(1\\le b=c-a\\le n+1-c\\); the number of integer solutions is again at most \\(n-1\\).\n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nNow suppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Using the bounds above, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nT(k)=k\\Bigl(\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-k)(n-1).\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nWe compare \\(T(k)\\) with \\(|S_n|\\) from (2.1).\n\n*If \\(k=2\\):*  \n\n\\[\nT(2)=2\\Bigl(\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-2)(n-1)\n      =n^{2}-3n+2+2\\Bigl(\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nA direct check for even \\(n=2r\\) and odd \\(n=2r+1\\) shows  \n\n\\[\nT(2)-|S_n|=r^{2}+r\\ge0,\n\\]\n\nso \\(k=2\\) is not ruled out.\n\n*If \\(k=3\\):*  \n\n\\[\nT(3)=3\\Bigl(\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)+(n-3)(n-1)\n      =n^{2}-4n+3+3\\Bigl(\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nAgain treating even and odd \\(n\\):\n\n* \\(n=2r\\): \\(T(3)-|S_n|=4r^{2}-2r-2-(2r^{2}-3r+1)=2r^{2}+r-3<0\\) for all \\(r\\ge1\\).\n* \\(n=2r+1\\): \\(T(3)-|S_n|=4r^{2}+2r-2-(2r^{2}+r)=2r^{2}+r-2<0\\) for all \\(r\\ge1\\).\n\nThus \\(T(3)<|S_n|\\); three sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(S_n\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le2 .\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Constructions achieving \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nDefine three families of non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nV_t:\\;x=t,\\qquad H_t:\\;y=t,\\qquad D_t:\\;x+y=t\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb N).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2.1 Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the infinite periodic list  \n\n\\[\nL_0=(V_1,\\,H_1,\\,D_2,\\,V_2,\\,H_2,\\,D_3,\\,V_3,\\,H_3,\\,D_4,\\dots).\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nTake the first \\(n\\) distinct lines of this list; denote the resulting set by \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*Covering proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n\n* If \\(a\\le b\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(D_{a+b}\\). In the list (2.6) the line \\(D_{a+b}\\) appears at position \\(3a-2\\) (because each block of three lines contributes one diagonal with index increasing by one). Since \\(a\\le n\\), we have \\(3a-2\\le 3n-2\\); but we on