n\nThus \\(T(3)<|S_n|\\); three sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(S_n\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le2 .\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Constructions achieving \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nDefine three families of non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nV_t:\\;x=t,\\qquad H_t:\\;y=t,\\qquad D_t:\\;x+y=t\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb N).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2.1 Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the infinite periodic list  \n\n\\[\nL_0=(V_1,\\,H_1,\\,D_2,\\,V_2,\\,H_2,\\,D_3,\\,V_3,\\,H_3,\\,D_4,\\dots).\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nTake the first \\(n\\) distinct lines of this list; denote the resulting set by \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*Covering proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n\n* If \\(a\\le b\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(D_{a+b}\\). In the list (2.6) the line \\(D_{a+b}\\) appears at position \\(3a-2\\) (because each block of three lines contributes one diagonal with index increasing by one). Since \\(a\\le n\\), we have \\(3a-2\\le 3n-2\\); but we only need the first \\(n\\) lines, and for every \\(a\\) with \\(a\\le n\\) the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\) appears no later than the \\(n\\)-th entry because the first \\(n\\) entries contain all diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots,D_{n+1}\\).  \n\n* If \\(a>b\\) then the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\). The line \\(V_a\\) occurs at position \\(3a-2\\) as well, and for \\(a\\le n\\) this position is within the first \\(n\\) entries for the same reason as above.\n\nHence each point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). All lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n#### 2.2.2 Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nStart from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and replace the vertical line \\(V_1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_1:\\;y=\\frac12x+\\frac12 .\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_1\\) contains the lattice points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,2)\\), both belonging to \\(S_n\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*Effect on coverage.*  \nThe only points that were covered exclusively by \\(V_1\\) are those with \\(x=1\\).  \n\n* \\((1,1)\\) is now on \\(S_1\\).  \n* Any other \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) satisfies \\(1+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal \\(D_{1+b}\\), which is already present in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\nAll other points of \\(S_n\\) are covered exactly as before. Thus the modified set contains exactly one sunny line and still covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 2.2.3 Construction for \\(k=2\\)\n\nAgain begin with \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and replace the two vertical lines \\(V_1\\) and \\(V_2\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_1 &: y=\\frac13x+\\frac23 ,\\\\\nS_2 &: y=\\frac23x+\\frac13 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nBoth lines pass through \\((1,1)\\); moreover \\(S_1\\) contains \\((4,2)\\) and \\(S_2\\) contains \\((3,2)\\), all of which belong to \\(S_n\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n* Points with \\(x=1\\): \\((1,1)\\) lies on both sunny lines; any \\((1,b),\\,b\\ge2\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{1+b}\\) already in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n* Points with \\(x=2\\): \\((2,2)\\) satisfies \\(2=\\frac23\\cdot2+\\frac13\\) and is on \\(S_2\\); \\((2,1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_3\\) (present); any \\((2,b),\\,b\\ge3\\) satisfies \\(2+b\\le n+1\\) and lies on \\(D_{2+b}\\) (present).\n* Points with \\(x\\ge3\\): the covering argument of the \\(k=0\\) construction remains valid because the only removed lines were \\(V_1\\) and \\(V_2\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is still covered, and exactly two sunny lines appear.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) can contain three or more sunny lines (inequality (2.5)).\n* For each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) we exhibited an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nTherefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
