f \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines, where  \n\n   \\[\n   k'=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   k      &\\text{if }k\\le n,\\\\[2pt]\n   n      &\\text{if }k=n+1 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   (When \\(k=n+1\\) we will later turn one of the non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) into a sunny line.)\n\n4. **Adjustment of the number of sunny lines.**  \n   *If \\(k\\le n\\)* we simply take the union  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\mathcal L_{n}\\cup\\{L_{x},L_{y}\\}.\n   \\]\n   The two added lines are non\u2011sunny, so the total number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).\n\n   *If \\(k=n+1\\)* we need one more sunny line.  \n   Choose any line \\(M\\) of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) that is non\u2011sunny (such a line exists because \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) contains at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines).  \n   Replace \\(M\\) by a parallel line \\(M'\\) that is slightly tilted, i.e. change its slope by a non\u2011zero amount so that \\(M'\\) is no longer parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   The new line \\(M'\\) still passes through all points of \\(M\\) that belong to \\(T_{n}\\) (because we keep the same intercept) and therefore continues to cover the required points.  \n   Now \\(M'\\) is sunny, increasing the sunny count by one.  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n+1}= (\\mathcal L_{n}\\setminus\\{M\\})\\cup\\{M',L_{x},L_{y}\\}.\n   \\]\n   The total number of sunny lines is \\(n+1\\).\n\n5. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f3 guarantees that all lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) are distinct.\n\nThus the inductive construction works for every admissible \\(k\\), completing the induction.\n\nThe two auxiliary lemmas used in the construction are proved separately and are elementary.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation\n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) define the triangular set  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Covering the outer border)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\\) (i.e. with \\(a+b=n+2\\)) lies on at least one of the two lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{x}:x=n+2,\\qquad L_{y}:y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(a=n+2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_{x}\\); otherwise \\(a\\le n+1\\) and because \\(a+b=n+2\\) we have \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b=n+2-a\\ge 1\\). In this case \\(b=n+2\\) cannot hold, so the only possibility is \\(b=n+2\\), which puts the point on \\(L_{y}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive covering)\n\nAssume a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\mathcal L_{n}\\cup\\{L_{x},L_{y}\\}\\) where \\(L_{x},L_{y}\\) are as in Lemma\u202f1.  \nThen \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPoints of \\(T_{n}\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\).  \nPoints of the outer border \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\\) are covered by Lemma\u202f1. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3 (Distinctness)\n\nIn the construction of the inductive step the lines added or replaced are never coincident with an existing line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe two auxiliary lines \\(L_{x}\\) and \\(L_{y}\\) are vertical and horizontal respectively, while every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) either passes through a point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (hence has at most one point with abscissa \\(n+2\\) or ordinate \\(n+2\\)) or is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Consequently none of them coincides with \\(L_{x}\\) or \\(L_{y}\\).\n\nWhen a non\u2011sunny line \\(M\\) is replaced by a slightly tilted line \\(M'\\) we change its slope, so \\(M'\\) cannot be identical to any line that kept the original slope, in particular not to any line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\setminus\\{M\\}\\) nor to \\(L_{x},L_{y}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe triangle \\(T_{3}\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): take the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x=2\\).  \n  They cover the three points and none is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=2\\): let  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_{1}: y=1\\quad(\\text{non\u2011sunny}),\\qquad \n  \\ell_{2}: x+y=2\\quad(\\text{non\u2011sunny}),\\qquad \n  \\ell_{3}: y=2x-1\\quad(\\text{sunny}).\n  \\]\n  One checks that each of the three points lies on at least one of these lines; exactly one line (\\(\\ell_{3}\\)) is sunny, i.e. \\(k=1\\) is **not** obtained.  \n  To obtain \\(k=2\\) we replace \\(\\ell_{2}\\) by a sunny line, e.g. \\(y=2x-1\\) (already sunny) and keep \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{3}\\); now two lines are sunny.\n\n* \\(k=3\\): take three distinct sunny lines, for instance  \n  \\[\n  y=2x-1,\\qquad y=\\tfrac12 x+ \\tfrac12,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac13 x+ \\tfrac43 .\n  \\]\n  Each passes through at least one of the three required points, and all three are sunny.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\) and all admissible \\(k\\in\\{0,2,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Inductive step\n\nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\) and \\(k\\neq 1\\).  \nWe construct a configuration for \\(n+1\\) lines.\n\n1. **Construction of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\).**  \n   By the induction hypothesis there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k'=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   k      &\\text{if }k\\le n,\\\\[2pt]\n   n      &\\text{if }k=n+1 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   sunny lines.\n\n2. **Adding the two non\u2011sunny border lines.**  \n   Define \\(L_{x}:x=n+2\\) and \\(L_{y}:y=n+2\\).  \n   Both are parallel to the coordinate axes, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n3. **Case \\(k\\le n\\).**  \n   Set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\mathcal L_{n}\\cup\\{L_{x},L_{y}\\}.\n   \\]\n   By Lemma\u202f2 the new set covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \n   The two added lines are non\u2011sunny, so the total number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).\n\n4. **Case \\(k=n+1\\).**  \n   Since \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) contains at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (otherwise we would already have \\(k=n\\) or more), it contains at least one non\u2011sunny line; denote it by \\(M\\).  \n   Choose a small \\(\\varepsilon\\neq 0\\) and define \\(M'\\) to be the line obtained from \\(M\\) by rotating it through an angle whose tangent is \\(\\varepsilon\\). Concretely, if \\(M\\) has equation \\(ax+by=c\\) with \\((a,b)\\) parallel to one of \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\), replace it by \\(a'x+b'y=c\\) where \\((a',b')\\) is a vector not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions but arbitrarily close to \\((a,b)\\).  \n   Because the constant term \\(c\\) is unchanged, every point of \\(M\\cap T_{n}\\) still lies on \\(M'\\); consequently all points of \\(T_{n}\\) remain covered.  \n   Moreover \\(M'\\) is sunny by construction.\n\n   Define  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n+1}= (\\mathcal L_{n}\\setminus\\{M\\})\\cup\\{M',L_{x},L_{y}\\}.\n   \\]\n   Lemma\u202f3 guarantees that the three lines are distinct from each other and from the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\).  \n   The number of sunny lines is now \\(k'=n\\) (the sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\)) plus one (the newly sunny \\(M'\\)), i.e. \\(n+1\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f2 again shows that \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nIn both cases we have produced \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering the required triangular set and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThus the statement holds for \\(n+1\\).\n\nBy induction the statement is true for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k=1\\) could not be resolved by the present method; no construction nor impossibility proof is known at this stage. \u220e"
