 lines are distinct.\n\nThus for any prescribed \\(k\\) we have exhibited a concrete configuration satisfying the\ntwo conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nWe shall construct \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) such that  \n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with positive integers \\(a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at\n  least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n  the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. The non\u2011sunny part\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nb\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL_b:\\; y=b .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence they are **not** sunny.\nThey are pairwise distinct because they have different ordinates.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. The sunny part\n\nFor the remaining ordinates  \n\n\\[\nb\\in\\{n-k+1,n-k+2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nchoose real numbers \\(\\alpha_b\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_b\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_{b}\\neq\\alpha_{b'}\\;(b\\neq b').\n\\]\n\n(The set \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) is infinite, so such a choice is always possible.)\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_b:\\; y=\\alpha_b x + (b-\\alpha_b).\n\\]\n\n*Why does \\(S_b\\) contain the required points?*  \nPlugging \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=\\alpha_b\\cdot 1 + (b-\\alpha_b)=b\\); hence \\((1,b)\\in S_b\\).\nBecause the slope of \\(S_b\\) is \\(\\alpha_b\\), the line passes through all points\n\\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\mathbb R,\\qquad y=b+\\alpha_b(a-1).\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb+\\alpha_b(a-1)=\\alpha_b a + (b-\\alpha_b),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_b\\).  \nThus \\(S_b\\) contains **all** lattice points of the form \\((a,b)\\) that satisfy\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and have the given ordinate \\(b\\).\n\nThe lines \\(\\{S_b\\mid b=n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\nEach of them is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(\\alpha_b\\neq0\\)), not parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis (\\(\\alpha_b\\) finite), and not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(\\alpha_b\\neq-1\\));\nhence they are **sunny**.  There are exactly \\(k\\) such lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Verification of the two required properties\n\n1. **Coverage of the triangular set.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\le n-k\\)*, then the horizontal line \\(L_b\\) (which is part of our family) passes\n   through \\((a,b)\\) because it contains every point with ordinate \\(b\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\)*, then by construction \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_b\\).\n\n   Hence every required point lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n2. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_b\\}\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines \\(\\{L_b\\}\\) are\n   horizontal and therefore non\u2011sunny.  Consequently the total number of sunny lines is\n   precisely \\(k\\).\n\n3. **Distinctness of the lines.**  \n   The horizontal lines have distinct ordinates, and the sunny lines have distinct slopes,\n   so no two lines coincide.  Thus we indeed have \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both conditions of the problem, and exactly \\(k\\) of\nthem are sunny.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the proof. \u220e"
