nce \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=\\alpha_b\\cdot1+(b-\\alpha_b)=b\\), the point \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(S_b\\).\nBecause the slope of \\(S_b\\) is \\(\\alpha_b\\), for any integer \\(a\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ny=\\alpha_b a+(b-\\alpha_b)=b+\\alpha_b(a-1).\n\\]\n\nHence for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\) we obtain \\(y=b\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)\\in S_b\\qquad\\text{for all }1\\le a\\le n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(S_b\\) contains **all** lattice points on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) that satisfy\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nThe lines \\(\\{S_b\\}\\) are distinct because their slopes are distinct, and each of them is sunny:\n\\(\\alpha_b\\neq0\\) (not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), \\(\\alpha_b\\) finite (not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis), and \\(\\alpha_b\\neq-1\\) (not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Verification of the required properties\n\n1. **Coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\le n-k\\)*, then the horizontal line \\(L_b\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\) because it contains every point with ordinate \\(b\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\)*, then by the construction above \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_b\\).\n\n   Hence every required point lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n2. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_b\\mid b=n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines \\(\\{L_b\\}\\) are horizontal and therefore non\u2011sunny.  Consequently the total number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n3. **Distinctness of the lines.**  \n   The horizontal lines have distinct ordinates, and the sunny lines have distinct slopes, so no two lines coincide.  Thus \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k