y of \\(n\\) lines is bounded above by  \n\n  \\[\n  v+h+d+2k\\le n\\cdot n+2k\\le n^2+2k .\n  \\]\n\n  (Here we used that each non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(P_n\\).)  \n\n  Since \\(|P_n|=n(n+1)/2\\), the inequality  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2+2k\n  \\]\n\n  must hold. Rearranging gives  \n\n  \\[\n  k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)-2n^2}{4}= \\frac{n-n^2}{4}.\n  \\]\n\n  For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right\u2013hand side is negative, so the inequality imposes **no restriction** on \\(k\\).  \n  To obtain a genuine contradiction we sharpen the estimate for sunny lines: a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(P_n\\) that is not already on a non\u2011sunny line.  \n  Indeed, if a sunny line met a non\u2011sunny line at a point of \\(P_n\\), the two lines would intersect at that lattice point; any other lattice point on the sunny line would give a second intersection with the same non\u2011sunny line, forcing the two lines to be parallel, which is impossible. Consequently each sunny line contributes **at most one** *new* point of \\(P_n\\).\n\n  Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n  \\[\n  n\\cdot n + k .\n  \\]\n\n  The covering condition therefore requires  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2+k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)-2n^2}{2}= \\frac{n- n^2}{2}.\n  \\]\n\n  Again the right\u2013hand side is negative, so the inequality is always satisfied.  \n  The real obstruction comes from the **distribution** of the points among the three families of non\u2011sunny lines.  \n  Observe that the points of \\(P_n\\) lying on a vertical line have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, those on a horizontal line have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, and those on a diagonal line have distinct values of \\(x+y\\).  \n  Consequently a set of at most \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\) distinct values of each of the three quantities \\(x,\\;y,\\;x+y\\).  \n\n  The set \\(P_n\\) contain