case \\(k=0\\).\n\n  In each of the three constructions the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\) and the required covering property holds.\n\nThus the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three directions\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_y:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_d:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in P_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).\nThe slopes between any two of them are equal, so\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n=\n\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}\n=\n\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nIf the common slope were \\(0\\) the line would be horizontal,\nif it were undefined the line would be vertical,\nand if it were \\(-1\\) the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\nAll three possibilities are excluded by the definition of sunny,\nhence a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a sunny line meets a non\u2011sunny line at a point of \\(P_n\\), then it does **not**\ncontain any other point of \\(P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell_s\\) be sunny and \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) be non\u2011sunny, and suppose they intersect\nat \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell_s\\) contained another point \\((a',b')\\in P_n\\), then the two distinct\nintersection points \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) would lie on both \\(\\ell_s\\) and\n\\(\\ell_{ns}\\).  Two distinct lines cannot have two common points; therefore\n\\(\\ell_s\\) and \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) would have to coincide, which would make \\(\\ell_s\\)\nnon\u2011sunny \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nAmong the points of \\(P_n\\) the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,\\;(n,1)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nhave pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct\nvalues of \\(x+y\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Immediate from the definition of the list (3). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \nTo cover the set (3) one needs at least one line of each of the three\nnon\u2011sunny families (vertical, horizontal, diagonal).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, for instance, that no vertical line is used.  Then every line that\ncovers a point of (3) has a fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate equal to the \\(x\\)-coordinate\nof some other point of (3).  Because the \\(x\\)-coordinates in (3) are all\ndifferent, at most one point of (3) could be covered, contradicting the\nrequirement that all \\(n\\) points be covered.  The same argument applies to\nthe horizontal and diagonal families. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Contradiction for \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two\nrequirements exists and that exactly \\(k\\ge 3\\) of them are sunny.\nConsequently at most \\(n-k\\le n-3\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\nBy Lemma&nbsp;4 we need at least one vertical, one horizontal and one diagonal\nline among the non\u2011sunny ones; therefore the number of non\u2011sunny lines is at\nleast three.  Hence the number of non\u2011sunny lines is **exactly** \\(n-k\\).\n\nEach non\u2011sunny line can realise at most \\(n\\) distinct values of the\ncorresponding quantity (\\(x\\), \\(y\\) or \\(x+y\\)).  With only \\(n-k\\le n-3\\)\nnon\u2011sunny lines we can obtain at most \\(n-3\\) distinct values of each\nquantity.  However the set (3) requires \\(n\\) distinct values of each\nquantity (Lemma&nbsp;3).  This is impossible.\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines cannot exist. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of \\(n\\) lines that satisfy the covering\ncondition and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the following \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\\\\n&y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2,\\\\\n&x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\) satisfies at least one of the three equations:\nif \\(a\\le n-2\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); if \\(b\\le n-2\\) it lies on\nthe horizontal line \\(y=b\\); otherwise \\(a+b=n+1\\) and it lies on the diagonal\nline.  All lines are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\qquad x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nand add the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) through the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\).\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) covers the point \\((1,1)\\), which is not covered by the\nchosen non\u2011sunny lines; all other points of \\(P_n\\) are covered as in the\ncase \\(k=0\\).  Hence we have exactly one sunny line.\n\n*Case \\(k=2\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) together with the diagonal\nline \\(x+y=n+1\\).  These \\(n-1\\) lines miss precisely the two points\n\\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  Add the two sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;(1,1)\\text{\u2013}(n,2),\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;(2,1)\\text{\u2013}(n,3).\n\\]\n\nEach of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) covers one of the missing points, and by Lemma&nbsp;2\nthey do not introduce any further uncovered point.  Thus all points of\n\\(P_n\\) are covered and exactly two lines are sunny.\n\nIn each of the three constructions the total number of lines is \\(n\\) and the\nrequired covering property holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nNo configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines exists, while configurations\nwith \\(k=0,1,2\\) do exist for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Hence the set of all\nnon\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]"
