"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines in a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each of the three values a concrete construction is given, and it is shown that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   \\[\n   P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Geometric lemmas.**  \n   * Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(P_n\\).  \n   * Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 if a sunny line meets a non\u2011sunny line at a point of \\(P_n\\) it cannot contain any other point of \\(P_n\\).  \n   * Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(x+y\\).  \n   * Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 to cover those \\(n\\) points one needs at least one line of each of the three non\u2011sunny families (vertical, horizontal, diagonal).\n\n3. **Upper bound on the number of points that can be covered.**  \n   * A non\u2011sunny line (vertical, horizontal or diagonal) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(P_n\\).  \n   * By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line contributes at most two points, and by Lemma\u202f2 at most one *new* point (a point not already on a non\u2011sunny line).  \n   Hence with \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\,n+2k\\;=\\;n^{2}-kn+2k .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Deriving a numerical restriction on \\(k\\).**  \n   From (1) and (2) we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-kn+2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\(k\\) (recall \\(n\\ge3\\)) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\frac{n^{2}-n}{2(n-2)}<3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\(k\\le 2\\).\n\n5. **Excluding the case \\(k=3\\) by a combinatorial argument.**  \n   If \\(k=3\\) then the three sunny lines together cover at most six points of \\(P_n\\).  \n   The remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-6\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma\u202f4 at least one vertical, one horizontal and one diagonal line are needed, so at most \\(n-4\\) lines are left to cover the rest.  \n   Each of those remaining lines can introduce at most one new value of the corresponding coordinate sum, and therefore they can realise at most \\(n-4\\) distinct values of each of the three quantities \\(x,\\;y,\\;x+y\\).  \n   Yet the set of points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) (Lemma\u202f3) requires \\(n\\) distinct values of each quantity.  This is impossible, so a configuration with \\(k=3\\) cannot exist.\n\n6. **Constructing configurations for \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\qquad\n   y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2,\\qquad\n   x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(P_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\qquad x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   and add the sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\).  The sunny line supplies the only point \\((1,1)\\) not covered by the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  Take the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) together with the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  These miss exactly the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  Add the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}:\\;(1,1)\\text{\u2013}(n,2),\\qquad\n   \\ell_{2}:\\;(2,1)\\text{\u2013}(n,3),\n   \\]\n\n   which cover the missing points.  All points of \\(P_n\\) are now covered.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. The set of points to be covered  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are \\(s-1\\) points with\n\\(a+b=s\\); summing gives  \n\n\\[\n|P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2. Non\u2011sunny and sunny lines  \n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad\n\\ell_y:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad\n\\ell_d:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are called **sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Lemmas about the geometry of \\(P_n\\)\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in P_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).\nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals the slope between any two of these points,\nhence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n=\n\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}\n=\n\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nIf the common slope were \\(0\\) the line would be horizontal,\nif it were undefined the line would be vertical,\nand if it were \\(-1\\) the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\nAll three possibilities are excluded by the definition of sunny,\nso a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a sunny line meets a non\u2011sunny line at a point of \\(P_n\\), then it contains no other point of \\(P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell_s\\) be sunny and \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) be non\u2011sunny, and suppose they intersect\nat \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\).\nIf \\(\\ell_s\\) contained another point \\((a',b')\\in P_n\\), then the two distinct\nintersection points \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) would lie on both \\(\\ell_s\\) and\n\\(\\ell_{ns}\\).  Two distinct lines cannot have two common points; therefore\n\\(\\ell_s\\) and \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) would have to coincide, which would make\n\\(\\ell_s\\) non\u2011sunny \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,\\;(n,1) \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nhave pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct\nvalues of \\(x+y\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Immediate from the description (2). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 4  \nTo cover the set (2) one needs at least one line of each of the three\nnon\u2011sunny families (vertical, horizontal, diagonal).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, for instance, that no vertical line is used.\nEvery line that covers a point of (2) then has a fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate equal\nto the \\(x\\)-coordinate of some other point of (2).  Because the \\(x\\)-coordinates\nin (2) are all different, at most one point of (2) could be covered, contradicting\nthe requirement that all \\(n\\) points be covered.  The same argument applies\nto the horizontal and diagonal families. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4. Upper bound on the number of points that can be covered\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(P_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.  \nThe same bound holds for horizontal lines and for diagonal lines\n\\(x+y=c\\) (the admissible values of \\(c\\) are \\(2,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  \nThus each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(P_n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(P_n\\).  \nMoreover, by Lemma\u202f2 at most one of those points can be *new* (i.e. not already\nlying on a non\u2011sunny line).  Consequently a sunny line contributes at most\ntwo points to the total count, and at most one point that is not already\ncovered by the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nLet the configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nThe total number of points of \\(P_n\\) that can be covered is therefore bounded by  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n+2k=n^{2}-kn+2k .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5. Numerical restriction on \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (1) we must have  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-kn+2k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n^{2}-n}{2(n-2)} .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the right\u2013hand side of (4) is strictly smaller than\n\\(3\\) (it equals \\(3\\) for \\(n=3,4\\) and decreases afterwards).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6. Excluding the case \\(k=3\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(k=3\\) sunny lines exists.\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the three sunny lines together cover at most six points of \\(P_n\\);\nthe remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-6\\) points must be covered by the\n\\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nLemma\u202f4 tells us that among those \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines there must be at\nleast one vertical, one horizontal and one diagonal line.\nConsequently at most \\(n-4\\) non\u2011sunny lines are left to provide additional\nvalues of the three quantities \\(x,\\;y,\\;x+y\\).\n\nEach vertical line introduces at most one new \\(x\\)-value, each horizontal line\nat most one new \\(y\\)-value, and each diagonal line at most one new value of\n\\(x+y\\).  Hence with at most \\(n-4\\) lines we can realise at most \\(n-4\\)\ndistinct values of each of the three quantities.\n\nHowever the set of points (2) (Lemma\u202f3) requires **\\(n\\)** distinct values of\n\\(x\\), of \\(y\\) and of \\(x+y\\).  This is impossible, so a configuration with\n\\(k=3\\) cannot exist.  Together with (5) we obtain the final restriction  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7. Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nAll constructions use distinct lines.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\\\\n  &y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2,\\\\\n  &x+y=n+1 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\) satisfies at least one of the three equations,\n  so the covering condition holds.  All lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\qquad x+y=n+1,\n  \\]\n\n  and add the sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\).  \n  The sunny line supplies the only point \\((1,1)\\) not covered by the\n  non\u2011sunny lines; all other points are covered as before.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) together with the\n  diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  These miss exactly the points \\((1,1)\\) and\n  \\((2,1)\\).  Add the two sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{1}:\\;(1,1)\\text{\u2013}(n,2),\\qquad\n  \\ell_{2}:\\;(2,1)\\text{\u2013}(n,3),\n  \\]\n\n  which cover the missing points.  No other point of \\(P_n\\) is affected,\n  so the covering condition is satisfied.\n\nIn each case the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\) and the required\nproperty holds.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem\nis precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]"
