If a sunny line meets a non\u2011sunny line at a point of \\(P_n\\), then it contains no other point of \\(P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell_s\\) be sunny and \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) be non\u2011sunny, and suppose they intersect\nat \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\).\nIf \\(\\ell_s\\) contained another point \\((a',b')\\in P_n\\), then the two distinct\nintersection points \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) would lie on both \\(\\ell_s\\) and\n\\(\\ell_{ns}\\).  Two distinct lines cannot have two common points; therefore\n\\(\\ell_s\\) and \\(\\ell_{ns}\\) would have to coincide, which would make\n\\(\\ell_s\\) non\u2011sunny \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,\\;(n,1) \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nhave pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct\nvalues of \\(x+y\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Immediate from the description (2). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 4  \nTo cover the set (2) one needs at least one line of each of the three\nnon\u2011sunny families (vertical, horizontal, diagonal).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, for instance, that no vertical line is used.\nEvery line that covers a point of (2) then has a fixed \\(x\\)-coordinate equal\nto the \\(x\\)-coordinate of some other point of (2).  Because the \\(x\\)-coordinates\nin (2) are all different, at most one point of (2) could be covered, contradicting\nthe requirement that all \\(n\\) points be covered.  The same argument applies\nto the horizontal and diagonal families. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4. Upper bound on the number of points that can be covered\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(P_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.  \nThe same bound holds for horizontal lines and for diagonal lines\n\\(x+y=c\\) (the admissible values of \\(c\\) are \\(2,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  \nThus each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(P_n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(P_n\\).  \nMoreover, by Lemma\u202f2 at most one of those points can be *new* (i.e. not already\nlying on a non\u2011sunny line).  Consequently a sunny line contributes at most\ntwo points to the total count, and at most one point that is not already\ncovered by the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nLet the configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nThe total number of points of \\(P_n\\) that can be covered is therefore bounded by  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\,n+2k=n^{2}-kn+2k .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5. Numerical restriction on \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (1) we must have  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-kn+2k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\frac{n^{2}-n}{2(n-2)} .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the right\u2013hand side of (4) is strictly smaller than\n\\(3\\) (it equals \\(3\\) for \\(n=3,4\\) and decreases afterwards).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6. Excluding the case \\(k=3\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(k=3\\) sunny lines exists.\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the three sunny lines together cover at most six points of \\(P_n\\);\nthe remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-6\\) points must be covered by the\n\\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nLemma\u202f4 tells us that among those \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines there must be at\nleast one vertical, one horizontal and one diagonal line.\nConsequently at most \\(n-4\\) non\u2011sunny lines are left to provide additional\nvalues of the three quantities \\(x,\\;y,\\;x+y\\).\n\nEach vertical line introduces at most one new \\(x\\)-value, each horizontal line\nat most one new \\(y\\)-value, and each diagonal line at most one new value of\n\\(x+y\\).  Hence with at most \\(n-4\\) lines we can realise at most \\(n-4\\)\ndistinct values of each of the three quantities.\n\nHowever the set of points (2) (Lemma\u202f3) requires **\\(n\\)** distinct values of\n\\(x\\), of \\(y\\) and of \\(x