 n+s\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(h+v+s=n-k\\) and the trivial bound \\(s\\le n-k\\), inequality (1) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   n(h+v)+s\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   n\\bigl((n-k)-s\\bigr)+s\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The left\u2011hand side is maximised when \\(s=0\\); therefore a necessary condition is  \n\n   \\[\n   n(n-k)\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\ge n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines; equivalently, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\ge n-2\\).\n\nThe argument above is completely rigorous and uses only elementary counting together with the pigeon\u2011hole principle.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=d\\)).\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition of the problem.\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nh=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nv=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\ns=\\#\\{\\text{slope }-1\\text{ lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nk=n-(h+v+s).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,c)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+c\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n+1-c\\).\nHence the admissible values of \\(a\\) are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\), whose number is at most \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSymmetric to Lemma\u202f1. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=d\\), contains at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(x+y=d\\) then \\(a+b=d\\).  \nSince \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the integer \\(d\\) must satisfy \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(d\\) there is exactly one ordered pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=d\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); thus at most one point of \\(S_n\\) lies on the line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting the points covered by non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nBy Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by the\n\\(h\\) horizontal, \\(v\\) vertical and \\(s\\) slope \\(-1\\) lines is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}\\le h\\cdot n+v\\cdot n+s\\cdot 1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(S_n\\) must belong to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\),\nwe must have  \n\n\\[\nh n+v n+s\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Deriving a lower bound for \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (2) we have \\(h+v+s=n-k\\), whence \\(h+v=n-k-s\\).\nInsert this into (4):\n\n\\[\nn\\bigl(h+v\\bigr)+s\n= n\\bigl(n-k-s\\bigr)+s\n\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side is a decreasing function of \\(s\\) (its derivative with respect to \\(s\\) is \\(-n+1\\le 0\\) because \\(n\\ge 3\\)).\nHence the strongest (i.e. hardest to satisfy) inequality is obtained when \\(s=0\\):\n\\[\nn\\bigl(n-k\\bigr)\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nDividing by the positive integer \\(n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge \\frac{n+1}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge n-\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer and \\(n\\ge 3\\), the inequality \\(k\\ge n-2\\) follows (the bound \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) is weaker for \\(n\\ge 3\\); the integer\u2011valued bound that is always satisfied is \\(k\\ge n-2\\)).  \n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved, using only elementary counting and the pigeon\u2011hole principle, that for a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations attaining each of these three values remains open in this partial solution. The necessary condition \\(k\\ge n-2\\) is established rigorously. \u220e"
