hat every lattice point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines:\n   - If \\(b\\le n-k\\) the point is on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   - Otherwise \\(b>n-k\\). Then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le k\\); consequently \\(a-b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\}\\) and the point lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+d_i\\) with \\(d_i=a-b\\).\n\n   All lines are distinct (different constants give different lines) and the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\). Hence a configuration exists for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n   Combining the upper bound and the construction yields the desired characterisation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThus the three families of *non\u2011sunny* lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Upper bound)\n\nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the conditions of the problem, then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.* By definition the configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, each of which is either sunny or non\u2011sunny. Hence the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\). The lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) is obvious. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are sunny,  \n2. every point of \\