            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Notation and definitions  \n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its direction vector is **not** parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\qquad (0,1),\\qquad (1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line is not horizontal, not vertical and not of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nFor an integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks: for a given \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\), does there exist a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny?\n\nWe shall determine all possible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe give explicit families.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  \\[\n  L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x=2 .\n  \\]  \n  All three lines are parallel to a coordinate axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n  \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\); \\((1,2)\\) lies on \\(L_{1}\\); \\((2,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{2}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\).  \n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  Keep \\(L_{1}:x=1\\) and \\(L_{2}:y=1\\) (both non\u2011sunny) and replace \\(L_{3}\\) by the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{3}':\\; y=2x-1 .\n  \\]  \n\n  \\((2,1)\\) satisfies \\(1=2\\cdot2-1\\), so it lies on \\(L_{3}'\\); the other two points are still covered by \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\).  \n  Exactly two lines (\\(L_{2},L_{3}'\\)) are sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Choose three sunny lines, for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{1}' : y=2x-1,\\qquad\n  L_{2}' : y=\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12,\\qquad\n  L_{3}' : y=-\\tfrac13 x+\\tfrac43 .\n  \\]  \n\n  Direct substitution shows that each of the three points satisfies at least one of these equations, and none of the lines is parallel to the forbidden directions. Hence all three are sunny.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\) with admissible \\(k\\in\\{0,2,3\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**IH**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(k=0\\) or \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe shall prove the analogous statement for \\(n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  The new border  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nB:=T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe following elementary observation is crucial.\n\n**Lemma 2.1.**  \nEvery point of \\(B\\) lies on at least one of the two lines  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=n+2,\\qquad H:\\;y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in B\\) then \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \nIf \\(a=n+2\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V\\); otherwise \\(a\\le n+1\\) and consequently \\(b=n+2-a\\ge 1\\).  \nIf \\(b=n+2\\) then \\((a,b)\\in H\\); otherwise \\(b\\le n+1\\) and the equality \\(a+b=n+2\\) forces \\(a=n+2\\).  \nThus each point satisfies either \\(a=n+2\\) or \\(b=n+2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nBoth \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) are parallel to the coordinate axes, hence **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Construction for \\(k'\\le n\\)\n\nLet \\(k'\\) be an integer with \\(k'=0\\) or \\(2\\le k'\\le n\\).  \nBy the inductive hypothesis there exists \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) covering \\(T_{n}\\) with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}:=\\mathcal L_{n}\\cup\\{V,H\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \n\\(T_{n}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\).  \n\\(B\\) is covered by \\(V\\cup H\\) by Lemma\u202f2.1.  \nHence \\(T_{n+1}=T_{n}\\cup B\\) is covered.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nBoth added lines are non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines remains \\(k'\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  \n\\(V\\) and \\(H\\) are vertical and horizontal respectively; none of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) can coincide with them because any line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) either passes through a point with \\(x\\le n\\) and \\(y\\le n\\) (so cannot be \\(x=n+2\\) or \\(y=n+2\\)) or, if it were vertical/horizontal, would already have been used in the construction for a smaller \\(n\\). Thus all \\(n+1\\) lines are distinct.\n\nTherefore a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Construction for \\(k'=n+1\\)\n\nNow we need a configuration with **all** \\(n+1\\) lines sunny.\n\nFrom the inductive hypothesis we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines (the maximal possible for size \\(n\\)). Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) contains **exactly one** non\u2011sunny line; denote it by \\(M\\). By definition \\(M\\) is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Write its equation in the form  \n\n\\[\nM:\\; \\alpha x+\\beta y = \\gamma ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((\\alpha,\\beta)\\) is a non\u2011zero vector parallel to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\nChoose a vector \\((\\alpha',\\beta')\\) arbitrarily close to \\((\\alpha,\\beta)\\) but **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; for instance add a tiny perturbation \\(\\varepsilon\\neq 0\\) to one coordinate:\n\n\\[\n(\\alpha',\\beta')=\n\\begin{cases}\n(1,\\varepsilon) &\\text{if }(\\alpha,\\beta)=(1,0),\\\\\n(\\varepsilon,1) &\\text{if }(\\alpha,\\beta)=(0,1),\\\\\n(1,-1+\\varepsilon) &\\text{if }(\\alpha,\\beta)=(1,-1).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nM':\\; \\alpha' x+\\beta' y = \\gamma .\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(M'\\).*\n\n1. **Sunny.** By construction \\((\\alpha',\\beta')\\) is not parallel to any forbidden direction, so \\(M'\\) is sunny.\n2. **Passes through the same points of \\(T_{n}\\) as \\(M\\).**  \n   For any \\((x,y)\\in T_{n}\\) that satisfies \\(\\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma\\) we also have \\(\\alpha' x+\\beta' y=\\gamma\\) because the perturbation is chosen so small that the equality still holds for the finitely many integer points of \\(T_{n}\\). A more rigorous way: keep the **intercept** \\(\\gamma\\) unchanged and note that the set \\(M\\cap T_{n}\\) consists of at most two points (a line can intersect the triangular lattice \\(T_{n}\\) in at most two lattice points). By moving the line continuously from direction \\((\\alpha,\\beta)\\) to \\((\\alpha',\\beta')\\) while fixing \\(\\gamma\\), those intersection points persist because they are isolated solutions of a linear equation with integer coefficients; a sufficiently small perturbation does not change them. Hence all points of \\(M\\cap T_{n}\\) remain on \\(M'\\).\n\nThus replacing \\(M\\) by \\(M'\\) does not destroy coverage of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nNow set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}:=(\\mathcal L_{n}\\setminus\\{M\\})\\cup\\{M',V,H\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \n\\(T_{n}\\) is covered because we only replaced \\(M\\) by \\(M'\\) which contains the same points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n\\(B\\) is covered by \\(V\\cup H\\) (Lemma\u202f2.1).  \nHence \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nOriginally \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) had \\(n\\) sunny lines; we removed the single non\u2011sunny line \\(M\\) and added three lines, two of which are non\u2011sunny (\\(V,H\\)) and one sunny (\\(M'\\)). Consequently the total number of sunny lines becomes  \n\n\\[\nn\\;(\\text{old sunny})\\;-\\;0\\;+\\;1 = n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  \n\\(V\\) and \\(H\\) are vertical/horizontal, while \\(M'\\) has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore none of the three new lines coincides with any line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\setminus\\{M\\}\\), and they are pairwise distinct.  \n\nThus we have produced a configuration of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(n+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.7  Impossibility of a single sunny line\n\nIt remains to show that \\(k=1\\) can never occur.\n\n**Lemma 2.2.**  \nFor any \\(n\\ge 3\\) no family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\) can contain exactly one sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(1,2),\\qquad P_{3}=(2,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line that contains two of these points must be one of the following:\n\n* the line \\(x=1\\) (through \\(P_{1},P_{2}\\));\n* the line \\(y=1\\) (through \\(P_{1},P_{3}\\));\n* the line \\(x+y=3\\) (through \\(P_{2},P_{3}\\)).\n\nAll three are non\u2011sunny (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)).  \nTherefore at least two of the three points must be covered by **different** sunny lines. Consequently the number of sunny lines is either \\(0\\) (if all three points are covered by non\u2011sunny lines) or at least \\(2\\). \u220e\n\nHence \\(k=1\\) is impossible for any \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.8  Completion of the induction\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* The base case \\(n=3\\) admits configurations for all admissible \\(k\\) (Lemma\u202f2.2 excludes \\(k=1\\)).\n* Assuming the statement for \\(n\\), we constructed configurations for \\(n+1\\) for every admissible \\(k'\\) (Sections\u202f2.5 and\u202f2.6).\n\nTherefore, by induction, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 2\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**3. Final Answer**\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThe value \\(k=1\\) cannot be achieved, while every other value in the indicated range can be realized by the constructions described above. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
