rder\u2019\u2019  \n\nFor a given \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nB:=T_{n+1}\\setminus T_{n}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(B\\) lie on the single line  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}:x+y=n+2 .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_{n+1}\\) has slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nMoreover, for any \\(n\\) no line of a configuration that already covers \\(T_{n}\\) can be equal to \\(S_{n+1}\\), because every line that meets \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies an equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\le n+1\\), whereas \\(S_{n+1}\\) has constant term \\(n+2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Base configurations  \n\n#### 3.1  A configuration with **no** sunny lines  \n\nFor any \\(n\\ge 3\\) the three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=n+1\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nare respectively vertical, horizontal and of slope \\(-1\\); hence all are non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies at least one of the following:\n\n* if \\(a=1\\) then it lies on \\(L_{1}\\);\n* else if \\(b=1\\) then it lies on \\(L_{2}\\);\n* else \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{3}\\).\n\nThus (3.1) covers \\(T_{n}\\) with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.2  A configuration with **all** lines sunny  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  \nWe claim that the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{n}:=\\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{n}\\}\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\). Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) we can write  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{a-1}{a}\\,a + \\frac{1}{a}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_{a-1}\\) (note that \\(1\\le a-1\\le n-1\\); for the case \\(a=n\\) we use \\(S_{n}\\), 