w how to obtain a configuration of the same size \\(m\\) with \\(s+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause \\(s\\le m-1\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) is non\u2011sunny; call it \\(N\\).  \nA non\u2011sunny line is either\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nIn each case the line contains **at most two** points of the lattice set \\(T_{m}\\) (a vertical or horizontal line meets the triangle in a single column or row, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets it in at most two points because the sum of coordinates is constant). Let those points be \\(P\\) and possibly \\(Q\\) (they may coincide if the line meets the triangle in a single lattice point).\n\nChoose a sunny line \\(N'\\) that passes through **the same two points** \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\). This is always possible because two distinct points determine a unique line, and the line determined by \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) cannot be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\) unless the two points themselves already lie on such a forbidden direction. But if that were the case, the original line \\(N\\) would already be sunny, contradicting the choice of \\(N\\). Hence the line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is automatically sunny.\n\nReplace \\(N\\) by \\(N'\\) and keep all other lines unchanged. The new family still covers every point of \\(T_{m}\\) (the points formerly covered by \\(N\\) are now covered by \\(N'\\); all other points are untouched). The number of sunny lines has increased by one, and the total number of lines stays \\(m\\).\n\nApplying this replacement repeatedly we can raise the sunny count from \\(0\\) up to \\(m\\). Because the case \\(k=1\\) is excluded by the argument of the next section, we obtain configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Impossibility of a single sunny line  \n\nConsider the three lattice points  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,1),\\qquad B=(1,2),\\qquad C=