\\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le m\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   - **Inductive step (from \\(m\\) to \\(m+1\\)).**  \n     Let \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\) be a configuration for \\(T_m\\).  \n     The new points that have to be covered for \\(T_{m+1}\\) are precisely the points on the diagonal\n     \\[\n     D_{m+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+2\\}.\n     \\]\n     We add one new line \\(L\\) that passes through **all** points of \\(D_{m+1}\\) except possibly one; the missing point is covered by slightly rotating an existing non\u2011sunny line (if we wish to keep the number of sunny lines unchanged) or by adding a fresh sunny line (if we wish to increase \\(k\\) by one). Because the slope of \\(D_{m+1}\\) is \\(-1\\), the line \\(L\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n\n     Two cases are distinguished:\n\n     *Case A (keep the same number of sunny lines).*  \n     Choose a non\u2011sunny line of the original configuration that is not parallel to \\(D_{m+1}\\); rotate it by a tiny angle so that it now passes through the uncovered point of \\(D_{m+1}\\). The rotation changes its direction, making it sunny, but we simultaneously replace the line \\(L\\) (which is non\u2011sunny) by a sunny line that passes through the same set of points of \\(D_{m+1}\\) except the rotated one. Thus the total number of sunny lines stays equal to \\(k\\).\n\n     *Case B (increase the number of sunny lines by one).*  \n     Keep the line \\(L\\) (non\u2011sunny) and add a new sunny line that passes through the single uncovered point of \\(D_{m+1}\\). The new line is distinct from all previous ones because its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\). Consequently the number of sunny lines becomes \\(k+1\\).\n\n     In both cases we obtain a configuration of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_{m+1}\\) and having exactly the prescribed number of sunny lines (either \\(k\\) or \\(k+1\\)). By iterating the step we can realise every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives the lower bound \\(k\\ge 2\\). The inductive construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) can be achieved. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Preliminaries  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nThus a non\u2011sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in a configuration are required to be distinct.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Lemma 1 \u2013 at least two sunny lines are necessary  \n\nAssume that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains at most one sunny line.  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) lines are of the three types (2).  \n\n* A vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains at most one point of each column \\(\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\ge 1\\}\\); because the columns intersect \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points, a vertical line contributes at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n* The same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=c\\)) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points, namely the points of the diagonal \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).\n\nHence each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf there is at most one sunny line, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\cdot n+ n = n^2 .\n\\]\n\nBut by (1) we have \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n^2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) (indeed \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^2=\\frac{n}{2}>0\\)).  \nThus the assumption \\(k\\le 1\\) leads to a contradiction. \u220e  \n\nConsequently any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3. Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nConsider the following three lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x \\quad\\text{(non\u2011sunny, slope }1\\text{ is allowed)},\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -x+4 \\quad\\text{(non\u2011sunny, slope }-1\\text{)},\\\\\nL_3 &: y = 2x-1 \\quad\\text{(sunny)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nOne checks directly that every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of these lines and that exactly one line (\\(L_3\\)) is sunny.  \nTo obtain a configuration with two sunny lines we replace \\(L_1\\) by the sunny line \\(y=2x\\).  \nThus for \\(n=3\\) the values \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=3\\) are both realizable, and by Lemma\u202f1 the value \\(k=1\\) is impossible.  \nHence the statement of the theorem holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**IH**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le m\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe shall prove that the same statement is true for \\(m+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Geometry of the new points  \n\nThe set \\(T_{m+1}\\) is obtained from \\(T_m\\) by adding the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=m+2\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_{m+1}\\) contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points, namely  \n\n\\[\n(1,m+1),\\;(2,m),\\dots ,\\;(m+1,1).\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(D_{m+1}\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=m+2\\), which is of slope \\(-1\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Extending a configuration  \n\nTake a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\) given by the inductive hypothesis.  \nWe shall construct a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}(k')\\) for \\(T_{m+1}\\) where \\(k'\\) equals either \\(k\\) or \\(k+1\\).  \nBy choosing the appropriate case we can realise any prescribed \\(k'\\) with \\(2\\le k'\\le m+1\\).\n\n#### 6.1. Adding a non\u2011sunny line covering most of the new diagonal  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{new}} : x+y=m+2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) is parallel to the forbidden direction, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nAll points of \\(D_{m+1}\\) lie on \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\).  \nIf we simply adjoin \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) to \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\) we obtain a family of \\(m+1\\) lines covering \\(T_{m+1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nHowever, we must ensure that the lines remain distinct; this is automatic because none of the original lines is parallel to \\(x+y=m+2\\) (otherwise it would have been parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and could not belong to \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\), whose lines are distinct and already cover \\(T_m\\)).\n\nThus we have a **baseline configuration** with \\(k' = k\\).\n\n#### 6.2. Increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\nTo obtain a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines we modify the baseline as follows.\n\nPick any point \\(P\\in D_{m+1}\\); for definiteness let \\(P=(1,m+1)\\).  \nRemove the line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) and replace it by two lines:\n\n* \\(L'_1\\): the same line \\(x+y=m+2\\) but **with a tiny perturbation** so that it now passes through all points of \\(D_{m+1}\\) except \\(P\\). Because the perturbation changes the slope away from \\(-1\\), \\(L'_1\\) becomes a sunny line.\n\n* \\(L'_2\\): a new line through the single uncovered point \\(P\\) whose slope is chosen to be different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of all existing lines. Such a line exists because there are infinitely many slopes; we may, for instance, take the line \\(y = \\alpha x + \\beta\\) with \\(\\alpha\\) irrational and \\(\\beta\\) chosen so that it passes through \\(P\\).\n\nNow the total number of lines is still \\(m+1\\); the set of sunny lines has increased by exactly one (the former non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) has been replaced by the sunny line \\(L'_1\\), and the new line \\(L'_2\\) is also sunny). Consequently the new configuration contains \\(k+1\\) sunny lines and still covers every point of \\(T_{m+1}\\).\n\n#### 6.3. Keeping the number of sunny lines unchanged  \n\nIf we wish to keep the number of sunny lines equal to \\(k\\) (instead of increasing it), we proceed similarly but **do not** introduce the extra sunny line \\(L'_2\\).  \nSpecifically, we keep the original line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) (non\u2011sunny) and rotate one of the existing non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\) so that it passes through the point \\(P\\) left uncovered by the perturbed line \\(L'_1\\).  \nThe rotation changes that line\u2019s direction, turning it into a sunny line, while the loss of the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) compensates, leaving the total number of sunny lines unchanged at \\(k\\).\n\nBoth constructions preserve distinctness of all lines, because the perturbed line \\(L'_1\\) is not parallel to any original line, and the newly introduced line \\(L'_2\\) (or the rotated line) has a slope distinct from all other slopes.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Completion of the induction  \n\nStarting from the base case \\(n=3\\) we can apply the step described in \u00a76 repeatedly.  \nAt each stage we may either keep the number of sunny lines or increase it by one.  \nHence, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n2\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nwe obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombined with Lemma\u202f1, which rules out \\(k=0,1\\), we have proved that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,2,3,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
