            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1. Preliminaries  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \nThus a non\u2011sunny line has one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in a configuration are required to be distinct.\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. Lemma 1 \u2013 at least two sunny lines are necessary  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains at most one sunny line.  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) lines are of the three types (2.1).\n\n* A vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets each column \\(\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\ge1\\}\\) in at most one point; the column intersected with \\(T_n\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points, so a vertical line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n* The same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).\n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=c\\)) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points, namely the points of the diagonal \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).\n\nHence each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf there is at most one sunny line, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\cdot n + n = n^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBut by (2.2) we have \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (since \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^{2}= \\frac{n}{2}>0\\)).  \nThus a configuration with \\(k\\le1\\) cannot exist. \u220e  \n\nTherefore any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.3. Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nConsider the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x \\quad\\text{(non\u2011sunny, slope }1\\text{ is allowed)},\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -x+4 \\quad\\text{(non\u2011sunny, slope }-1\\text{)},\\\\\nL_3 &: y = 2x-1 \\quad\\text{(sunny)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows that every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of these lines and that exactly one line (\\(L_3\\)) is sunny.  \nReplacing \\(L_1\\) by the sunny line \\(y=2x\\) yields a configuration with **two** sunny lines; adding a further sunny line (for instance \\(y=3x-2\\)) gives a configuration with **three** sunny lines.  \nThus for \\(n=3\\) the values \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=3\\) are realizable, and by Lemma\u202f1 the value \\(k=1\\) is impossible. The statement of the theorem holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some integer \\(m\\ge3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**IH**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le m\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5. Inductive step (from \\(m\\) to \\(m+1\\))\n\nThe set \\(T_{m+1}\\) is obtained from \\(T_m\\) by adding the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=m+2\\}.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n\\(D_{m+1}\\) contains \\(m+1\\) points and lies on the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{new}}: x+y=m+2,\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\nTake a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\) given by the inductive hypothesis.\n\n#### 2.5.1. Baseline configuration (keeps the number of sunny lines)\n\nAdd the line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) to \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\).  \nAll points of \\(D_{m+1}\\) lie on \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\); the other points are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\).  \nSince none of the original lines is parallel to \\(x+y=m+2\\) (otherwise it would be of the forbidden form (2.1) and could not belong to \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\)), the new line is distinct from all previous ones.  \nThus we obtain a family of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{m+1}\\) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.5.2. Increasing the number of sunny lines by one\n\nSelect a point \\(P\\in D_{m+1}\\); for definiteness let \\(P=(1,m+1)\\).\n\n1. **Replace \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) by a sunny line.**  \n   Define \\(L'_1\\) to be the unique line passing through all points of \\(D_{m+1}\\) except \\(P\\).  \n   Because the set of points is not collinear (the missing point prevents collinearity with slope \\(-1\\)), the slope of \\(L'_1\\) differs from \\(-1\\); hence \\(L'_1\\) is sunny.  \n   \\(L'_1\\) is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\) because its slope is new.\n\n2. **Add a sunny line through the uncovered point.**  \n   Choose any slope \\(\\sigma\\) with \\(\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and \\(\\sigma\\) different from the slopes of all lines already present.  \n   Let \\(L'_2\\) be the line of slope \\(\\sigma\\) passing through \\(P\\).  \n   By construction \\(L'_2\\) is sunny and distinct from all existing lines.\n\nNow the total number of lines is \\((m-0)+2 = m+1\\).  \nCompared with the baseline configuration we have removed the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) and added two sunny lines \\(L'_1\\) and \\(L'_2\\); consequently the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one, from \\(k\\) to \\(k+1\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_{m+1}\\) are covered: points of \\(D_{m+1}\\setminus\\{P\\}\\) lie on \\(L'_1\\), the point \\(P\\) lies on \\(L'_2\\), and the remaining points are covered by the unchanged lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\).\n\nThus we have constructed a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}(k+1)\\).\n\n#### 2.5.3. Keeping the number of sunny lines unchanged (alternative)\n\nIf we wish to obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (instead of \\(k+1\\)), we proceed as follows.\n\n* Keep the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) in the family.  \n* Choose a non\u2011sunny line \\(M\\) from \\(\\mathcal L_m(k)\\) that is **not** parallel to \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) (such a line exists because at most two of the three forbidden directions can be represented among the non\u2011sunny lines).  \n* Rotate \\(M\\) by an arbitrarily small angle so that it now passes through the point \\(P\\). The rotation changes its slope away from the forbidden set, turning \\(M\\) into a sunny line.  \n\nThe rotation does not create a coincidence with any existing line because the new slope is distinct from all previous slopes.  \nConsequently the total number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\) (the former sunny line count is unchanged, the rotated line becomes sunny, and the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) stays non\u2011sunny).  \nAll points are still covered: \\(P\\) is now on the rotated line, the other points of \\(D_{m+1}\\) lie on \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\), and the rest of \\(T_{m+1}\\) is covered by the unchanged lines.\n\nHence a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}(k)\\) is also obtainable.\n\n#### 2.5.4. Completion of the induction\n\nStarting from the base case \\(n=3\\) we can apply the step of \u00a72.5 repeatedly.  \nAt each stage we may either keep the current number of sunny lines or increase it by one; therefore for any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) we can construct a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nCombined with Lemma\u202f1, which shows that \\(k\\ge2\\) is necessary, we obtain the full description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is admissible }\\iff k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.}\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
