"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following two necessary conditions that any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy:\n\n1.  \\(k\\ge \\left\\lceil \\dfrac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil\\);\n2.  \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\nConsequently the set of possible \\(k\\) is contained in the interval  \n\\[\n\\Bigl\\{\\; \\left\\lceil \\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil,\\;\n\\left\\lceil \\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\;\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe proof rests on two lemmas concerning the covering of the lattice points\n\\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) by lines that are either *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions) or *non\u2011sunny* (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Step 1 \u2013 Counting points on a non\u2011sunny line.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis can contain at most one point of the set, because all points have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates. The same holds for a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis. A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\); it can contain at most one point of the set as well, because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces all admissible points to have distinct values of \\(a+b\\). Hence each non\u2011sunny line covers **at most one** of the required points.\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 Lower bound for the number of sunny lines.*  \nThere are exactly \\(\\displaystyle N=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points that must be covered (the number of integer pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)). Since each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one point, at most three of the \\(n\\) lines can be non\u2011sunny (otherwise we would have more than \\(n\\) lines covering at most \\(n\\) points). Consequently at least \\(n-3\\) of the lines must be sunny. However, a sunny line can also cover at most two of the required points: if a sunny line contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) then the slope is \\(\\dfrac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\), which cannot be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence each sunny line can cover **at most two** points.  \n\nIf we let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\\[\n2s + (n-s) = s + n .\n\\]\nSince we must cover \\(N\\) points we need \\(s+n\\ge N\\), i.e.  \n\\[\ns \\ge N-n = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(s\\le n\\), this inequality forces  \n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\le 3 .\n\\]\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) the above crude estimate is too weak; we refine it by observing that a sunny line can never contain three points of the set (otherwise two of the three points would have the same \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinate or would satisfy \\(a+b=c\\) for a constant \\(c\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line). Hence the bound \u201cat most two points per sunny line\u2019\u2019 is sharp.\n\nCounting more carefully, let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines and let \\(t=n-s\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines. The total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(2s+t\\). Since we must cover \\(N\\) points we obtain\n\\[\n2s+t \\ge N \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 2s+(n-s)\\ge N \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad s\\ge N-n .\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\ns\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(s\\le n\\), the only possible values of \\(n\\) for which this inequality can hold are \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\). For \\(n\\ge5\\) the inequality is impossible, which shows that **no configuration exists for \\(n\\ge5\\)**.  \n\nFor the admissible values \\(n=3,4\\) we can exhibit explicit constructions (see below) that achieve the lower bound \\(s=\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil\\). In each case the number of sunny lines equals exactly \\(\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil\\); adding any further sunny line would force the total number of lines to exceed \\(n\\). Hence for \\(n=3,4\\) the only possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil\\).\n\n*Step 3 \u2013 Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.*  \nSince a non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one required point, we can have at most three non\u2011sunny lines (one of each forbidden direction). Consequently at least \\(n-3\\) lines must be sunny, giving the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\). A sharper bound follows from the observation that any two sunny lines intersect in a point that is not required to be covered; therefore we can replace a sunny line by a non\u2011sunny one without losing coverage, provided we keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\). This yields the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Step 4 \u2013 Summary of the bounds.*  \nCombining the lower and upper bounds we obtain\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\left\\lceil \\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil \\;\\le\\; k \\;\\le\\; n-2 } .\n\\]\nFor \\(n=3,4\\) the lower and upper bounds coincide, giving a unique admissible value of \\(k\\). For \\(n\\ge5\\) the interval is non\u2011empty, but the construction step (which would show that every integer in the interval can actually be realized) remains open.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most one point of the set  \n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis it has equation \\(y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\). All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates (they are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\)), so at most one point can satisfy \\(y=c\\). The same argument works for a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\). For points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the quantity \\(a+b\\) ranges over the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\), each value occurring exactly once. Hence at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can lie on a given line \\(x+y=c\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nA line that is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions (i.e. a sunny line) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\). The slope of the line is\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\]\nIf two of the three points share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the line would be vertical, contradicting sunniness. If two share the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, the line would be horizontal, again a contradiction. If two satisfy \\(a_i+b_i=a_j+b_j\\), then the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). Since the three points are distinct, at least one of these three possibilities must occur, which is impossible. Hence a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 3.*  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines in a configuration satisfying the covering condition. Then\n\\[\n2s+t\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); by Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line covers at most two points. Therefore the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(s\\) sunny and \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(2s+t\\). Since every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be covered, we must have \\(2s+t\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Corollary 4 (Lower bound for \\(k\\)).*  \nLet \\(k\\) denote the number of sunny lines. Then\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we have \\(2k+(n-k)\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), i.e.\n\\[\nk\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(k\\le n\\), the inequality can hold only when \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le n\\), which is equivalent to \\(n\\le 3\\). For \\(n\\ge3\\) a sharper estimate is obtained by observing that at most three non\u2011sunny lines can be used (one of each forbidden direction). Hence \\(t\\le 3\\) and Lemma\u202f3 yields\n\\[\n2k+3\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)-6}{4}.\n\\]\nA direct verification shows that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the right\u2011hand side is at least \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Corollary 5 (Upper bound for \\(k\\)).*  \n\\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAt most three lines can be non\u2011sunny (Lemma\u202f1). If we had \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines, then only one line would be non\u2011sunny, which could cover at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines could cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points, giving a total of at most \\(2n-1\\) points. Since \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-1\\) for all \\(n\\ge5\\), a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible. For \\(n=3,4\\) a direct inspection shows that \\(k=n-1\\) also fails to meet the covering requirement. Hence \\(k\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Theorem (Necessary conditions).*  \nIf there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem, then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;\\le\\;k\\;\\le\\;n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe lower bound is Corollary\u202f4, the upper bound is Corollary\u202f5. \u220e\n\n\n\n*Partial constructions.*  \n\n- **Case \\(n=3\\).**  \n  The set \\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  Take the three lines  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\; y=1,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\; x=1,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\; x+y=3 .\n  \\]\n  \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are non\u2011sunny, \\(L_3\\) is sunny. Thus \\(k=1=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{3-1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\).\n\n- **Case \\(n=4\\).**  \n  Here \\(\\mathcal P_4=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n  Use the four lines  \n  \\[\n  L_1:\\; y=1,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\; x=1,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\; x+y=5,\\qquad\n  L_4:\\; y=2x-1 .\n  \\]\n  \\(L_1,L_2\\) are non\u2011sunny, \\(L_3,L_4\\) are sunny, covering all six points. Hence \\(k=2=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{4-1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\).\n\nThese constructions show that the lower bound is attainable for \\(n=3,4\\). For larger \\(n\\) the existence of configurations attaining every integer \\(k\\) in the interval remains open. \u220e"
