"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a **complete solution** of the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\;\\Bigr\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. all integers between \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\) and \\(n-2\\) inclusive.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Counting points.**  \n   The required set of lattice points is  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Maximum coverage of a line.**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1.* A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (a *non\u2011sunny* line) contains **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   *Lemma\u202f2.* A sunny line (i.e. not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions) contains **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n3. **Necessary bounds for \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(2k+t\\).  \n   Hence  \n   \\[\n   2k+t\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Because a non\u2011sunny line can be of at most three different directions, we have \\(t\\le 3\\). Substituting \\(t=n-k\\le 3\\) into the inequality \\(2k+t\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|\\) gives the sharper lower bound  \n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, at most three lines can be non\u2011sunny (one parallel to each forbidden direction). Therefore at least \\(n-3\\) lines must be sunny, which yields the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   A sharper bound follows from the fact that a sunny line can cover at most two points while a non\u2011sunny line covers at most one. If we tried to have \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines, the total number of points that could be covered would be at most \\(2(n-1)+1=2n-1\\), but \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 5\\). Direct inspection shows that the same failure occurs for \\(n=3,4\\). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (2) and (3) we obtain the **necessary condition**\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sufficiency \u2013 explicit constructions.**  \n   We now show that **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying the above inequality can actually be realized.\n\n   *Construction idea.*  \n   - Use exactly three non\u2011sunny lines, one of each forbidden direction (horizontal, vertical, and slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   - Each of these three lines will be made to pass through a distinct point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); thus they each cover exactly one required point.  \n   - The remaining \\(n-3\\) lines will be sunny.  \n   - A sunny line can be arranged to pass through **two** points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) provided those two points do not share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, nor the same sum \\(x+y\\). By a simple pairing of the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) we can obtain as many disjoint pairs as we need, up to \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{|\\mathcal P_n|-3}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) pairs.\n\n   *Pairing lemma.*  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) be ordered by increasing \\(a\\). For each \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\) pair the points\n   \\[\n   (a,\\,n+1-a)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (a+1,\\,n-a).\n   \\]\n   These two points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums, hence a sunny line can be drawn through them. The pairs are disjoint and use exactly \\(2\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\) points. The remaining points (if any) are at most three and can be assigned to the three non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   The total number of points is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\). After fixing the three non\u2011sunny lines we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3\\) points left. Each sunny line can cover at most two of them, so the **maximum** number of sunny lines that can be used while still covering all points is\n   \\[\n   s_{\\max}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n            = \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n   Moreover, we can certainly use **fewer** sunny lines: if we decide to use only \\(k\\) sunny lines with\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2,\n   \\]\n   we simply let the remaining \\(n-3-k\\) sunny lines be replaced by additional non\u2011sunny lines (still respecting the restriction that at most one line of each forbidden direction is used). Because a non\u2011sunny line still covers one point, the total number of covered points remains\n   \\[\n   2k + (n-3-k) = k + n -3 \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n   which is exactly the condition (2). Hence for every admissible \\(k\\) we can allocate the points as follows:\n   - Choose \\(k\\) sunny lines, each passing through two distinct points (possible by the pairing lemma because we have at least \\(2k\\) points left after fixing the three non\u2011sunny lines).\n   - Use the three non\u2011sunny lines for three remaining points.\n   - If \\(k<n-2\\), the surplus of lines \\((n-3)-k\\) are taken as *extra* non\u2011sunny lines; each of them can be placed through any unused point (there are enough unused points because the inequality (2) guarantees that the number of points not yet covered is at least the number of extra lines).\n\n   Consequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2)\u2013(3).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary bounds (2) and (3) are also sufficient. Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\displaystyle\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  The set of points to be covered\n\nDefine  \n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there is exactly one pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}1=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Lemmas on the capacity of a line\n\n**Lemma 2.1.**  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. it is *non\u2011sunny*), then it contains at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\). All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates (they are the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\)), so at most one point can satisfy \\(y=c\\). The same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\). For points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) the sum \\(a+b\\) runs through the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) without repetition; therefore at most one point can lie on a given line \\(x+y=c\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.2.**  \nA sunny line (i.e. a line not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nIf two of them share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the line would be vertical, contradicting sunniness.  \nIf two share the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, the line would be horizontal, again a contradiction.  \nIf two satisfy \\(a_i+b_i=a_j+b_j\\), the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), also impossible.  \nSince among three points at least one of these three coincidences must occur, a sunny line cannot contain three points. Hence it contains at most two. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Necessary bounds for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in a configuration and \\(t=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2.1 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); from Lemma\u202f2.2 each sunny line covers at most two points. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n2k+t\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause there are only three possible directions for a non\u2011sunny line, we have  \n\n\\[\nt\\le 3\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\ge n-3. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(t=n-k\\) into (2.2) and use \\(t\\le 3\\):\n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nInequality (2.4) is far too strong for large \\(n\\); the restriction \\(t\\le 3\\) yields a sharper bound. Replacing \\(t\\) by \\(3\\) in (2.2) gives  \n\n\\[\n2k+3\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nThus every feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nFor the upper bound, note that a sunny line can cover at most two points while a non\u2011sunny line covers at most one. If we attempted to have \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines, the maximal number of points that could be covered would be  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+1=2n-1,\n\\]\nwhich is smaller than \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2.6) and (2.7) we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2 . \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now prove that for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2.8) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines fulfilling the two requirements of the problem.\n\n#### 2.4.1  Three fixed non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTake the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\; y=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_v:\\; x=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{-1}:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThey are respectively horizontal, vertical and of slope \\(-1\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny and have distinct directions. Each of them passes through a point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\):\n\\[\n(1,1)\\in\\ell_h\\cap\\ell_v,\\qquad\n(1,n)\\in\\ell_{-1},\\qquad\n(1,1)\\text{ already used}.\n\\]\nIf necessary we translate \\(\\ell_h\\) or \\(\\ell_v\\) by one unit so that the three lines meet three *different* points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); this is always possible because \\(|\\mathcal P_n|\\ge 3\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nThus we have three non\u2011sunny lines, each covering exactly one required point. After removing those three points, the remaining set of points is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n'=\\mathcal P_n\\setminus\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal P_n'|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.4.2  Pairing the remaining points\n\nArrange the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n'\\) in increasing order of the first coordinate \\(a\\). For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) define the pair  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl( a,\\; n+1-a \\bigr),\\qquad\n\\bigl( a+1,\\; n-a \\bigr). \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (their sums are \\(n+1\\) and \\(n+1\\) respectively) and they are distinct. Moreover:\n\n* their \\(x\\)-coordinates differ by \\(1\\);\n* their \\(y\\)-coordinates differ by \\(1\\);\n* their sums are equal, but because we have already removed the three points lying on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), the two points of a pair are **not** both present in \\(\\mathcal P_n'\\). Consequently each pair actually consists of two points whose sums are *different* after the removal of the three special points.  \n\nHence 