ll the remaining points with \\(n-2\\) axis\u2011parallel lines.  \n\nIn each case we verify that the \\(n\\) lines are distinct, that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them, and that the prescribed number of sunny lines is attained.\n\nThus the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=0),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=0),\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  No configuration with three sunny lines\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\[\n\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\n\\]\ncovering \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) and that at least three of them are sunny.  \nSelect three sunny lines and denote them by \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).\n\nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to any of the three lines in (2), each sunny line has a well\u2011defined finite slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  By applying a suitable rotation of the plane (which preserves the covering property and the notion of \u201csunny\u201d), we may assume that the three chosen sunny lines are\n\n\\[\nL_{1}\\colon y=m_{1}x,\\qquad\nL_{2}\\colon y=m_{2}x,\\qquad\nL_{3}\\colon y=m_{3}x,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwith distinct slopes  \n\n\\[\nm_{1},m_{2},m_{3}\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nEach sunny line \\(L_{i}\\) contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\(L_{1}\\) contained at most one point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  Remove \\(L_{1}\\) from the family; the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines would then have to cover all \\(|\\mathcal{P}_{n}|\\) points, but each line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) (a line intersecting the integer lattice in the region \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) cannot meet more than \\(n\\) such points, because the projection onto the \\(x\\)-axis yields at most \\(n\\) distinct integer abscissae).  Hence the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n-1\\) lines is at most \\((n-1)n<n(n+1)/2\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), contradicting (1).  \u220e\n\nConsequently each of the three sunny lines contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  Since the slopes are distinct, the three lines are pairwise non\u2011parallel and intersect pairwise in three distinct points.  Denote these intersection points by  \n\n\\[\nP_{12}=L_{1}\\cap L_{2},\\qquad\nP_{13}=L_{1}\\cap L_{3},\\qquad\nP_{23}=L_{2}\\cap L_{3}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nAll three points \\(P_{12},P_{13},P_{23}\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach of the three lines contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  Choose on \\(L_{1}\\) two distinct points \\(A,B\\in\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  The line \\(L_{2}\\) cannot be parallel to \\(L_{1}\\); therefore it meets the segment \\(AB\\) at a unique point, which must be \\(P_{12}\\).  Since \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie in the convex polygon defined by \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\), the whole segment \\(AB\\) lies inside that polygon, and consequently its interior point \\(P_{12}\\) also lies inside.  The same argument applied to the pairs \\((L_{1},L_{3})\\) and \\((L_{2},L_{3})\\) yields that \\(P_{13},P_{23}\\in\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\). \u220e\n\nThus the three intersection points are distinct elements of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\n--------