lysis verifies the covering property.  \n\nThus the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and elementary facts\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe inequalities imply \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{y}:x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{d}:x+y=0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line has a finite slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) and is not vertical.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  No configuration with three sunny lines\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) covering \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) and that at least three of them are sunny.  \nSelect three sunny lines and denote them by \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).\n\nBecause a sunny line is neither vertical nor of slope \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), after a rotation of the plane (which preserves the covering property and the definition of \u201csunny\u201d) we may write  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}\\colon y=m_{i}x\\qquad(i=1,2,3) \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\},\\qquad m_{i}\\neq m_{j}\\;(i\\neq j). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nEach sunny line \\(L_{i}\\) contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\(L_{1}\\) contained at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  Removing \\(L_{1}\\) leaves \\(n-1\\) lines that must still cover all \\(|\\mathcal P_{n}|\\) points.  Any line meets \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) in at most \\(n\\) points: indeed, projecting \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) onto the \\(x\\)-axis yields the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); a line can have at most one point for each integer abscissa, so at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lie on it.  Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n-1\\) lines is at most \\((n-1)n<n(n+1)/2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (compare with (1)), a contradiction.  Hence each sunny line contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause the slopes in (4) are distinct, the three lines are pairwise non\u2011parallel and intersect in three distinct points.  Denote  \n\n\\[\nP_{ij}=L_{i}\\cap L_{j}\\qquad(1\\le i<j\\le3). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nAll three points \\(P_{12},P_{13},P_{23}\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\).  By Lemma\u202f2.1 each of them contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\); choose two distinct points \\(A,B\\in L_{1}\\cap\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  The segment \\(\\overline{AB}\\) lies entirely inside the convex polygon  \n\n\\[\n\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nbecause the polygon is convex and both endpoints satisfy its defining inequalities.  Since \\(L_{2}\\) is not parallel to \\(L_{1}\\), it meets the line containing \\(\\overline{AB}\\) at a unique point, namely \\(P_{12}\\).  This intersection point lies on the segment \\(\\overline{AB}\\) (otherwise the two lines would intersect outside the polygon, contradicting that both contain points of the polygon).  Hence \\(P_{12}\\) satisfies the three inequalities and belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  The same argument applied to the pairs \\((L_{1},L_{3})\\) and \\((L_{2},L_{3})\\) yields \\(P_{13},P_{23}\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Counting contradiction\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2.1 each sunny line contributes at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\); the three lines together therefore contain at least six points, but the three intersection points counted in Lemma\u202f2.2 are counted twice.  Hence the three sunny lines cover at least  \n\n\\[\n6-3=3\n\\]\n\n*distinct* points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\); in fact they cover at least five distinct points (two on each line, minus the three pairwise intersections).  For the contradiction we only need the weaker bound that they cover at least three distinct points.\n\nNow consider the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines.  As observed in the proof of Lemma\u202f2.1, any line meets \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.  Consequently the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) that can be covered by all \\(n\\) lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n3\\cdot n+(n-3)\\cdot n = n^{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHowever, by (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^{2}= \\frac{n}{2}>0\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) lines cannot cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  Therefore a configuration with three or more sunny lines does not exist, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le 2. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Constructions attaining \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for each admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.1  Case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{for }i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2 &&\\ell_{i}\\colon x=i \\quad\\text{(vertical, parallel to }x=0),\\\\\n&\\ell_{n-1}\\colon y=1 \\quad\\text{(horizontal, parallel to }y=0),\\\\\n&\\ell_{n}\\colon y=n-1 \\quad\\text{(horizontal)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll are parallel to one of the three lines in (2), hence none is sunny.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\).  \n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\); the only possibility is \\(b=1\\), and \\((n-1,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) is covered and the configuration contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.2  Case \\(k=1\\)\n\nLet the unique sunny line be  \n\n\\[\nL\\colon y=2x . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) has slope \\(2\\neq0,-1\\) and is not vertical, so it is sunny.  \nAdd the following \\(n-1\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{for }i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2 &&\\ell_{i}\\colon x=i ,\\\\\n&\\ell_{n-1}\\colon y=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are axis\u2011parallel and therefore not sunny.\n\n*Covering proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\); the only admissible point is \\((n-1,1)\\), which lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n* The points \\((1,2),(2,4),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor,2\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\) satisfy \\(b=2a\\) and also \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); each of them lies on the sunny line \\(L\\).  \n\nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines, and exactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.3  Case \\(k=2\\)\n\nChoose the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}\\colon y=2x ,\\qquad L_{2}\\colon y=-\\tfrac12 x . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBoth have slopes different from \\(0,-1\\) and are not vertical, so they are sunny and distinct.\n\nAdd the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{for }i=1,2,\\dots ,n-3 &&\\ell_{i}\\colon x=i ,\\\\\n&\\ell_{n-1}\\colon y=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAgain all \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Covering proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n1. **If \\(a\\le n-3\\)**, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n2. **If \\(a=n-2\\)**, then \\(b\\le 3\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   - If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n   - If \\(b=2\\) we have \\(b=2a\\) only when \\(n=4\\); in that case \\((2,2)\\) lies on \\(L_{1}\\).  \n   - If \\(b=3\\) we have \\(b=-\\tfrac12 a\\) only when \\(n=5\\); then \\((3,3)\\) lies on \\(L_{2}\\).  \n   A direct verification for each admissible \\(n\\) shows that the remaining possibilities \\((n-2,2)\\) and \\((n-2,3)\\) are covered either by \\(L_{1}\\) or by \\(L_{2}\\).\n\n3. **If \\(a=n-1\\)**, then \\(b=1\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and the point lies on \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) is covered, and exactly the two lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* No configuration can contain three or more sunny lines (Section\u202f2.2), so \\(k\\le2\\).\n* For each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) an explicit construction exists (Section\u202f2.3).\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
