"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to prove that every integer \\(k\\) in the range  \n\\[\n0\\le k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]  \nis attainable, but I have proved rigorously that **no** configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can have more than  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\max}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  In other words, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in the interval\n\\[\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,k_{\\max}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe proof uses the pigeon\u2011hole principle together with the observation that a line which is *not* sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain many of the required lattice points, whereas a sunny line can contain at most two of them.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The condition \u201c\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)\u201d yields exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   N=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   lattice points.\n\n2. **Maximum number of points on a line.**  \n   * If a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. it is *not* sunny), then it can contain at most \\(n\\) of the points, because for a fixed \\(x=c\\) we have the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\) (similarly for the other two directions).  \n   * If a line is sunny, it cannot be parallel to any of those three directions; consequently it can contain **at most two** of the points (three collinear points would force the line to have one of the prohibited slopes).\n\n3. **Pigeonhole inequality.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   The total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n\\) lines is therefore at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2k + n\\,t = 2k + n(n-k)=n^{2}+k(2-n).\n   \\]\n\n   Since all \\(N\\) points must be covered, we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}+k(2-n)\\ge N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}2 + k(2-n)\\ge 0,\n   \\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad\n   k\\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\) is an integer,  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le k_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}\\).  \n   The bound is derived solely from the pigeonhole principle together with the maximal capacities of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Number of required points)\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) contributes exactly \\(s-1\\) points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\). Summing gives  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Points on a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Vertical* lines have equation \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\). The admissible points on such a line are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.  \n*Horizontal* lines are analogous.  \n*Lines of slope \\(-1\\)* have equation \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\); the points on them are \\((a,d-a)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\), again at most \\(n\\).  \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Points on a sunny line)\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is *