e \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, we finally have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le k_{\\max}:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\, } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) can contain at most  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\max}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,k_{\\max}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe bound follows directly from the pigeonhole principle together with Lemmas\u202f2 and\u202f3.  \u220e"
