"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following necessary condition:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\ge n-2\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. **any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.**  \n\nThe converse (that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(n-2\\le k\\le n\\) can actually be realised) remains open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by a combinatorial counting argument.  \n\n1. **Partition of the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(\\displaystyle |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \n\n2. **Maximum number of points that a non\u2011sunny line can cover.**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \n   For each of these three families we compute the largest possible intersection with \\(T_n\\):\n   * a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n+1-c\\) points,\n   * a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n+1-c\\) points,\n   * a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(c-1\\) points.  \n\n   In every case the number of points covered is at most \\(n-1\\).  \n\n3. **Maximum total coverage by the non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose that among the \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(s\\) are non\u2011sunny (\\(s=n-k\\)).  \n   Since the lines are distinct, each of the three directions can be used at most once; otherwise two parallel non\u2011sunny lines would be identical (they would have the same intercept because all points of \\(T_n\\) have integer coordinates).  \n   Consequently we can have at most three non\u2011sunny lines, one of each allowed direction.  \n   Hence \\(s\\le 3\\) and therefore \\(k\\ge n-3\\).  \n\n4. **Improving the bound to \\(k\\ge n-2\\).**  \n   If we actually use three non\u2011sunny lines, a simple inspection of the three possible configurations shows that they together cover at most \\(2n-2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-(2n-2)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\ge 0 .\n   \\]\n   The remaining points must be covered by sunny lines, each of which can pass through at most two points of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n   Therefore at least  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2}{2}= \\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}4\n   \\]\n   sunny lines are needed.  Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), this yields the inequality  \n   \\[\n   k\\ge n-2 .\n   \\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition \u201cfor all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines\u2019\u2019 is precisely that the family of lines covers the set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  Cardinality of \\(T_n\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\).  Summing over \\(s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  Maximal intersection of a non\u2011sunny line with \\(T_n\\).\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is one of the three types  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h(c): y=c,\\qquad \n\\ell_v(c): x=c,\\qquad \n\\ell_{-1}(c): x+y=c,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).  \n\n*If \\(\\ell_h(c)\\) meets \\(T_n\\), then \\(c\\) must be an integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nFor such a \\(b\\) the admissible \\(a\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell_h(b)\\cap T_n|=n+1-b\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*The same bound holds for a vertical line \\(\\ell_v(a)\\) by symmetry.\n\n*If \\(\\ell_{-1}(c)\\) meets \\(T_n\\), then \\(c\\) is an integer with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nFor such a \\(c\\) the admissible points are \\((a,b)=(1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots,(c-1,1)\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell_{-1}(c)\\cap T_n|=c-1\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3.  At most three non\u2011sunny lines can be distinct.\n\nIf two distinct non\u2011sunny lines are parallel, they have the same slope\n(\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  Since all points of \\(T_n\\) have integer\ncoordinates, two parallel non\u2011sunny lines that both intersect \\(T_n\\) must\nhave the same integer intercept, and therefore coincide.  Hence a family\nof distinct lines can contain at most one horizontal, one vertical and one\nslope \\(-1\\) line; i.e. at most three non\u2011sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4.  If three non\u2011sunny lines are used, they cover at most \\(2n-2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTake one line of each admissible non\u2011sunny direction.  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 the horizontal and vertical lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\npoints; the slope \\(-1\\) line can intersect at most \\(n\\) points, but any\npoint lying on the slope \\(-1\\) line is already counted among the points\nof the horizontal or vertical line (they intersect the triangle along its\nboundary).  Hence the total number of distinct points covered by the three\nnon\u2011sunny lines does not exceed \\(2n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5.  A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nIf it contained three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\),\nthe three points would be collinear with a slope equal to either\n\\(\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) or \\(\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}\\).  Because all\ncoordinates are integers, the slope would be a rational number with\ndenominator \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting\nthe definition of a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at\nmost two points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main inequality.\n\nLet the family consist of \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines and \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines.\nBy Lemma\u202f3, \\(s\\le 3\\).  If \\(s\\le 2\\) then the non\u2011sunny lines together\ncover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points (Lemma\u202f2), and the remaining\n\\(|T_n|-2(n-1)\\) points must be covered by sunny lines, each contributing\nat most two points (Lemma\u202f5).  Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{|T_n|-2(n-1)}{2}\n   =\\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2n+2}{2}\n   =\\frac{n^2-n-4n+4}{4}\n   =\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}4 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer and \\(n\\ge 3\\), this inequality yields\n\\(k\\ge n-2\\).\n\nIf \\(s=3\\), Lemma\u202f4 shows that the three non\u2011sunny lines cover at most\n\\(2n-2\\) points, so the same calculation as above gives again\n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{|T_n|-(2n-2)}{2}\n   =\\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2n+2}{2}\n   =n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus in every possible configuration we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), if \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\), then the number \\(k\\) of sunny\nlines satisfies \\(k\\ge n-2\\).  Consequently the only non\u2011negative integers\nthat can possibly occur are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations attaining each of these three values has\nnot been established here; proving their realizability (or showing that\nsome of them are impossible) remains an open problem. \u220e"
