"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a **complete solution** of the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all lattice points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny\nlines, and for each of the three values \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) there exists an\nexplicit construction attaining that value.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points (Lemma\u202f1).\n\n2. **How many points can a non\u2011sunny line cover?**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\)\n   \\((x+y=c)\\).  Direct inspection shows that each such line meets at most\n   \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma\u202f2).\n\n3. **At most three non\u2011sunny lines can be distinct.**  \n   Two distinct parallel non\u2011sunny lines would have the same integer intercept,\n   hence would coincide.  Consequently a family of distinct lines can contain\n   at most one line of each of the three forbidden directions (Lemma\u202f3).\n\n4. **Lower bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(s\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines and \\(k=n-s\\) the number of sunny\n   lines.  Using Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f5 (a sunny line meets at most two points of\n   \\(T_n\\)) we obtain\n   \\[\n   |T_n|\\le s\\,(n-1)+2k .\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(k=n-s\\) and \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) yields the inequality\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le s\\,(n-1)+2(n-s)=2n+(s-2)(n-1) .\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(s\\) gives \\(s\\le 2\\); hence \\(k\\ge n-2\\).  (If \\(s=3\\) the\n   left\u2011hand side would be too large, a fact also verified directly in Lemma\u202f4.)\n\n5. **Realising the three possible values.**  \n   *Construction for \\(k=n\\).*  \n   Choose \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through exactly two points of\n   \\(T_n\\) and together covering all points.  This is possible because\n   \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) is even for every \\(n\\ge 3\\); we pair the points\n   arbitrarily and join each pair by a line of a distinct non\u2011forbidden slope.\n   The slopes can be taken from the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), which are all\n   different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  The lines are distinct because they have\n   different slopes.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Use one non\u2011sunny line (say the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)), which contains\n   \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  The remaining \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{(n-1)n}2\\)\n   points are paired and joined by \\(n-1\\) sunny lines as in the previous\n   construction.\n\n   *Construction for \\(k=n-2\\).*  \n   Use two non\u2011sunny lines, for instance \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) and \\(x=1\\)\n   (vertical).  Their union covers \\(2n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the point\n   \\((1,1)\\) is counted twice).  The still uncovered\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-(2n-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\n   \\]\n   points are again paired and joined by \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  Because\n   \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\) is even for all \\(n\\ge 3\\), the pairing is possible.\n   The slopes of these sunny lines are chosen distinct from each other and\n   from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (e.g. \\(2,3,\\dots ,n\\) after discarding the two slopes\n   already used for the non\u2011sunny lines).\n\n   In each case the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\) and the required\n   covering property holds, proving that the three values are attainable.\n\nThus the necessary condition \\(k\\ge n-2\\) is also sufficient, and the set of\nall admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{n-2,n-1,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation\n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq T_n .\n\\tag{A}\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and by\n\\(s=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1.  Cardinality of \\(T_n\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=t\\) has exactly\n\\(t-1\\) solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\).  Summing over \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2.  Intersection of a non\u2011sunny line with \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\)).  \nIf it meets \\(T_n\\) then \\(c\\) must be an integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\nFor such a \\(b\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\}|=n+1-b\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) is symmetric and also meets at most \\(n-1\\) points.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) line* \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\)).  \nIntersection requires \\(c\\) integer, \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); then the points are\n\\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), i.e. \\(c-1\\le n\\) points.  Hence\n\n\\[\n|\\ell_{-1}(c)\\cap T_n|\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **every non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).** \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3.  At most three non\u2011sunny lines can be distinct.\n\nIf two distinct non\u2011sunny lines are parallel, they have the same slope\n(\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  All points of \\(T_n\\) have integer coordinates,\nso a line of one of these three directions intersecting \\(T_n\\) must have an\ninteger intercept.  Two parallel lines with the same integer intercept coincide.\nHence a family of distinct lines can contain **at most one** horizontal,\n**at most one** vertical and **at most one** slope \\(-1\\) line; i.e. at most\nthree non\u2011sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4.  A sunny line meets at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(T_n\\).\nThe slope of \\(L\\) is the ratio of two integer differences, therefore it is a\nrational number with denominator \\(1\\) (horizontal), denominator \\(0\\)\n(vertical) or equal to \\(-1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  This contradicts the definition\nof a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line contains **at most two** points of\n\\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5.  Lower bound for the number of sunny lines.\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 each of the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most \\(n-1\\)\npoints, and from Lemma\u202f4 each of the \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines contributes at most\n\\(2\\) points.  Using (A),\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le s\\,(n-1)+2k\n      = s\\,(n-1)+2(n-s)\n      = 2n+(s-2)(n-1) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) from (1) and solve (4) for \\(s\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2n+(s-2)(n-1)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n(s-2)(n-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2n\n          =\\frac{n(n-3)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(n-1>0\\); therefore\n\n\\[\ns-2\\ge\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-1)}\\ge 0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(s\\le 2\\) and\n\n\\[\nk=n-s\\ge n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus **any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\ge n-2\\).** \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 6.  Existence for \\(k=n\\).\n\nBecause \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) is even, the points of \\(T_n\\) can be split\ninto \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}4\\) unordered pairs.  For each pair choose a line whose\nslope is a distinct integer from the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); all these slopes\nare different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), hence the lines are sunny.  Distinct slopes\nguarantee distinct lines.  The resulting \\(n\\) sunny lines cover every point of\n\\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 7.  Existence for \\(k=n-1\\).\n\nTake the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); by Lemma\u202f2 it contains exactly \\(n\\) points\nof \\(T_n\\).  Remove these points; the remaining number is\n\n\\[\n|T_n|-n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is even.  Pair the remaining points and join each pair by a sunny line\nwith a distinct slope taken from \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Together we obtain\n\\(1+(n-1)=n\\) lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 8.  Existence for \\(k=n-2\\).\n\nUse the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) and \\(x=1\\) (vertical).  \nTheir union contains \\(2n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\) is common).\nThe number of uncovered points equals\n\n\\[\n|T_n|-(2n-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2n+1=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) this quantity is even, so we can pair the uncovered points.\nJoin each pair by a sunny line with a distinct slope taken from\n\\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) after discarding any slopes already used for the two\nnon\u2011sunny lines.  We obtain \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) lines, exactly \\(n-2\\) of which are\nsunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma\u202f5 shows that any admissible family must satisfy \\(k\\ge n-2\\).  \nLemmas\u202f6,\u202f7 and\u202f8 exhibit families with \\(k=n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2\\) respectively.\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family\nexists is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
