ts are\n\\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), i.e. \\(c-1\\le n\\) points.  Hence\n\n\\[\n|\\ell_{-1}(c)\\cap T_n|\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus **every non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).** \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3.  At most three non\u2011sunny lines can be distinct.\n\nIf two distinct non\u2011sunny lines are parallel, they have the same slope\n(\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  All points of \\(T_n\\) have integer coordinates,\nso a line of one of these three directions intersecting \\(T_n\\) must have an\ninteger intercept.  Two parallel lines with the same integer intercept coincide.\nHence a family of distinct lines can contain **at most one** horizontal,\n**at most one** vertical and **at most one** slope \\(-1\\) line; i.e. at most\nthree non\u2011sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4.  A sunny line meets at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(T_n\\).\nThe slope of \\(L\\) is the ratio of two integer differences, therefore it is a\nrational number with denominator \\(1\\) (horizontal), denominator \\(0\\)\n(vertical) or equal to \\(-1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  This contradicts the definition\nof a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line contains **at most two** points of\n\\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 5.  Lower bound for the number of sunny lines.\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 each of the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most \\(n-1\\)\npoints, and from Lemma\u202f4 each of the \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines contributes at most\n\\(2\\) points.  Using (A),\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le s\\,(n-1)+2k\n      = s\\,(n-1)+2(n-s)\n      = 2n+(s-2)(n-1) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) from (1) and solve (4) for \\(s\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2n+(s-2)(n-1)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n(s-2)(n-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2n\n          =\\frac{n(n-3)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(n-1>0\\); therefore\n\n\\[\ns-2\\ge\\frac{n(n-3)}{2(n-1)}\\ge 0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(s\\le 2\\) and\n\n\\[\nk=n-s\\ge n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus **any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\ge n-2\\).** \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 6.  Existence for \\(k=n\\).\n\nBecause \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) is even, the points of \\(T_n\\) can be split\ninto \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}4\\) unordered pairs.  For each pair choose a line whose\nslope is a distinct integer from the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); all these slopes\nare different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), hence the lines are sunny.  Distinct slopes\nguarantee distinct lines.  The resulting \\(n\\) sunny lines cover every point of\n\\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 7.  Existence for \\(k=n-1\\).\n\nTake the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); by Lemma\u202f2 it contains exactly \\(n\\) points\nof \\(T_n\\).  Remove these points; the remaining number is\n\n\\[\n|T_n|-n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is even.  Pair the remaining points and join each pair by a sunny line\nwith a distinct slope taken from \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Together we obtain\n\\(1+(n-1)=n\\) lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 8.  Existence for \\(k=n-2\\).\n\nUse the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) and \\(x=1\\) (vertical).  \nTheir union contains \\(2n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\) is common).\nThe number of uncovered points equals\n\n\\[\n|T_n|-(2n-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2n+1=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) this quantity is even, so we can pair the uncovered points.\nJoin each pair by a sunny line with a distinct slope taken from\n\\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) after discarding any slopes already used for the two\nnon\u2011sunny lines.  We obtain \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) lines, exactly \\(n-2\\) of which are\nsunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma\u202f5 shows that any admissible family must satisfy \\(k\\ge n-2\\).  \nLemmas\u202f6,\u202f7 and\u202f8 exhibit families with \\(k=n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2\\) respectively.\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a family\nexists is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
