g argument in Lemma\u202f1 are correct: \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). No issues.\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Non\u2011sunny lines)\n- The analysis of horizontal, vertical, and slope\u202f\\(-1\\) lines correctly shows each can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\). The reasoning is sound.\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (At most three non\u2011sunny lines)\n- The argument that two distinct non\u2011sunny lines of the same prohibited direction cannot both intersect \\(T_n\\) with different integer intercepts is correct. Hence at most one line of each prohibited direction can appear, giving at most three non\u2011sunny lines. No issue.\n\n### **Lemma\u202f4 (Sunny line meets at most two points of\u202f\\(T_n\\))**\n- **Claim:** \u201cAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(T_n\\). The slope of \\(L\\) is the ratio of two integer differences, therefore it is a rational number with denominator\u202f1 (horizontal), denominator\u202f0 (vertical) or equal to \\(-1\\) (slope\u202f\\(-1\\)).\u201d\n- **Error:** The slope of a line through two integer points is a rational number, but its denominator need not be\u202f1,\u202f0, or give slope\u202f\\(-1\\). For example, the line through \\((1,1),(2,3),(3,5)\\) has slope \\(2\\), which is **sunny**. Thus a sunny line can contain three (or more) points of \\(T_n\\). The proof incorrectly restricts all rational slopes to the prohibited set.\n- **Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the statement of Lemma\u202f4 is false, and the proof provides a faulty argument. This invalidates any later result that depends on Lemma\u202f4.\n\n### Lemma\u202f5 (Lower bound for the number of sunny lines)\n- The inequality \\(|T_n|\\le s\\,(n-1)+2k\\) uses the bound \u201ceach sunny line contributes at most\u202f2 points of\u202f\\(T_n\\)\u201d, which is precisely the false claim of Lemma\u202f4.\n- Because Lemma\u202f4 is false, the derived inequality is not guaranteed. Consequently the conclusion \\(k\\ge n-2\\) is unsupported