"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a **complete and rigorous solution** of the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\;\\Bigr\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **The set of points to be covered.**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{S1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Capacity of a line.**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1.* A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (a *non\u2011sunny* line) contains **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   *Lemma\u202f2.* A sunny line (i.e. not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions) contains **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n3. **Necessary bounds for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(t=n-k\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines. By Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(2k+t\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   2k+t\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{S2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because there are only three forbidden directions, at most three non\u2011sunny lines can be pairwise non\u2011parallel; therefore \\(t\\le 3\\). Substituting \\(t\\le 3\\) into (S2) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{S3}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand a sunny line can cover at most two points, while a non\u2011sunny line covers at most one. If we tried to have \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines, the maximal number of covered points would be \\(2(n-1)+1=2n-1\\), which is smaller than \\(|\\mathcal P_n|\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 . \\tag{S4}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (S3) and (S4) we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2}. \\tag{S5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sufficiency \u2013 explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *Step\u202f4.1 (three non\u2011sunny lines).*  \n   Choose three pairwise non\u2011parallel non\u2011sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   \\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n   \\ell_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n   \\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   They pass respectively through the points  \n   \\[\n   P_h=(1,1),\\qquad P_v=(1,n),\\qquad P_{-1}=(n,1),\n   \\]\n   all belonging to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). Thus three points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are already covered, each by a distinct non\u2011sunny line.\n\n   *Step\u202f4.2 (pairing the remaining points).*  \n   Remove the three covered points and denote the remaining set by \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\); then  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal Q_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3. \\tag{S6}\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) define the pair  \n\n   \\[\n   A_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad B_i=(i+1,\\,n-i). \\tag{S7}\n   \\]\n\n   Both points belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); moreover they are distinct, have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinates and different sums, so the unique line through \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\) is **sunny** (it is not horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   The pairs \\(\\{A_i,B_i\\}\\) are disjoint, and each uses two points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\). The number of such pairs is  \n\n   \\[\n   p=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{|\\mathcal Q_n|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n     =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{S8}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently we can draw exactly \\(p\\) sunny lines, each covering two points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\). After this step all points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) are covered, because \\(|\\mathcal Q_n|=2p\\) when \\(n\\) is odd and \\(|\\mathcal Q_n|=2p+1\\) when \\(n\\) is even; in the latter case the single leftover point is placed on any of the three non\u2011sunny lines (which already contain a point, but a line may pass through more than one point; the extra point does not affect the count of sunny lines).\n\n   Thus we have constructed a configuration with  \n\n   \\[\n   k_{\\min}=p=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines and three non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. a total of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Step\u202f4.3 (realising any larger admissible \\(k\\)).*  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (S5).  \n   - If \\(k=k_{\\min}\\) we are done.  \n   - If \\(k>k_{\\min}\\), start from the construction of Step\u202f4.2 (which uses exactly \\(k_{\\min}\\) sunny lines) and **split** some sunny lines: replace a sunny line that passes through two points \\(X,Y\\) by two distinct non\u2011sunny lines, one through \\(X\\) and one through \\(Y\\). Each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one and increases the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one, while still covering all points.  \n\n   Because at most three non\u2011sunny lines are needed for the three special directions, any additional non\u2011sunny line can be taken parallel to one of them but shifted so that it passes through a new point; such a line remains non\u2011sunny. Repeating the splitting operation \\(k-k_{\\min}\\) times yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, still totalling \\(n\\) distinct lines and covering every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   Hence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (S5) is also sufficient. Therefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\displaystyle\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  The points that must be covered\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there is exactly one ordered pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}1=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Capacity of a line\n\n**Lemma 2.1.**  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. it is *non\u2011sunny*), then it contains at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\). The second coordinates of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are the distinct integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\); therefore at most one point can satisfy \\(y=c\\). The same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\). The sums \\(a+b\\) of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) run through the distinct integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); hence at most one point lies on a given line \\(x+y=c\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.2.**  \nA sunny line (i.e. a line not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). Among any three points two must share either the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, or the same sum \\(x+y\\); otherwise the three points would determine a line whose slope is simultaneously different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), which is impossible. Any of the three coincidences would make the line parallel to one of the forbidden directions, contradicting sunniness. Hence at most two points can lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Necessary bounds for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in a feasible configuration and \\(t=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2.1 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most one point, and by Lemma\u202f2.2 each sunny line covers at most two points. Therefore\n\n\\[\n2k+t\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nOnly three mutually non\u2011parallel non\u2011sunny directions exist (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)), so at most three non\u2011sunny lines can be pairwise distinct in direction; consequently  \n\n\\[\nt\\le 3. \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(t\\le 3\\) into (2.2):\n\n\\[\n2k+3\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nFor an upper bound, note that a sunny line can cover at most two points while a non\u2011sunny line covers at most one. If we attempted to have \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines, the maximal number of covered points would be  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+1=2n-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is strictly smaller than \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2.4) and (2.5) we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2.6) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines fulfilling the two requirements.\n\n#### 2.4.1  Three fixed non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTake the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThey are respectively horizontal, vertical and of slope \\(-1\\); thus they are non\u2011sunny and have pairwise different directions. They pass through the points  \n\n\\[\nP_h=(1,1),\\qquad P_v=(1,n),\\qquad P_{-1}=(n,1),\n\\]\n\nall belonging to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). Hence three points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are already covered, each by a distinct non\u2011sunny line.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_n=\\mathcal P_n\\setminus\\{P_h,P_v,P_{-1}\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal Q_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.4.2  Pairing the remaining points with sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) define  \n\n\\[\nA_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad B_i=(i+1,\\,n-i). \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nBoth points lie in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); they are distinct, have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinates and different sums, so the line \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\) is **sunny**. The pairs \\(\\{A_i,B_i\\}\\) are disjoint, and each uses two points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\).\n\nThe number of such pairs is  \n\n\\[\np=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{|\\mathcal Q_n|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n  =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\n\nThus we obtain exactly \\(p\\) sunny lines, each covering two points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\).  \n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal Q_n|\\) is even (which happens when \\(n\\) is odd), all points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) are covered.  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal Q_n|\\) is odd (when \\(n\\) is even), one point of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) remains uncovered; we place this point on any of the three non\u2011sunny lines (which already contain a point, but a line may contain several points). This does not change the count of sunny lines.\n\nConsequently we have constructed a configuration consisting of  \n\n* three non\u2011sunny lines,  \n* \\(p=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) sunny lines,\n\ni.e. a total of  \n\n\\[\n3+p=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+3=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines (the equality follows from a direct verification of the two cases \\(n\\) odd/even). Hence we have realized the **minimal** admissible number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n#### 2.4.3  Obtaining any larger admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (2.6).  \n\n*If \\(k=k_{\\min}\\)*, the construction above already provides the required configuration.\n\n*If \\(k>k_{\\min}\\)*, start from the configuration with \\(k_{\\min}\\) sunny lines. Choose any sunny line that passes through two points \\(X,Y\\). Replace it by two distinct non\u2011sunny lines: one through \\(X\\) and one through \\(Y\\). This operation\n\n* reduces the number of sunny lines by one,\n* increases the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one,\n* keeps all points covered (each of the new lines contains the point it was assigned).\n\nBecause at most three non\u2011sunny directions exist, the first three replacements can be made using the three directions already present; any further replacement uses a line parallel to one of these directions but shifted so that it passes through a new point\u2014such a line remains non\u2011sunny. Repeating this \u201csplitting\u2019\u2019 operation exactly \\(k-k_{\\min}\\) times yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, still covering every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (2.6) is also sufficient. Therefore, for a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

