ny of those coincidences would make the line parallel to one of the forbidden directions, contradicting sunniness. Hence at most two points can lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Necessary bounds for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in a feasible configuration and let \\(t=n-k\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Each sunny line contributes at most two points* (Lemma\u202f2.2), so the sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\n*Each non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points.*  \nIndeed, by Lemma\u202f2.1 the maximal size among horizontal, vertical and slope \\(-1\\) lines is attained for the line \\(y=1\\) (or \\(x=1\\)), which contains \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). Hence the \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines together can cover at most \\(tn\\) points.\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n2k+tn\\;\\ge\\;|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nOnly three distinct forbidden directions exist, so at most three non\u2011sunny lines can be pairwise non\u2011parallel; consequently  \n\n\\[\nt\\le 3. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(t\\le 3\\) into (3.1):\n\n\\[\n2k+3n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nFor an upper bound, note that a sunny line contributes at most two points while a non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points. If we attempted to have \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines, the total number of points that could be covered would be  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+n = 3n-2.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(3n-2<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) (and a direct check shows the inequality also fails for \\(n=3\\)), such a configuration is impossible. Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3.3) and (3.4) we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2}. \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now prove that each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3.5) can indeed be realised.\n\n#### 4.1  Three fixed non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTake the three pairwise non\u2011parallel non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThey pass respectively through the points  \n\n\\[\nP_h=(1,1),\\qquad P_v=(1,n),\\qquad P_{-1}=(n,1),\n\\]\n\nall belonging to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). Thus three points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are already covered, each by a distinct non\u2011sunny line.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_n=\\mathcal P_n\\setminus\\{P_h,P_v,P_{-1}\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal Q_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n#### 4.2  Pairing the remaining points with sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) define  \n\n\\[\nA_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad B_i=(i+1,\\,n-i). \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nBoth points lie in \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\); they have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinates and different sums, so the line \\(\\ell_i\\) through \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\) is sunny (it is not horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)). The pairs \\(\\{A_i,B_i\\}\\) are disjoint, each using two points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\).\n\nThe number of such pairs is  \n\n\\[\np=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nHence we obtain exactly \\(p\\) sunny lines, each covering two points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\).  \n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal Q_n|\\) is even (which occurs when \\(n\\) is odd), all points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) are covered.  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal Q_n|\\) is odd (when \\(n\\) is even), one point of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) remains; we place this point on any of the three non\u2011sunny lines (which already contain a point, but a line may contain several points). This does not affect the count of sunny lines.\n\nThus we have constructed a configuration consisting of  \n\n* three non\u2011sunny lines,  \n* \\(p=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) sunny lines,\n\ni.e. a total of  \n\n\\[\n3+p = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines (the equality follows from a straightforward case check for odd/even \\(n\\)). Consequently we have realised the **minimal** admissible number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n#### 4.3  Obtaining any larger admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (3.5).  \n\n*If \\(k=k_{\\min}\\)*, the construction above already provides the required family.\n\n*If \\(k>k_{\\min}\\)*, start from the configuration with \\(k_{\\min}\\) sunny lines. Choose a sunny line that passes through two points \\(X,Y\\). Replace this sunny line by two distinct non\u2011sunny lines: one passing through \\(X\\) and one passing through \\(Y\\). This operation\n\n* reduces the number of sunny lines by one,\n* increases the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one,\n* keeps every point covered (each new line contains the point it was assigned).\n\nBecause we already have three non\u2011sunny directions, the first three replacements can be made using those directions; any further replacement can be performed with a line parallel to one of them but shifted so that it passes through the chosen point\u2014such a line remains non\u2011sunny. Repeating the replacement exactly \\(k-k_{\\min}\\) times yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, still covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nHence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (3.5) is also sufficient. Therefore, for a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
