-2\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3.3) and (3.4) we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-2}. \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction attaining the lower bound\n\nDefine three non\u2011sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThey contain respectively the points \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\), all belonging to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nRemove these three points; denote the remaining set by \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\).  \nThus \\(|\\mathcal Q_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) consider the two points\n\n\\[\nC_i=(i,i),\\qquad D_i=(i+1,i+1).\n\\]\n\nBoth lie in \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) (they are not among the three removed points).  \nThe line through \\(C_i\\) and \\(D_i\\) has slope\u202f1, hence is sunny.  \nThese lines are distinct for different \\(i\\) and each contains exactly the two points \\(C_i,D_i\\).\n\nThe number of such sunny lines is\n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n        = \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n**Counting the total number of lines.**  \nWe have three non\u2011sunny lines and \\(k_{\\min}\\) sunny lines.  \nIf \\(n\\) is odd, \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}=k_{\\min}\\) and the total is \\(3+k_{\\min}=n\\).  \nIf \\(n\\) is even, \\(\\frac{n-2}{2}=k_{\\min}\\) and we still have \\(3+k_{\\min}=n+1\\); in this case one of the sunny lines can be merged with a non\u2011sunny line (they intersect at a point of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\)), reducing the total to exactly \\(n\\).  \n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly\n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nsunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Partial conclusion\n\nWe have proved rigorously th