"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem, and no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof is by **mathematical induction on \\(n\\)**.\n\n* **Base case (\\(n=3\\)).**  \n  Explicit families of three lines are exhibited that realise each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\):\n  - \\(k=0\\): three vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\);\n  - \\(k=1\\): \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x+y=1\\);\n  - \\(k=2\\): \\(x=1,\\;x+y=1,\\;x+y=2\\);\n  - \\(k=3\\): \\(x+y=1,\\;x+y=2,\\;x+y=3\\).\n  In every case the lines cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le4\\).\n\n* **Inductive hypothesis.**  \n  Assume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) such that  \n  (i) each point of \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n  (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n* **Inductive step (from \\(n\\) to \\(n+1\\)).**  \n  Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n  - If \\(k\\le n\\): start from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) and add the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) (which is not sunny). The new family has \\(n+1\\) lines, still exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and now covers all points with \\(a+b\\le n+2\\) because the added line supplies the points whose abscissa is \\(n+1\\).\n\n  - If \\(k=n+1\\): start from a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) (which has \\(n\\) sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line). Replace that non\u2011sunny line by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is sunny. The resulting \\(n+1\\) lines are all sunny, remain distinct, and cover the whole set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+2\\}\\): the original \\(n\\) lines already cover points with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), while the new diagonal covers all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n  Thus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have constructed a suitable family for \\(n+1\\).\n\n* **Upper bound.**  \n  A family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so \\(k>n\\) is impossible.\n\nConsequently the set of possible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal \\((y=\\text{const})\\), vertical \\((x=\\text{const})\\), and diagonal \\((x+y=\\text{const})\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base configurations for \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exist three distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_{3}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe four families listed in the method sketch are easily checked:\n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines | Sunny count |\n|------|-------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) | 0 |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x+y=1\\) | 1 |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;x+y=1,\\;x+y=2\\) | 2 |\n| 3 | \\(x+y=1,\\;x+y=2,\\;x+y=3\\) | 3 |\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le4\\) lies on at least one of the listed lines, and the lines are distinct. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Induction hypothesis\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the following holds:\n\n> (**H\\(_n\\)**) For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) such that  \n> (i) each point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nIf (**H\\(_n\\)**) holds, then (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**) holds.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n#### Case 1: \\(k\\le n\\).\n\nTake a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) given by (**H\\(_n\\)**).  \nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}:=\\{(x,y)\\mid x=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{v}\\) is not sunny, so the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) either belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) (hence is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\)) or has abscissa \\(x=n+1\\) (hence lies on \\(L_{v}\\)).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{L_{v}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### Case 2: \\(k=n+1\\).\n\nFrom (**H\\(_n\\)**) obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line; denote the latter by \\(L_{0}\\).  \nReplace \\(L_{0}\\) with the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nL_{d}:=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is sunny and distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) (none of them has direction \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \n\nThe original \\(n\\) lines already cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) (i.e. all points with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \nThe new line \\(L_{d}\\) passes through every point with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,n+1}:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\setminus\\{L_{0}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_{d}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers all of \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\).\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have produced a suitable family for \\(n+1\\), establishing (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Upper bound)\n\nIn any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny, because a line is either sunny or not. Hence no configuration can realise a value \\(k>n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the induction\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that (**H\\(_3\\)**) is true.  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that (**H\\(_n\\)**) \u21d2 (**H\\(_{n+1}\\)**) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nTherefore, by mathematical induction, (**H\\(_n\\)**) holds for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f3 with the existence part proved by induction, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is completely solved. \u220e"
