n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction for any \\(k\\)).\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe collection \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(S_n\\); thus the case \\(k=0\\) is settled.\n\nAssume now \\(1\\le k\\le n\\). Choose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\n1\\le r_1<r_2<\\dots <r_k\\le n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j\\) set  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y=(r_j-1)x-(r_j-1)r_j .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(M_j\\) equals \\(r_j-1\\); because \\(r_j\\ge1\\) we have \\(r_j-1\\neq0\\), and clearly \\(r_j-1\\neq -1\\). Hence each \\(M_j\\) is sunny. Substituting \\(x=r_j\\) into (6) yields \\(y=r_j\\); therefore \\(M_j\\) passes through the point  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(r_j,r_j)\\in S_n,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nsince \\(2r_j\\le n+1\\) (the inequality follows from \\(r_j\\le n\\)).  \n\nNow define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\Bigl\\{M_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le k\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{L_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\; i\\notin\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_k\\}\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(M_j\\) and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\notin\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_k\\}\\) then the vertical line \\(L_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n* If \\(a=r_j\\) for some \\(j\\), then \\(b\\le n+1-a=n+1-r_j\\).  \n  The equation of \\(M_j\\) can be rewritten as  \n\n  \\[\n  y = (r_j-1)(x-r_j)+r_j .\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n  For the fixed column \\(x=r_j\\) the right\u2011hand side equals \\(r_j\\); thus the only point of \\(M_j\\) with first coordina