vial.  \nI was unable to complete a rigorous construction that works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) is a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\), then the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains exactly the points whose first coordinate equals some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); in other words, the projection of \\(S_n\\) onto the \\(x\\)-axis is the whole set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nA sunny line is not vertical, so it intersects each \\(V_i\\) in at most one point. Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate equals a prescribed integer \\(i\\).\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Since there are only \\(n\\) possible first coordinates, by the pigeon\u2011hole principle two distinct sunny lines would have to contain points of \\(S_n\\) with the same first coordinate. This contradicts the observation above that a sunny line can meet a given column in at most one point. Hence the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks on the converse direction\n\nThe lemma shows that any admissible value of \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThe case \\(k=0\\) is easily realized by the family of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\), which covers \\(S_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\n\nTo prove that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) is also attainable, one would need to exhibit, for each such \\(k\\), a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nA natural approach is to start from the vertical family and replace a chosen subset of the vertical lines by sunny lines. The main obstacle is that a sunny line cannot cover the whole column it replaces; therefore the points of that column must be covered by other lines in the family. Designing a systematic replacement scheme that works for an arbitrary number of columns while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) has proved to be delicate, and a fully rigorous construction has not been completed here.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nWe have established the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations achieving every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\) remains an open sub\u2011problem within this answer. Consequently the complete determination of all admissible \\(k\\) is not yet achieved."
