unny lines would have to contain points of \\(S_n\\) with the same first coordinate. This contradicts the observation above that a sunny line can meet a given column in at most one point. Hence the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks on the converse direction\n\nThe lemma shows that any admissible value of \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nThe case \\(k=0\\) is easily realized by the family of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\), which covers \\(S_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\n\nTo prove that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) is also attainable, one would need to exhibit, for each such \\(k\\), a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nA natural approach is to start from the vertical family and replace a chosen subset of the vertical lines by sunny lines. The main obstacle is that a sunny line cannot cover the whole column it replaces; therefore the points of that column must be covered by other lines in the family. Designing a systematic replacement scheme that works for an arbitrary number of columns while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) has proved to be delicate, and a fully rigorous construction has not been completed here.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nWe have established the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations achieving every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\) remains an open sub\u2011problem within this answer. Consequently the complete determination of all admissible \\(k\\) is not yet achieved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
