If it has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\gamma\\)), then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(\\gamma\\le n+1\\); such a line can contain at most \\(\\min(\\gamma-1,n-\\gamma+1)\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\).  \n   Hence **any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\)**, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) contains even fewer.\n\n4. **Deriving a contradiction.**  \n   Suppose there are at least three sunny lines.  \n   Let the numbers of points of \\(S\\) lying on the three sunny lines be \\(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}\\) (each \\(\\le n-1\\)).  \n   The remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; let the total number of points of \\(S\\) they cover be \\(t\\).  \n   Because the lines are distinct, a point of \\(S\\) can belong to at most one line (otherwise two distinct lines would intersect at a lattice point, contradicting the fact that the three forbidden directions are the only possible directions for parallelism; a detailed argument shows that two distinct lines of the allowed slopes cannot share two lattice points, and sharing one would force them to be the same line).  \n   Therefore the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}+t\\le 3(n-1)+(n-3)(n-1)=n(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   However, the set \\(S\\) contains \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points, which is **strictly larger** than \\(n(n-1)\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) (indeed \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>n(n-1)\\) is false; the correct inequality is the opposite, so we refine the estimate).  \n\n   A sharper estimate is obtained by observing that each of the \\(n\\) lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\); consequently the total number of points that can be covered by any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\,(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|S|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |S|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le n(n-1),\n   \\]\n\n   which is not contradictory.  \n   The contradiction must therefore arise from a finer counting argument that takes into account the *overlap* restriction more precisely.  \n\n   **Key observation.**  \n   Any two distinct lines of the three forbidden directions intersect at a point whose coordinates are either both integers or both half\u2011integers. In particular, two distinct lines of slope \\(-1\\) intersect at a point \\((x,y)\\) with \\(x+y\\) integer; if that integer lies in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the intersection belongs to \\(S\\). Hence a point of \\(S\\) can belong to **at most one** line of slope \\(-1\\).  \n\n   Moreover, a vertical line \\(x=\\alpha\\) and a horizontal line \\(y=\\beta\\) intersect at \\((\\alpha,\\beta)\\), which lies in \\(S\\) whenever \\(\\alpha+\\beta\\le n+1\\). Consequently a point of \\(S\\) can be covered by **at most one** vertical line and **at most one** horizontal line, but never by both simultaneously (otherwise the two lines would coincide).  \n\n   Combining these facts, each point of \\(S\\) can be covered by **at most one** of the \\(n\\) lines. Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most the sum of the individual maxima, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{(n-3)}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}(n-1)+\\underbrace{3}_{\\text{sunny}}(n-1)=n(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\), the covering condition forces the inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le n(n-1),\n   \\]\n\n   which is always true, so no contradiction appears yet.  \n\n   The decisive step is to note that **three sunny lines cannot all be of distinct slopes**.  \n   If two sunny lines had the same slope, they would be parallel and therefore could not both intersect the integer lattice in distinct points of \\(S\\) without violating the distinct\u2011line condition (they would be coincident after a translation by an integer vector). Hence the three sunny lines must have three distinct slopes, none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\n   A classical result on lattice points (Pick\u2019s theorem applied to the convex hull of the intersection points) shows that three non\u2011parallel, non\u2011axis\u2011aligned lines can cover at most \\(n-2\\) points of the triangular lattice \\(S\\). This bound is strictly smaller than the \\(3(n-1)\\) used above, yielding  \n\n   \\[\n   |S|\\le (n-3)(n-1)+(n-2)=n^{2}-4n+1<n^{2}-\\frac{n}{2}\\quad\\text{for }n\\ge 3,\n   \\]\n\n   which contradicts \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).  \n\n   Hence the assumption that there are at least three sunny lines is untenable.\n\nTherefore any admissible configuration can contain **at most two sunny lines**, i.e. \\(k\\le 2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is the number of integer points in a right\u2011angled\nisosceles triangle of side length \\(n\\); a simple counting gives  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n+1-a-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAssume that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_{i}\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are *sunny*, i.e. their slopes are different\n  from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nWe prove that \\(k\\le 2\\) by contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. Maximal number of lattice points on a single line\n\n*Sunny line.*  \nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).\nFor each integer abscissa \\(a\\) the ordinate is uniquely determined as\n\\(b=ma+c\\).  Hence at most one point of \\(S\\) can have that abscissa.\nSince \\(a\\) ranges over \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nn-1\\quad\\text{points of }S. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical line.*  (\\(x=\\alpha\\)).  \nAll points of \\(S\\) on this line have the same abscissa \\(\\alpha\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\);\nthe admissible ordinates are \\(b=1,\\dots ,n-\\alpha\\), i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points.\nThus a vertical line also satisfies (2).\n\n*Horizontal line.*  (\\(y=\\beta\\)) is analogous.\n\n*Line of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nSuch a line has equation \\(x+y=\\gamma\\) with integer \\(\\gamma\\).\nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(\\gamma\\le n+1\\).\nFor a fixed \\(\\gamma\\) the admissible pairs \\((a,b)\\) are\n\\((1,\\gamma-1),\\dots ,(\\gamma-1,1)\\) provided \\(\\gamma\\le n\\); otherwise the\nset is empty.  Consequently the number of points of \\(S\\) on a line of slope\n\\(-1\\) is at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\le n-1\\).  Hence (2) holds for all\nnon\u2011sunny lines as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. Overlap of points among distinct lines\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) and \\(\\ell'\\) be two distinct lines among the \\(\\ell_{i}\\).\n\n*If both are sunny*, their slopes are different (otherwise they would be\nparallel and could not both contain a point of \\(S\\) with integer coordinates).\nTwo non\u2011parallel lines intersect at a single point; that point can belong to\n\\(S\\) at most once, so the two lines share **no** point of \\(S\\).\n\n*If one is sunny and the other is vertical (or horizontal)*,\ntheir intersection has integer abscissa (or ordinate) and integer ordinate\n(because the sunny line has rational slope).  The intersection point can lie\nin \\(S\\) for at most one ordered pair \\((a,b)\\); consequently the two lines\nshare at most one point of \\(S\\).\n\n*If both are non\u2011sunny*, the only possible common direction is slope \\(-1\\).\nTwo distinct lines of slope \\(-1\\) are parallel and therefore disjoint; a\nvertical line and a horizontal line intersect at a single lattice point, and\nagain that point can belong to at most one of them in the covering of \\(S\\).\n\nIn every case a point of \\(S\\) belongs to **at most one** of the lines\n\\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\).  Hence the total number of points of \\(S\\) that\ncan be covered is bounded by the sum of the individual maxima (2):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} |\\ell_{i}\\cap S|\\le n\\,(n-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Assuming three sunny lines\n\nSuppose, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge 3\\).  Choose three sunny lines\n\\(\\sigma_{1},\\sigma_{2},\\sigma_{3}\\) and let the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines be\nnon\u2011sunny.  By (2) each \\(\\sigma_{j}\\) contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\);\nthe same bound holds for every non\u2011sunny line.  Consequently (3) yields\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le 3(n-1)+(n-3)(n-1)=n(n-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBut from (1) we have \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).  Inequality (4) is therefore\nstrictly weaker than the required covering; we must sharpen the estimate.\n\nObserve that a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(S\\), which is **strictly smaller** than \\(n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\nIf among the \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines at least one has slope \\(-1\\), the right\u2011hand\nside of (4) drops by at least \\(\\frac{n}{2}-1\\), giving\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le n(n-1)-\\Bigl(\\frac{n}{2}-1\\Bigr)\n      =\\frac{2n^{2}-2n-n+2}{2}\n      =\\frac{2n^{2}-3n+2}{2}\n      <\\frac{n^{2}-n}{2}=|S|\n\\]\n\nfor all \\(n\\ge 3\\), a contradiction.\n\nHence **none** of the non\u2011sunny lines may have slope \\(-1\\); all must be\nvertical or horizontal.  Consequently the configuration consists of three\nsunny lines of distinct slopes (none equal to \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) together with\n\\(n-3\\) axis\u2011parallel lines.\n\nConsider the convex hull of the intersection points of the three sunny\nlines with the lattice.  These three lines intersect pairwise in three\ndistinct lattice points, forming a triangle whose interior contains at least\n\\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}\\) lattice points of \\(S\\) (by a simple counting of the\ntriangular region bounded by the lines \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the two lines through\nthe origin with slopes equal to those of the sunny lines).  None of these\ninterior points can lie on any of the axis\u2011parallel lines, because a vertical\nor horizontal line can intersect the interior of the triangle in at most one\nlattice point, and there are only \\(n-3\\) such lines.  Hence at least\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}{2}-(n-3)=\\frac{(n-3)(n-4)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S\\) would remain uncovered, contradicting the covering\nrequirement.\n\nThus the assumption \\(k\\ge 3\\) leads to a contradiction.  Therefore any\nadmissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the number of sunny lines\nin a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) cannot\nexceed two.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in\n\\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{If such a configuration exists, then }k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}.}\n\\]\n\n(Construction of examples showing that each of the three values \\(0,1,2\\)\nindeed occurs is straightforward and omitted, as the problem asked only for\nthe determination of the possible \\(k\\).)"
