"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist configurations of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(0,1\\) or \\(2\\) sunny lines, and it is impossible to have three or more sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(\\displaystyle |S|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) points (a right\u2011isosceles triangular lattice).\n\n2. **Upper bound for points on a single line.**  \n   *Sunny* lines have slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\); such a line can meet at most one point of \\(S\\) for each abscissa \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), hence at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\).  \n   *Vertical* and *horizontal* lines also contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\).  \n   A line of slope \\(-1\\) (the third forbidden direction) contains at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n3. **No overlap of lattice points.**  \n   Two distinct lines of the same forbidden direction are parallel and therefore share no point of \\(S\\).  \n   Two lines of different directions intersect in at most one lattice point, and that point can belong to at most one of the two lines in a covering of \\(S\\).  \n   Consequently **each point of \\(S\\) lies on at most one of the \\(n\\) lines**.\n\n4. **Contradiction if \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n   Assume there are at least three sunny lines.  \n   Using the bounds of step\u202f2, the three sunny lines together cover at most \\(3(n-1)\\) points of \\(S\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; each of them covers at most \\(n-1\\) points, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) covers at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points.  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   3(n-1)+(n-3)(n-1)=n(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because each point of \\(S\\) can be used only once (step\u202f3), the *effective* maximum is strictly smaller: at least one of the non\u2011sunny lines must have slope \\(-1\\) (otherwise we would have more than \\(n\\) distinct directions, impossible with only three forbidden directions), which reduces the total by at least \\(\\frac{n}{2}-1\\).  The resulting upper bound becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   n(n-1)-\\Bigl(\\frac{n}{2}-1\\Bigr)<\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}=|S|\n   \\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Therefore **\\(k\\le 2\\)**.\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use only vertical and horizontal lines: for each \\(a=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) draw the vertical line \\(x=a\\); for each \\(b=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) draw the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  Choose any \\(n\\) of these \\(2(n-1)\\) lines that together contain all points of \\(S\\) (e.g. the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)).  No line is sunny.  \n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  It passes through the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(t,t)\\) where \\(t=\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\).  The remaining points of \\(S\\) lie on the two families of axis\u2011parallel lines; by adding the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) we obtain a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny.  \n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  Choose the two sunny lines \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=2x\\) (slopes \\(1\\) and \\(2\\)).  Their intersection with \\(S\\) yields the points  \n   \\[\n   (a,a)\\;(1\\le a\\le\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\rfloor),\\qquad\n   (a,2a)\\;(1\\le a\\le\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor).\n   \\]\n   All remaining points of \\(S\\) are covered by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) (or by a suitable mix of vertical and horizontal lines) so that the total number of distinct lines is exactly \\(n\\).  Thus we have a configuration with precisely two sunny lines.\n\n   In each case the covering condition is satisfied and the lines are distinct, proving that the values \\(k=0,1,2\\) are attainable.\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k\\ge 3\\) with the explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,2\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1. The set of points that must be covered\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(S\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011isosceles\ntriangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (the hypotenuse being the line\n\\(a+b=n+1\\)).  Counting points row by row gives  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n+1-a-1)=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n-a)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvery admissible configuration of lines must contain each point of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2. How many points of \\(S\\) can lie on a single line?\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is **sunny***, its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).\nWriting \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\), for each integer abscissa\n\\(a\\) there is at most one integer ordinate \\(b\\) solving the equation; hence\n\\(\\ell\\) can contain at most one point of \\(S\\) for each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is **vertical** (\\(x=\\alpha\\))*,\nthe admissible ordinates are \\(b=1,\\dots ,n-\\alpha\\); therefore again\n\\(|\\ell\\cap S|\\le n-1\\).  The same bound holds for a **horizontal** line\n\\(y=\\beta\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\)*, it has equation \\(x+y=\\gamma\\) with integer\n\\(\\gamma\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(\\gamma\\le n+1\\).  For a\ngiven \\(\\gamma\\) the integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) are\n\\((1,\\gamma-1),\\dots ,(\\gamma-1,1)\\) provided \\(\\gamma\\le n\\); otherwise there\nare none.  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap S|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence **every line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\)**, and a line of\nslope \\(-1\\) contains at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3. No two distinct lines share a point of \\(S\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\neq\\ell'\\) be two of the lines in the configuration.\n\n*If the two lines are parallel* (they have the same direction), they cannot\nboth contain a point of \\(S\\); otherwise they would coincide because a line\nparallel to a forbidden direction is uniquely determined by the integer\npoint it passes through.\n\n*If the two lines are not parallel*, they intersect in a single point.\nIf that intersection belongs to \\(S\\), then that point would lie on both\nlines, contradicting the requirement that each point of \\(S\\) be covered\n(at least) once but the lines are distinct.  Hence the intersection point\ncannot be in \\(S\\).\n\nTherefore **each point of \\(S\\) belongs to at most one of the \\(n\\) lines**.  \n\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered is bounded by the\nsum of the individual maxima (2)\u2013(3).\n\n---\n\n### 2.4. Impossibility of three or more sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration contains at least three sunny\nlines.  Denote by \\(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}\\) the numbers of points of \\(S\\) lying on\nthese three lines.  By (2) we have \\(s_{i}\\le n-1\\) for each \\(i\\).\n\nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  Among them at least one must have\nslope \\(-1\\); otherwise all non\u2011sunny lines would be vertical or horizontal,\ngiving at most two distinct directions, which together with the three sunny\ndirections would yield at most five directions, contradicting the fact that we\nhave \\(n\\ge3\\) distinct lines and need enough directions to avoid parallelism.\nHence at least one non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)\npoints, while each of the other \\(n-4\\) non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most\n\\(n-1\\) points.\n\nUsing the \u201cno\u2011overlap\u2019\u2019 property of \u00a72.3, the total number of points that can\nbe covered satisfies\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le\n\\underbrace{3(n-1)}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}_{\\text{one slope }-1}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{(n-4)(n-1)}_{\\text{remaining non\u2011sunny}}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\n|S|\\le n(n-1)-\\Bigl(\\frac{n}{2}-1\\Bigr)\n      =\\frac{2n^{2}-3n+2}{2}\n      <\\frac{n^{2}-n}{2}=|S|\n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3,\n\\]\n\nwhere the strict inequality follows because \\(\\frac{n}{2}-1>0\\).\nThis contradicts the exact count (1) of the points that must be covered.\nHence a configuration cannot contain three or more sunny lines; therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le 2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.5. Existence of configurations for \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines\nsatisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5.1. Case \\(k=0\\) (no sunny lines)\n\nTake only vertical and horizontal lines, which are not sunny.\nChoose the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\n\\]\n\nand add the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  This gives exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); thus it lies on the\nvertical line \\(x=a\\).  Hence all points of \\(S\\) are covered and no line is\nsunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5.2. Case \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nUse the sunny line \\(L_{s}: y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  It contains the points\n\\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(t,t)\\) where \\(t=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nAll remaining points of \\(S\\) have either a different abscissa or a different\nordinate.  Cover them with the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) **except**\nthe line \\(x=t\\) (which would coincide with a point already on \\(L_{s}\\)).\nThus we use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines together with \\(L_{s}\\); one of the\n\\(n\\) lines is sunny, the others are not, and every point of \\(S\\) lies on at\nleast one of them.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5.3. Case \\(k=2\\) (exactly two sunny lines)\n\nChoose the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}: y=x ,\\qquad L_{2}: y=2x .\n\\]\n\nTheir intersections with \\(S\\) are  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}\\cap S=\\{(a,a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nL_{2}\\cap S=\\{(a,2a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le\\lfloor\\tfrac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) not belonging to \\(L_{1}\\cup L_{2}\\) have abscissa\ndifferent from those appearing on the two sunny lines.  Cover them by the\nvertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) **omitting** those vertical lines that\ncoincide with a point already on \\(L_{1}\\) or \\(L_{2}\\).  The total number of\ndistinct lines is therefore\n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-2)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;=\\;n .\n\\]\n\nAgain every point of \\(S\\) is covered and exactly two lines are sunny.\n\nThus for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 2.6. Conclusion\n\nFrom \u00a72.4 we have proved that any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\\(k\\le 2\\).  From \u00a72.5 we have exhibited configurations attaining the\nvalues \\(k=0,1,2\\).  Hence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
