ds \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Existence for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *We explicitly construct a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all required points.*  \n\n   - Choose \\(n-2-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots , y=n-2-k\\).  \n   - Choose \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots , x=k\\).  \n   - Choose one line of slope \\(-1\\), namely \\(x+y=n\\).  \n   - Finally, add a single sunny line with a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) that passes through the remaining uncovered point \\((n,1)\\).  \n\n   One checks directly that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of these lines, and that exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny (the only sunny line is the last one, and we may replace it by any set of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines while adjusting the horizontal/vertical choices accordingly). By varying the numbers of horizontal and vertical lines we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Coverage by a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nA sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the line have equation \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct points of \\(S\\) on the line, then  \n\n\\[\nb-ma = b'-ma'.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nm = \\frac{b-b'}{a-a'}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b,a',b'\\) are integers, the right\u2011hand side is a rational number with denominator dividing \\(|a-a'|\\). Because \\(m\\) is fixed, the only way for two distinct integer points to satisfy the equation is that the denominator equals \\(1\\) and the numerator equals \\(0\\), i.e. \\(a=a'\\) and \\(b=b'\\), a contradiction. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Maximum points covered by non\u2011sunny lines)\n\n*Horizontal line.* The line \\(y=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) (namely \\((1,c),(2,c),\\dots ,(n+1-c,c)\\)).  \n\n*Vertical line.* The line \\(x=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) (namely \\((c,1),(c,2),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\)).  \n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) line.* The line \\(x+y=c\\) contains at most one point of \\(S\\), because the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces each admissible sum \\(c\\) to appear at most once among the points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nConsequently there are \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;1 each sunny line covers at most one point of \\(S\\); therefore the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points.  \n\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;2 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points, and at most one of them can be of slope \\(-1\\). Hence the total number of points that the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k-1)\\cdot n + 1 = n(n-k-1)+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) must be covered, so\n\n\\[\nk + \\bigl[n(n-k-1)+1\\bigr]\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-nk +1 \\ge \\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n2n^{2}-2nk+2 \\ge n^{2}+n\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nn^{2}-2nk+2-n \\ge 0.\n\\]\n\nTreating this as a linear inequality in \\(k\\),\n\n\\[\n2nk \\le n^{2}+2-n\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk \\le \\frac{n^{2