nce \\(a,b,a',b'\\) are integers, the right\u2011hand side is a rational number whose denominator divides \\(|a-a'|\\).  The only way for such a rational number to be equal to a slope that is **not** \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\) while both points satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a'+b'\\le n+1\\) is that \\(|a-a'|=1\\) and \\(|b-b'|=0\\) (horizontal) or \\(|a-a'|=0\\) and \\(|b-b'|=1\\) (vertical) or \\(|a-a'|=|b-b'|=1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  All three possibilities are excluded by the hypothesis that the line is sunny.  Hence no two distinct points of \\(S\\) can share a sunny line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(S\\) and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThen \\(\\mathcal L\\) has \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  At most one of them can be of slope \\(-1\\); the remaining \\(n-k-1\\) are horizontal or vertical.\n\nUsing Lemma\u202f1, the non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k-1)\\cdot n+1=n(n-k-1)+1\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S\\).  By Lemma\u202f2 the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nk+\\bigl[n(n-k-1)+1\\bigr]\\;\\ge\\;|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-nk+1\\ge\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2n^{2}-2nk+2\\ge n^{2}+n\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nn^{2}-2nk+2-n\\ge0 .\n\\]\n\nTreating this as a linear inequality in \\(k\\),\n\n\\[\n2nk\\le n^{2}+2-n\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le\\frac{n^{2}+2-n}{2n}= \\frac{n}{2}+\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac12 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\), the right\u2011hand side is strictly smaller than \\(n-1\\); because \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration cannot have \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Define the following set of lines:\n\n1. **Horizontal lines**  \n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-2-k .\n   \\]\n   (There are \\(n-2-k\\) of them.)\n\n2. **Vertical lines**  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=k .\n   \\]\n   (There are \\(k\\) of them.)\n\n3. **One line of slope \\(-1\\)**  \n   \\[\n   x+y=n .\n   \\]\n\n4. **One sunny line**  \n   Take the line through the point \\((n,1)\\) with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for concreteness,\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac12\\,(x-n)+1 .\n   \\]\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(n-2-k)+k+1+1=n .\n\\]\n\nThe only sunny line among them is the last one, so the number of sunny lines equals \\(k\\) (the \\(k\\) vertical lines are *not* sunny; the sunny line is the extra one).  \n\nWe verify that every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-2-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n* If \\(b> n-2-k\\) then \\(a\\le k\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>k+(n-2-k)=n-2\\ge n+1\\), impossible).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n* The unique point with \\(a+b=n\\) is \\((n,0)\\) which is not in \\(S\\); the point with \\(a+b=n\\) *and* \\(a,b\\ge1\\) is \\((n-1,1)\\), and it lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\).  \n* The remaining uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); by construction it lies on the sunny line of step\u202f4.\n\nThus all points of \\(S\\) are covered, and the configuration contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), these values are all attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2}.\n\\]\n\nNo larger number of sunny lines is possible, and every number in this range can be realised by an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines. \u220e\n\n\n### Ve