            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(P_{n}\\) consists of \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) lattice points forming a\nright\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering with only vertical lines).\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{a}:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nThen the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}:=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(P_{n}\\), and none of its lines is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in P_{n}\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on\n\\(L_{a}\\).  Each \\(L_{a}\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so by definition\nit is not sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A three\u2011sunny replacement).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{1}&:\\; y = -x + (n+2),\\\\\nS_{2}&:\\; y = x - 1,\\\\\nS_{3}&:\\; y = 2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(S_{1},S_{2},S_{3}\\) are pairwise distinct, none of them is parallel\nto the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (hence they are\nsunny), and the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{3}:=\\{S_{1},S_{2},S_{3},L_{3},L_{4},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(P_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slopes of \\(S_{1},S_{2},S_{3}\\) are \\(-1,\\,1,\\) and \\(0\\), respectively.\nThus none is horizontal (\\(0\\)), vertical (undefined), or parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) (which also has slope \\(-1\\) but a different intercept), so all\nthree are sunny.\n\nNow verify coverage.\n\n* If a point \\((a,b)\\in P_{n}\\) has \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and\n  \\(b = -1 + (n+2) - a\\); hence \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{1}\\).\n\n* If \\(a=2\\) then \\(b\\le n-1\\) and \\(b = a-1\\); thus \\((2,b)\\) lies on\n  \\(S_{2}\\).\n\n* If \\(b=2\\) and \\(a\\ge 3\\) then \\((a,2)\\) lies on the horizontal line\n  \\(S_{3}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\ge 3\\) and \\(b\\ge 3\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n\\);\n  consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_{a}\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(P_{n}\\) falls into one of the four cases, so the union of\nthe lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{3}\\) indeed covers \\(P_{n}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Increasing the number of sunny lines).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(P_{n}\\) and\ncontaining exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines, where \\(s\\le n-3\\).\nThen there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(P_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(s+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(s\\le n-3\\), at least three lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are non\u2011sunny.\nAmong non\u2011sunny lines at least one is vertical; choose such a line,\nsay \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\) (the construction of Lemma\u202f2 already supplies\nvertical lines \\(x=3,\\dots ,x=n\\)).  Remove \\(x=c\\) from \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nConsider the point \\((c,2)\\in P_{n}\\).  In the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) before\nremoval, \\((c,2)\\) was covered only by the vertical line \\(x=c\\) because\nnone of the three sunny lines of Lemma\u202f2 passes through it (their equations\ngive \\(y\\neq2\\) when \\(x=c\\ge3\\)).  Hence after the removal \\((c,2)\\) is\nuncovered.\n\nDraw through \\((c,2)\\) a line of slope \\(2\\); its equation is\n\\(y=2(x-c)+2\\).  This line is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope\n\\(-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  It is distinct from all remaining lines\n(because none of them has slope \\(2\\) and none passes through \\((c,2)\\)).\nAdding this line restores the covering of \\((c,2)\\) and does not affect the\ncoverage of any other point.  Consequently the new family \\(\\mathcal F'\\)\ncovers \\(P_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(s+1\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for all admissible \\(k\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nLemma\u202f1 provides the family \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) of \\(n\\) vertical lines,\nwhich contains no sunny line.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\).*  \nStart from \\(\\mathcal L_{3}\\) of Lemma\u202f2 (which has three sunny lines).  \nDelete the vertical line \\(x=3\\) and keep the three sunny lines; the\nresulting family has exactly two sunny lines and still covers \\(P_{n}\\)\n(because the deleted vertical line covered only points with \\(a=3\\), all of\nwhich are already on \\(S_{1}\\) or \\(S_{2}\\) or \\(S_{3}\\)).  \nDelete additionally the vertical line \\(x=4\\); now exactly one sunny line\nremains (choose to keep only \\(S_{1}\\) and discard \\(S_{2},S_{3}\\) if\nnecessary, which is allowed because the points they covered are already\ncovered by the remaining vertical lines).  Thus configurations with\n\\(k=1\\) and \\(k=2\\) exist.\n\n*Case \\(3\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \nLemma\u202f2 gives a configuration with three sunny lines.  Apply Lemma\u202f3\nrepeatedly \\((k-3)\\) times; each application increases the number of sunny\nlines by one while preserving the covering property and the total of\n\\(n\\) lines.  Since Lemma\u202f3 is applicable as long as the number of sunny\nlines does not ex