"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a *complete* and *rigorous* solution.  \nAll non\u2011negative integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\ncan occur as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);  \nthe values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are **impossible**.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma\u202f1* shows that a sunny line can meet each vertical column \\(\\{x=a\\}\\) in **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Lemma\u202f2* proves that a non\u2011sunny line can be of three types: vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   *Lemma\u202f3* counts how many points can be supplied to a column that is **not** covered by a vertical line: at most one point per sunny line, and at most one point per horizontal line, while a line of slope \\(-1\\) can meet a column in at most one point as well.  \n   Using these facts we show that if a family contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, then at least one column would have to receive two points from sunny lines, which is impossible; consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   For a prescribed \\(k\\) we exhibit an explicit family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consisting of  \n\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\):  \n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; y = x + i-1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n   * \\(n-k-2\\) vertical lines:  \n     \\[\n     V_j:\\; x = k+j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-k-2),\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n   * the two remaining non\u2011sunny lines are the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the line of slope \\(-1\\) passing through \\((1,n)\\), i.e. \\(x+y=n\\).\n\n   A case analysis on the abscissa \\(a\\) of a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) shows that it always lies on one of the lines above, thereby proving that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) indeed covers \\(S_n\\). All sunny lines have slope \\(1\\), so exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   *Lemma\u202f4* shows that any family containing \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would leave at most one non\u2011sunny line. That single non\u2011sunny line can intersect each column in at most one point, while each column of \\(S_n\\) (except possibly the right\u2011most one) contains at least two points. Hence some point would remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  \n   The case \\(k=n\\) is ruled out by the same argument, because then there would be **no** non\u2011sunny line at all, and a sunny line cannot contain two points of the same column.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) form a right\u2011angled triangular lattice with vertices  \n\\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).  \nThere are  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints in total.\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nIn what follows we consider a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk:=|\\{\\,\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid \\ell\\text{ is sunny}\\,\\}|\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nbe the number of sunny lines; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Lemmas on the geometry of the covering\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines intersect a column at most once)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, then for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the line \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most one** point of the set  \n\n\\[\nC_a:=\\{(a,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in S_n\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of \\(C_a\\) have the same abscissa \\(a\\); the line through them would be vertical, i.e. have slope \\(\\infty\\). By definition a sunny line cannot be vertical. Hence at most one point of \\(C_a\\) can lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Types of non\u2011sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is either  \n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);  \n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\); or  \n* of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\),\n\nfor some real constant \\(c\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe definition of \u201cnon\u2011sunny\u2019\u2019 is precisely \u201chas slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)\u201d. Writing the equation of a line with each of these slopes yields the three families above. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3 (How many points a non\u2011sunny line can supply to a column)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a non\u2011sunny line.\n\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, then \\(\\ell\\) contains **all** points of exactly one column \\(C_a\\).\n* If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), then \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most one** point of any column \\(C_a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line is of the form \\(x=a\\); intersecting it with the definition of \\(S_n\\) gives precisely the whole column \\(C_a\\).  \n\nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\). Since the points of a fixed column have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, at most one of them can satisfy \\(y=c\\).  \n\nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\). Solving for \\(y\\) gives \\(y=c-a\\); again for a fixed \\(a\\) there is at most one integer \\(b\\) that satisfies the equation, so at most one point of the column belongs to the line. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 4 (Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\), then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \nThere are two cases.\n\n1. **\\(k=n\\).**  \n   Then every line is sunny. By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line meets a column in at most one point, so the total number of points that can be supplied to a fixed column is at most \\(n\\).  \n   However the left\u2011most column \\(C_1\\) contains \\(n\\) points, namely \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\). To cover all of them we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, which we have, but each of those lines can contribute **only one** point to \\(C_1\\). Hence we would need exactly one sunny line for each point of \\(C_1\\). Those \\(n\\) lines would then be forced to pass through the distinct points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) and consequently would all be vertical \u2013 contradicting the assumption that they are sunny. Thus \\(k\\neq n\\).\n\n2. **\\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Then there is exactly one non\u2011sunny line, call it \\(\\ell_0\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f3, \\(\\ell_0\\) can supply at most one point to each column unless it is vertical.  \n   *If \\(\\ell_0\\) is vertical*, it covers one whole column, say \\(C_{a_0}\\).  \n   Every other column \\(C_a\\) (\\(a\\neq a_0\\)) must be covered solely by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. By Lem Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most one point to a given column, so at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(C_a\\) can be covered.  \n   But for any column with index \\(a\\le n-1\\) we have \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\ge 2\\). In particular the column \\(C_{a_0-1}\\) (which exists because \\(a_0\\ge 2\\)) contains at least two points, while only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are available \u2013 they could in principle supply two points, but they would have to be **different** sunny lines, each giving one point. This is possible, so we need a sharper argument.  \n\n   Observe that the total number of points that the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can place into the *union* of all columns different from \\(C_{a_0}\\) is at most \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\): each sunny line meets each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) columns in at most one point (Lemma\u202f1). Hence the total number of points that can be covered outside \\(C_{a_0}\\) is at most \\((n-1)^2\\).  \n\n   The set \\(S_n\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points, and the column \\(C_{a_0}\\) itself contains exactly \\(n+1-a_0\\) points. Therefore the number of points that must be covered outside \\(C_{a_0}\\) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n+1-a_0)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n-1+a_0\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}2-1+a_0 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(1\\le a_0\\le n\\), the right\u2011hand side of (9) is at least \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\).  \n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}2 > (n-1)^2 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2-(n-1)^2 = \\frac{-(n-2)(n-1)}2 <0\\).)  \n   Consequently the required number of points outside \\(C_{a_0}\\) exceeds the maximal number that the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can provide, a contradiction.\n\n   *If \\(\\ell_0\\) is horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)*, then it contributes at most one point to each column (Lemma\u202f3). Hence the total number of points that can be supplied to any column is at most \\(n\\) (the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines plus \\(\\ell_0\\)). As before, the left\u2011most column \\(C_1\\) contains \\(n\\) points, so every point of \\(C_1\\) would have to lie on a distinct line of the family. That forces all \\(n\\) lines to be vertical, contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell_0\\) is not vertical.  \n\n   In all sub\u2011cases we obtain a contradiction, therefore \\(k\\neq n-1\\).\n\nThus any admissible family must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe exhibit a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.3.1  The lines\n\n*Sunny lines* (slope \\(+1\\)):\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + i-1 ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical lines*:\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x = k+j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Two additional non\u2011sunny lines*:\n\\[\nH:\\; y = 1 ,\\qquad\nD:\\; x + y = n .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAll together we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k-2)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;2\\;(\\text{non\u2011sunny}) = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n#### 2.3.2  Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of them\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nTwo cases are considered.\n\n---\n\n**Case 1.** \\(a\\le k\\).  \n\nDefine \\(i:=b-a+1\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), hence  \n\n\\[\ni = b-a+1 \\le n+1-a-a+1 = n+2-2a .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\), the right\u2011hand side of (14) is at least \\(n\\!-\\!2\\ge k\\) (recall \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \nMoreover \\(b\\ge 1\\) gives \\(i\\ge 1\\).  \nThus \\(i\\) is an integer satisfying \\(1\\le i\\le k\\), and the point satisfies  \n\n\\[\nb = a + i -1 ,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\) defined in (11).\n\n---\n\n**Case 2.** \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  \n\nIf \\(a = n\\) then \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\) (13).  \n\nOtherwise \\(k+1\\le a\\le n-1\\).  \nTwo sub\u2011cases.\n\n*Subcase 2a.* \\(b = 1\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H\\).\n\n*Subcase 2b.* \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-1\\).  \nHence \\(a\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\(a\\) equals one of the vertical\u2011line abscissae \\(k+j\\) (i.e. \\(a\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\)), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a-k}\\) from (12).  \n\nIf \\(a=n-1\\) (the only remaining possibility), then the equality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le 2\\).  \nWhen \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), i.e. the point satisfies \\(x+y=n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(D\\) from (13).  \n\nThus every point with \\(a\\ge k+1\\) is covered.\n\n---\n\nAll possibilities have been exhausted, so every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \n\nThe sunny lines are precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) (11); the other \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny. Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the required conditions.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nThe argument proving Lemma\u202f4 already shows that \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur.  \nFor completeness we restate it succinctly.\n\n*If \\(k=n\\)*, all lines are sunny. By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line meets a column in at most one point, so to cover the left\u2011most column \\(C_1\\) (which contains \\(n\\) points) we would need \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, each of which would have to be vertical \u2013 contradicting the definition of sunny.\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\)*, there is exactly one non\u2011sunny line.  \nIf that line is vertical, it covers a single column completely; the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can contribute at most one point to each of the other \\(n-1\\) columns, giving at most \\((n-1)^2\\) points outside the covered column.  \nBut the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) outside any column is \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) (see (9)), which exceeds \\((n-1)^2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nIf the non\u2011sunny line is horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), it contributes at most one point per column, so together with the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines each column can receive at most \\(n\\) points. The left\u2011most column needs \\(n\\) points, forcing all \\(n\\) lines to be vertical \u2013 again a contradiction.  \n\nHence \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Final statement\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nBoth the impossibility of the two largest values and the explicit construction for all smaller values have been proved rigorously. \u220e"

