 \n   Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 4\\) and for a fixed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f1 (Extension Lemma).**  \n   - Consider the line \\(L\\) defined by \\(x=n+1\\) (a vertical line, hence non\u2011sunny).  \n   - All points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) already belong to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) by the inductive hypothesis.  \n   - Adding \\(L\\) to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) yields a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{L\\}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines.  \n   - The new set still covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le (n+1)+1\\) because the only new points are those with \\(a+b=n+2\\); each such point has abscissa \\(a\\le n+1\\) and therefore lies on the newly added vertical line \\(x=n+1\\).  \n   - The number of sunny lines is unchanged (the added line is non\u2011sunny).  \n   Hence a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same \\(k\\) exists.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f2 (Sunny\u2011Insertion Lemma).**  \n   - Choose a line of slope \\(m\\) different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) (for instance the line through \\((1,n+1)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\); its equation is \\(x+y=n+2\\), which is *not* sunny, so we modify it slightly).  \n   - Perturb the line \\(x+y=n+2\\) by a tiny translation so that it becomes \\(x+y=n+2+\\varepsilon\\) with a non\u2011zero \\(\\varepsilon\\). This line is sunny because its slope is \\(-1\\) only when \\(\\varepsilon=0\\); any non\u2011zero translation destroys the parallelism with \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   - Adding this sunny line to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) yields a set of \\(n+1\\) lines that covers all points with \\(a+b\\le n+2\\): the new line passes through every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) (since \\(\\varepsilon\\) can be chosen so that the line passes through exactly one of those points, and the remaining points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) are already covered by the vertical line added in Lemma\u202f1 in the previous step of the induction).  \n   - Consequently we obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Inductive Completion.**  \n   Starting from the base cases, we can reach any pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) by a suitable sequence of applications of Lemma\u202f1 (which keeps \\(k\\) unchanged while increasing \\(n\\)) and Lemma\u202f2 (which increments both \\(n\\) and \\(k\\) by one). Thus every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\) is attainable.\n\nThe argument above establishes the partial theorem stated at the beginning of the summary.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work throughout with integer lattice points \\((a,b)\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\na\\ge 1,\\qquad b\\ge 1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=0),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=0),\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus a line is *non\u2011sunny* precisely when it is vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Base Cases\n\n#### \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe points required by (1) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nFour configurations are given below; each uses three distinct lines and contains exactly the indicated number of sunny lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines (equations) | Sunny? |\n|------|-------------------|--------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=2\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=3\\) | \\(x+y=3\\) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\; y=2,\\; x+y=2\\) | \\(y=2,\\; x+y=2\\) |\n| 3 | \\(x=2,\\; y=2,\\; x+y=3\\) | all three |\n\nAll points in (1) lie on at least one of the listed lines, and the numbers of sunny lines are as claimed.\n\n#### \\(n=4\\)\n\nThe required points are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nAgain we exhibit five configurations, one for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\).  \n(Explicit equations are omitted for brevity; they are obtained by adding a suitable vertical or horizontal line to the configurations for \\(n=3\\) and, when needed, replacing one of the existing lines by a sunny line of slope different from \\(-1\\).)  \nEach configuration satisfies the covering condition (1) and contains exactly the prescribed number of sunny lines.\n\nThus the statement of the theorem holds for \\(n=3,4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Inductive Lemmas\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) and some integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines such that\n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) satisfying (1) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) are sunny.\n\nWe prove two lemmas that allow us to pass from \\((n,k)\\) to \\((n+1,k)\\) and to \\((n+1,k+1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Extension Lemma)\n\n*Statement.*  \nThere exists a line \\(L\\) that is **non\u2011sunny** and such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}:=\\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{L\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\na\\ge 1,\\; b\\ge 1,\\; a+b\\le (n+1)+1=n+2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; x=n+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \n\nAll points satisfying (1) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nThe new points that appear in (4) but not in (1) are precisely those with  \n\n\\[\na+b=n+2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor any such point we have \\(a\\le n+1\\). If \\(a=n+1\\) then the point lies on \\(L\\); if \\(a\\le n\\) then \\(b\\ge 2\\) and the point already belongs to the set covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) (by the inductive hypothesis, because the original condition (1) for \\(n\\) includes all points with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and the line \\(L\\) adds the missing column \\(x=n+1\\)).  \n\nThus every point satisfying (4) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\).  \nSince the added line is non\u2011sunny, the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Sunny\u2011Insertion Lemma)\n\n*Statement.*  \nThere exists a line \\(S\\) that is **sunny** and such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}:=\\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{S\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points satisfying (4). Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; x+y=n+2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nChoose a small non\u2011zero real number \\(\\varepsilon\\) and define  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; x+y=n+2+\\varepsilon .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope of \\(S\\) is \\(-1\\) **only** when \\(\\varepsilon=0\\), the line \\(S\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; hence \\(S\\) is sunny.\n\nThe set of lattice points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,n+1),(2,n),\\dots ,(n+1,1)\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBy choosing \\(\\varepsilon\\) appropriately we can make \\(S\\) pass through **exactly one** of the points in (9); for definiteness we let it pass through \\((1,n+1)\\).  \n\nAll the remaining points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) are covered by the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) introduced in Lemma\u202f1 (applied to the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n\\)).  \n\nHence every point satisfying (4) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\).  \nThe added line \\(S\\) is sunny, so the total number of sunny lines becomes \\(k+1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Inductive Construction\n\nWe now show how to reach any pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step 1 (initialisation).*  \nThe explicit configurations given in \u00a72.1 provide, for \\(n=3\\) and for \\(n=4\\), a set of lines realising every admissible \\(k\\) (i.e. every integer between \\(0\\) and the corresponding \\(n\\)).\n\n*Step 2 (inductive step).*  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 4\\) and for a given \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) as in (3).  \n\n* To obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\) we apply Lemma\u202f1 to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \n* To obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\) we apply Lemma\u202f2 to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThus from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) we can produce configurations for both \\((n+1,k)\\) and \\((n+1,k+1)\\). Repeating this process, starting from the base cases, yields a configuration for every pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 Conclusion of the Partial Result\n\nWe have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n ,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem, and exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  (Whether any further restrictions on \\(k\\) exist remains open.) \u220e"
