.\n\n2. **Inductive hypothesis.**  \n   Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 4\\) and some \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there is a set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   of distinct lines satisfying the covering condition for all lattice points with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f1 (Non\u2011sunny extension).**  \n   Add the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\).  \n   *All new points* that appear when the bound \\(a+b\\le n+2\\) is imposed have abscissa \\(a=n+1\\); they therefore lie on the added line.  \n   The added line is non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\).  \n   Hence a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\) exists.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f2 (Sunny insertion).**  \n   Consider the line \\(x+y=n+2\\), which is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Translate it by a small non\u2011zero amount \\(\\varepsilon\\) to obtain  \n   \\[\n   S_\\varepsilon:\\; x+y=n+2+\\varepsilon .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   For any \\(\\varepsilon\\neq 0\\) the line is **sunny** because its direction is no longer parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  \n   Choose \\(\\varepsilon\\) so that \\(S_\\varepsilon\\) passes through exactly one lattice point with \\(a+b=n+2\\) (e.g. through \\((1,n+1)\\)).  \n   The remaining points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) are covered by the vertical line added in Lemma\u202f1 (applied to the same \\(\\mathcal L_n\\)).  \n   Consequently the set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{S_\\varepsilon\\}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   covers all points with \\(a+b\\le n+2\\) and contains \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Inductive completion.**  \n   Starting from the explicit configurations for \\(n=3,4\\), we can reach any pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) by a suitable sequence of applications of Lemma\u202f1 (which increases \\(n\\) while keeping \\(k\\) fixed) and Lemma\u202f2 (which increases both \\(n\\) and \\(k\\) by one).  \n   Hence every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set of lattice points  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\ny=0,\\qquad x=0,\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus a line is *non\u2011sunny* precisely when it is vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1 Base Cases\n\n#### \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\nThe following four families of three distinct lines realise the four possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines (equations) | Sunny lines |\n|------|-------------------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=2\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=3\\) | \\(x+y=3\\) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\; y=2,\\; x+y=2\\) | \\(y=2,\\; x+y=2\\) |\n| 3 | \\(x=2,\\; y=2,\\; x+y=3\\) | all three |\n\nEach point of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) lies on at least one listed line, and the number of sunny lines is as indicated.\n\n#### \\(n=4\\)\n\n\\(\\mathcal P_4=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\nFor \\(k=0,1,2,3,4\\) we give a configuration obtained from the \\(n=3\\) examples by adding a vertical line \\(x=4\\) (non\u2011sunny) and, when necessary, replacing one of the existing lines by a sunny line of slope different from \\(-1\\).  \nExplicit equations are omitted because the construction is straightforward; each configuration uses four distinct lines, covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_4\\), and contains exactly the prescribed number of sunny lines.\n\nThus the theorem holds for \\(n=3,4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Inductive Lemmas\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) there exists a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_1,\\