ilon\\) is sunny.\n\nThe lattice points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,n+1),(2,n),\\dots ,(n+1,1)\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nChoose \\(\\varepsilon\\) so that \\(S_\\varepsilon\\) passes through exactly one of these points, say \\((1,n+1)\\).  \nAll the remaining points of (12) have abscissa \\(a=n+1\\); they are covered by the vertical line \\(V\\) introduced in Lemma\u202f1 (which we may add *before* applying Lemma\u202f2, or equivalently we may use the already existing line \\(x=n+1\\) if it appears in the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n\\); if it does not, we first apply Lemma\u202f1 to obtain a configuration containing \\(V\\) and then apply Lemma\u202f2).  \n\nConsequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\).  \nSince the added line is sunny, the total number of sunny lines is \\(k+1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3 Inductive Construction of All Pairs \\((n,k)\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration satisfying the problem statement.\n\n*Base.*  \nThe explicit constructions for \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) given in \u00a72.1 realise every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the claim holds for some \\(n\\ge 4\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* If we wish to obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\), we start from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) and apply Lemma\u202f1.  \n* If we wish to obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\), we start from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\), first apply Lemma\u202f1 to introduce the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) (so that the points with \\(a+b=n+2\\) are all covered except possibly one), and then apply LemLemma\u202f2 to add the sunny line \\(S_\\varepsilon\\). The resulting set has \\(n+1\\) lines and exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus from any admissible pair \\((n,k)\\) we can reach both \\((n+1,k)\\) and \\((n+1,k+1)\\). By repeatedly using these two operations, starting from the base cases, we can reach every pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4 Conclusion\n\nWe have shown that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n ,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition for all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence **all** non\u2011negative integers up to \\(n\\) are attainable as the number of sunny lines. (Whether additional restrictions on \\(k\\) exist remains an open question.) \u220e"
