ines by a sunny line of slope different from \\(-1\\).  \nExplicit equations are omitted because the construction is straightforward; each configuration uses four distinct lines, covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_4\\), and contains exactly the prescribed number of sunny lines.\n\nThus the theorem holds for \\(n=3,4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2 Inductive Lemmas\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) there exists a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nof distinct lines satisfying\n\n* (C1) every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\);\n* (C2) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, where \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nWe prove two lemmas that allow us to pass from \\((n,k)\\) to \\((n+1,k)\\) and to \\((n+1,k+1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Non\u2011sunny extension)\n\n*Statement.*  \nDefine the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=n+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{V\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe new set \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) consists of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) together with the points satisfying  \n\n\\[\na+b=n+2,\\qquad a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIf a point of (9) has abscissa \\(a=n+1\\) it lies on \\(V\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n\\) then \\(b\\ge 2\\) and the point already belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); consequently it is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) because of (C1).  \nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n+1}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\).  \n\nThe added line \\(V\\) is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Sunny insertion)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_\\varepsilon:\\; x+y=n