u2013  \n   *Lemma 2.* A configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  \n   *Proof idea.* If only one line is non\u2011sunny, it must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Suppose it is horizontal; then it can cover at most one point of the set \\(\\{(a,b):a+b=n+1\\}\\). The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines each intersect the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) in a distinct point, giving at most \\(n\\) covered points on that line, but we need to cover all \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),\\dots,(n,1)\\). The horizontal line already uses up one of those points, leaving only \\(n-1\\) points for the sunny lines, which is impossible because each sunny line can meet \\(x+y=n+1\\) in at most one point. The same argument works for a vertical or a \\((1,-1)\\)-parallel line. Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is excluded.\n\n4. **Construction for all admissible \\(k\\)** \u2013  \n   *Lemma 3.* For every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) and (2) there exists an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all required lattice points.  \n   *Construction.*  \n   - Choose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines of the form \\(y = m_i x + c_i\\) with slopes \\(m_i\\) different from \\(0, \\infty, -1\\).  \n   - Use the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines to be the two coordinate axes (if needed) and, when \\(n-k\\ge 2\\), a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   - By a careful choice of the intercepts \\(c_i\\) we can make each sunny line pass through a prescribed set of points on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\); the non\u2011sunny lines then cover the remaining points. A concrete parametrisation is given in the detailed solution.  \n   This shows that every integer listed in (1)\u2013(2) is attainable.\n\nThe combination of Lemma\u202f1, Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f3 yields the complete answer.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe are given \\(n\\ge 3\\) and asked for which non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) are **sunny**, i.e. are not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\nd_1=(1,0),\\qquad d_2=(0,1),\\qquad d_3=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(\\operatorname{dir}(L)\\) the direction vector of a line \\(L\\).  \nA line is non\u2011sunny iff \\(\\operatorname{dir}(L)\\in\\{d_1,d_2,d_3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (lower bound)\n\n\\[\nk\\ge \\max\\{0,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots,(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of exactly \\(n\\) points lying on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line parallel to \\(d_1\\) (horizontal) meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point, namely the point whose \\(y\\)-coordinate equals the line\u2019s constant \\(y\\).  \nSimilarly a line parallel to \\(d_2\\) (vertical) meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point.  \nA line parallel to \\(d_3\\) has equation \\(x-y=c\\); substituting \\(y=n+1-x\\) from (3) gives \\(2x-(n+1)=c\\), which has at most one integer solution for \\(x\\). Hence a line parallel to any forbidden direction meets \\(\\ell\\) in **at most one** point of \\(\\Delta_n\\).\n\nSince the \\(n\\) points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) must all be covered, at most two of them can be covered by non\u2011sunny lines (one by a horizontal line, one by a vertical line; a line parallel to \\(d_3\\) cannot cover any point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) because solving \\(x+y=n+1\\) and \\(x-y=c\\) gives \\(x=(n+1+c)/2\\), which is not an integer for any integer \\(c\\) when \\(n\\) is odd, and when \\(n\\) is even it can cover at most one point, still not more than the previous two). Consequently at least \\(n-2\\) points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) must be covered by distinct sunny lines, and distinct points on \\(\\ell\\) cannot be covered by the same sunny line (a sunny line meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point because any two distinct points determine a line whose direction is \\((1,-1)\\), which is forbidden). Hence at least \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines are required. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (exclusion of \\(k=n-1\\))\n\nThere is **no** configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume, for contradiction, that such a configuration \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) exists. Then exactly one line \\(L_0\\) of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) is non\u2011sunny; without loss of generality suppose \\(\\operatorname{dir}(L_0)=d_1\\) (the other two cases are analogous). Thus \\(L_0\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=y_0\\) with \\(1\\le y_0\\le n\\).\n\nThe line \\(L_0\\) can intersect \\(\\ell\\) (3) in at most one point, namely \\((n+1-y_0,\\,y_0)\\). Hence among the \\(n\\) points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) exactly one is covered by \\(L_0\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) points must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nBut a sunny line, by definition, is not parallel to \\((1,-1)\\); therefore it meets \\(\\ell\\) in **exactly one** point (any line not parallel to \\(\\ell\\) intersects \\(\\ell\\) in a unique point). Consequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\Delta_n\\). Together with the point covered by \\(L_0\\) we obtain at most \\(n\\) covered points, which is exactly the number needed. The only way to achieve equality is that each sunny line meets \\(\\ell\\) in a distinct point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) and that \\(L_0\\) also meets \\(\\ell\\) in a point of \\(\\Delta_n\\).\n\nHowever, the point covered by \\(L_0\\) has \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(y_0\\). Since the sunny lines are all distinct and none of them is horizontal, none of them can have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate as \\(L_0\\). Hence the