t \\((i,\\,n+1-i)\\in\\Delta_n\\).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); solving the equation  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{i}{n}a + (n+1-i)\n\\]\n\nfor \\(i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ni = \\frac{n(b-a)+n(n+1)}{n+a+b}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are positive integers with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the right\u2011hand side is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\). Hence the family (5) satisfies the required covering and contains \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 2: \\(n-k=1\\) (exactly one non\u2011sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\))\n\nThis case is impossible by Lemma\u202f2, so it does not need a construction.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 3: \\(n-k\\ge 2\\)\n\nWrite \\(m=n-k\\) (\\(m\\ge 2\\)).  \nWe shall use the following non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* a horizontal line \\(H:\\; y=1\\);\n* a vertical line \\(V:\\; x=1\\);\n* if \\(m\\ge 3\\) we also use a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), namely \\(D:\\; x-y = 0\\).\n\nThus we have at most three non\u2011sunny lines; the remaining \\(k\\) lines will be sunny.\n\nNow we describe the sunny lines.  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = \\alpha_t x + \\beta_t ,\n\\qquad\\text{where}\\quad\n\\alpha_t = -\\frac{t-1}{t},\\;\\;\n\\beta_t = n+2-t .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector of \\(S_t\\) is \\((1,\\alpha_t)\\); because \\(\\alpha_t\\neq 0,-1\\) for all \\(t\\ge 2\\), each \\(S_t\\) is sunny. Moreover \\(S_t\\) meets the diagonal \\(\\ell\\) at the point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(t,\\;n+1-t\\bigr)\\in\\Delta_n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) already cover all points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) except \\((1,n)\\). The point \\((1,n)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).\n\nIt remains to verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) is covered by one of the lines \\(H,V,D\\) or some \\(S_t\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(V\\).  \n*If \\(b=1\\)*,