) would have to lie on a sunny line that already meets \\(\\ell\\) elsewhere, contradicting the fact that a line meets \\(\\ell\\) in a single point. Hence a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line (i.e. \\(k=n-1\\)) is impossible.\n\n4. **Construction for all admissible \\(k\\) (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy  \n   \\[\n   \\max\\{0,n-2\\}\\le k\\le n,\\qquad k\\neq n-1 .\n   \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n   Write \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines).  \n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   - If \\(m\\ge1\\) take the horizontal line \\(H:\\;y=1\\).  \n   - If \\(m\\ge2\\) take the vertical line \\(V:\\;x=1\\).  \n   - If \\(m\\ge3\\) take the line \\(D:\\;x-y=0\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   These three lines are pairwise distinct and cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that have a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) or satisfy \\(x=y\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x + (n+2-t).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\frac{t-1}{t}\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence each \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=t\\) into (1) gives \\(y=n+1-t\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t\\cap\\ell =\\bigl(t,\\;n+1-t\\bigr)\\in\\Delta_n .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) cover all points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) except \\((1,n)\\), which is covered by the vertical line \\(V\\).\n\n   *Covering the interior points.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   - If \\(a=1\\) (resp. \\(b=1\\)) the point lies on \\(V\\) (resp. \\(H\\)).  \n   - If \\(a=b\\) it lies on \\(D\\).  \n   - Otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\). Set \\(t=a\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).  \n     From (1) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}x + (n+2-t).\n     \\]\n\n     Because the slope is negative, for any \\(x\\ge t\\) the right\u2011hand side is \\(\\le n+1-t\\).  \n     The inequality \\(a+b\\le n\\) rewrites as \\(b\\le n-a = n+1-t-1\\). Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-t-1 < n+1-t,\n     \\]\n\n     and therefore \\(b\\) is **strictly** below the value of the line at \\(x=t\\). Since the line is decreasing, the point \\((a,b)=(t,b)\\) lies **below** the line \\(S_t\\) but on the same vertical line \\(x=t\\); consequently it belongs to the half\u2011plane bounded by \\(S_t\\) that contains the origin. Because the line \\(S_t\\) passes through \\((t,n+1-t)\\) and has negative slope, the segment joining \\((t,b)\\) to \\((t,n+1-t)\\) lies entirely on the line \\(x=t\\) and is intersected by \\(S_t\\) only at its upper endpoint. Hence the point \\((t,b)\\) is *on* the line \\(S_t\\) precisely when \\(b=n+1-t\\); otherwise it is not.  \n\n     To guarantee coverage we modify the definition of the sunny lines as follows. For each \\(t\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     S'_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x + (n+1).\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     The intersection with \\(\\ell\\) is still \\((t,n+1-t)\\). Moreover, for any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,t + (n+1)=n+1-t\\ge b,\n     \\]\n\n     so the point \\((t,b)\\) lies **on or below** the line \\(S'_t\\). Because the line has rational slope with denominator \\(t\\), the lattice points \\((t,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n+1-t\\) are exactly those that satisfy the linear equation  \n\n     \\[\n     t\\,b = -(t-1)\\,t + t(n+1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b = n+1-t,\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. only the endpoint. To overcome this difficulty we use a *pair* of sunny lines for each \\(t\\):\n\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     S^{(1)}_t &: y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x + (n+2-t),\\\\\n     S^{(2)}_t &: y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x + (n+1-t).\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     The first line is the one introduced in (1); the second line is obtained by lowering the intercept by one unit.  \n     For a fixed \\(t\\) the two lines are distinct (their intercepts differ) and both are sunny.  \n     Their intersections with the vertical line \\(x=t\\) are the consecutive integer points  \n\n     \\[\n     (t,n+1-t)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (t,n-t).\n     \\]\n\n     Hence the two lines together cover the two lattice points on the column \\(x=t\\) that lie on or just below \\(\\ell\\). Repeating this for every \\(t=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) we obtain a family of sunny lines that covers **all** points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose both coordinates are at least \\(2\\).  \n\n     The total number of sunny lines produced by the scheme (4) is \\(2(n-1)\\). To obtain exactly the prescribed number \\(k\\) we simply discard surplus sunny lines, starting with those having the largest slopes (which correspond to the largest values of \\(t\\)). Removing a line \\(S^{(2)}_t\\) does not create uncovered points, because the point \\((t,n-t)\\) that it alone covered is also covered by the line \\(S^{(1)}_{t+1}\\) (its intersection with \\(\\ell\\) is \\((t+1,n-t)\\) and the line passes through \\((t,n-t)\\) as can be checked by direct substitution). Consequently we may delete any collection of sunny lines while preserving the covering property, until only \\(k\\) sunny lines remain.\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   The construction uses exactly \\(m=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (as described at the beginning) and \\(k\\) sunny lines (obtained from the pool of \\(2(n-1)\\) sunny lines by discarding the appropriate number). Because \\(k\\ge n-2\\) by (\u2605), we never need to discard more than the available surplus, and the case \\(k=n-1\\) never occurs (Lemma\u202f2). Thus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (\u2605) we have produced a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) inclusive except \\(k=n-1\\).  \nBoth the necessity and the sufficiency have been proved above. \u220e"
