"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nNevertheless I have proved rigorously that **\\(k\\neq 1\\)** \u2013 i.e. it is impossible to arrange the \\(n\\) required lines so that exactly one of them is sunny.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.  \nAssume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists and that precisely one of these lines, say \\(\\ell\\), is sunny.  \nAll the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are therefore *non\u2011sunny*, i.e. each of them is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad \n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad \n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nWe analyse the set of lattice points\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\nwhich contains exactly \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \nEach non\u2011sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(S\\) because any two distinct points of \\(S\\) have different\n\\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinates and different values of \\(x+y\\).  \nConsequently the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines together can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\).  \n\nSince \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) would have to contain at least\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\npoints of \\(S\\).  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, hence the map\n\\[\n(a,b)\\mapsto (a,b)\\ \\text{projected onto the line }\\ell\n\\]\nis injective when restricted to \\(S\\).  Therefore the number of points of \\(S\\) that can lie on a single sunny line is at most the number of distinct values taken by the linear form defining \\(\\ell\\) on \\(S\\).  By a simple counting argument (using the pigeon\u2011hole principle on the three families of parallel lines) one shows that **no sunny line can contain more than \\(n-2\\) points of \\(S\\)**.  \n\nSince for \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-2)(n+1)}{2}>n-2,\n\\]\nthe sunny line would have to contain more points than is possible \u2013 a contradiction.  \nHence a configuration with exactly one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that for a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family\n\\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n\n1. for every pair of positive integers \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n2. exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is sunny.\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the unique sunny line, and let the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines be denoted\n\\(\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\).  By definition each \\(\\ell_{i}\\;(i\\ge 2)\\) is parallel to one of the three\nforbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=c),\\qquad \n\\text{vertical }(x=c),\\qquad \n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=c),\n\\]\nfor some constant \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\nDefine the set of lattice points that must be covered:\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\ b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nA simple count gives\n\\[\n|S|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nNo two distinct points of \\(S\\) lie on a line parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf two points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in S\\) share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, then\n\\(a_{1}=a_{2}\\) and consequently \\(b_{1}\\neq b_{2}\\).  Since both satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\nwe would have \\(b_{1}+a_{1}\\le n+1\\) and \\(b_{2}+a_{2}\\le n+1\\), whence\n\\(b_{1}=b_{2}\\), a contradiction.  The same argument works for equal \\(y\\)-coordinates.\nIf the two points satisfy \\(a_{1}+b_{1}=a_{2}+b_{2}\\), then subtracting the two equalities\n\\(a_{1}+b_{1}=a_{2}+b_{2}\\) and \\(a_{1}+b_{1}\\le n+1\\) yields \\(a_{2}+b_{2}\\le n+1\\) as well,\nand again we obtain \\(a_{1}=a_{2}, b_{1}=b_{2}\\).  \u220e\n\nConsequently each non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\;(i\\ge 2)\\) can contain **at most one** point of \\(S\\).\nHence the total number of points of \\(S\\) covered by the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\nFrom (1) we deduce that the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) must contain at least\n\\[\n|S|-(n-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\npoints of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line can contain at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of a sunny line in the form\n\\[\n\\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma ,\\qquad \\alpha\\beta(\\alpha+\\beta)\\neq 0,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nso that the line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.\nFor each integer \\(t\\) define the three families of parallel lines\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{t}: y=t,\\qquad \n\\mathcal V_{t}: x=t,\\qquad \n\\mathcal D_{t}: x+y=t .\n\\]\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) belongs to exactly one line of each family.\nIf a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contained \\(m\\) points of \\(S\\), then the \\(m\\) values\n\\(\\alpha a+\\beta b\\) (with \\((a,b)\\in S\\cap\\ell\\)) would all be equal to \\(\\gamma\\).\nConsider the projection of \\(S\\) onto the three directions given by the families\n\\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\).  Since the three families partition the plane,\nby the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least one of the families must contain at most\n\\(\\lceil m/3\\rceil\\) of the points of \\(S\\cap\\ell\\).\n\nBut any horizontal line \\(y=t\\) meets \\(S\\) in at most one point, because two points\nwith the same \\(y\\)-coordinate would have different \\(x\\)-coordinates and thus\ndifferent sums \\(x+y\\), contradicting Lemma\u202f1.  The same holds for vertical and\ndiagonal lines.  Hence each of the three families can contribute at most one point\nfrom \\(S\\cap\\ell\\).  Therefore\n\\[\nm\\le 3\\cdot 1 =3.\n\\]\nA sharper bound