y 1.**  \nEach non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\;(i\\ge2)\\) can contain at most one point of \\(S\\).\nIndeed, a line parallel to a horizontal, vertical or diagonal direction would\ncontain two points of \\(S\\) with equal \\(y\\), equal \\(x\\) or equal \\(x+y\\),\nrespectively, contradicting Lemma\u202f1.\n\nHence the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\).\nConsequently the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) must contain at least\n\\[\n|S|-(n-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n+1)}2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\npoints of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line can contain at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of a sunny line in the form\n\\[\n\\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma ,\\qquad \\alpha\\beta(\\alpha+\\beta)\\neq0,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nso that the line is not parallel to any of the three directions in (1).\n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f1, the three numbers \\(x\\), \\(y\\) and \\(x+y\\) are pairwise distinct\nfor distinct points of \\(S\\).  In particular, a sunny line cannot be vertical;\nhence the projection \\((a,b)\\mapsto a\\) is injective on \\(\\ell\\cap S\\).  Therefore the\n\\(x\\)-coordinates of the points of \\(\\ell\\cap S\\) are all different.\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); thus there are exactly \\(n+1-a\\) points of \\(S\\) having that\n\\(x\\)-coordinate.  Since \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical, along \\(\\ell\\) the \\(x\\)-coordinates\nmust increase strictly.  Consequently the set of possible \\(x\\)-coordinates of\n\\(\\ell\\cap S\\) is a subset of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with no repetitions, and its\ncardinality cannot exceed the size of the largest such subset that still allows the\ncorresponding \\(y\\)-coordinates to satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nIf the smallest \\(x\\)-coordinate on \\(\\ell\\cap S\\) were \\(1\\), the largest possible\n\