n\n3. **Using non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (resp. a vertical line \\(x=c\\), resp. a line \\(x+y=c\\))\n   can contain up to \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).  In particular, by taking\n   the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines to be the \\(n-k\\) distinct horizontal lines\n   \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) we can cover at most\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n-k}(n+1-i)= (n-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}\n   =\\frac{(n-k)(2n-k+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n4. **Deriving a necessary lower bound for \\(k\\).**  \n   The remaining uncovered points must be supplied by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most two points, so we need\n   \\[\n   2k\\ge |S_{n}|-\\frac{(n-k)(2n-k+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Rearranging gives the inequality of Lemma\u202f2:\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|S_{n}|-2(n-k)}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sufficiency (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) we construct a covering as follows.\n   * Choose the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  They cover exactly the points with second coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\).\n   * The set of uncovered points lies in the strip \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  Its cardinality is\n     \\[\n     |S_{n}|-\\frac{(n-k)(2n-k+1)}{2}\\le 2k .\n     \\]\n   * Pair the uncovered points arbitrarily (leaving at most one singleton if the number is odd) and for each pair draw the unique line passing through the two points.  Because the two points have distinct \\(a\\)-coordinates and distinct \\(b\\)-coordinates, the resulting line cannot be horizontal, vertical, nor have slope \\(-1\\); hence it is sunny.  Distinct pairs give distinct lines, providing at most \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   * If a singleton remains, we replace one of the previously drawn sunny lines by the line through the singleton and any already\u2011covered point; this line is still sunny because the added point does not lie on a forbidden