|S_{n}|-2(n-k)}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 sufficiency of the bound  \n\n*Statement.*  \nIf an integer \\(k\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|S_{n}|-2(n-k)}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nthen there exists a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which\nare sunny, that covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  They cover precisely\nthe points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\); denote\nthe uncovered set by \\(U\\).  From (3) we have  \n\n\\[\n|U|=|S_{n}|-\\frac{(n-k)(2n-k+1)}{2}\\le 2k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|U|=0\\) we are done (all lines are non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\)).  \nOtherwise pair the points of \\(U\\) arbitrarily; if \\(|U|\\) is odd, leave one\nsingleton aside.  For each pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\subseteq U\\) let \\(L_{PQ}\\) be the\nunique line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\).  Because the two points have different\n\\(x\\)-coordinates and different \\(y\\)-coordinates, \\(L_{PQ}\\) is neither\nvertical nor horizontal.  Moreover, if its slope were \\(-1\\) we would have\n\\(x_{P}+y_{P}=x_{Q}+y_{Q}\\), which cannot happen because both points belong to\n\\(U\\) and satisfy \\(x+y\\ge n-k+2\\) while also being distinct.  Hence each\n\\(L_{PQ}\\) is sunny.\n\nThe number of constructed sunny lines equals \\(\\lfloor|U|/2\\rfloor\\le k\\).\nIf \\(|U|\\) is even we already have at most \\(k\\) sunny lines; we may add\narbitrary distinct sunny lines (for instance, lines through a fixed point of\n\\(U\\) and a new point of \\(S_{n}\\)) until the total number of sunny lines is\nexactly \\(k\\).\n\nIf \\(|U|\\) is odd, after pairing we have \\((|U|-1)/2\\) sunny lines and one\nuncovered singleton \\(P\\).  Choose any already\u2011used sunny line \\(L\\) and any\npoint \\(Q\\) on 