e \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. a *sunny* line) contains at most two points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in S_{n}\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).\nEquality of slopes gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{1}-b_{2}}{a_{1}-a_{2}}=\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{3}}{a_{2}-a_{3}}=\n\\frac{b_{1}-b_{3}}{a_{1}-a_{3}} .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a_{1}=a_{2}\\) the line is vertical; if \\(b_{1}=b_{2}\\) it is horizontal.\nOtherwise we may cross\u2011multiply the first equality:\n\n\\[\n(b_{1}-b_{2})(a_{2}-a_{3})=(b_{2}-b_{3})(a_{1}-a_{2}).\n\\]\n\nA similar manipulation with the second equality yields  \n\n\\[\n(b_{2}-b_{3})(a_{1}-a_{3})=(b_{1}-b_{3})(a_{2}-a_{3}).\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the two relations and simplifying gives  \n\n\\[\n(a_{1}-a_{2})(b_{2}-b_{3})=(a_{2}-a_{3})(b_{1}-b_{2}).\n\\]\n\nSince none of the differences \\(a_{i}-a_{j}\\) and \\(b_{i}-b_{j}\\) is zero,\nwe obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{3}}{a_{2}-a_{3}}=\\frac{b_{1}-b_{2}}{a_{1}-a_{2}}=-1,\n\\]\n\nso the common slope would be \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny\nline.  Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_{n}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{c}: y=c\\qquad(c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct and none of them is sunny.  \n   For a given \\(c\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(b=c\\) forces\n   \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); therefore \\(L_{c}\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of\n   \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n2. **Points still uncovered.**  \n   The points not lying on any \\(L_{c}\\) are those with second coordinate\n   \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  For each such \\(b\\) the admissible values of \\(a\\) are\n   \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-b\\).  Hence the number of uncovered points is