ces (point\u2013line pairs) is at least \\(\\binom{n+1}{2}\\) because every\n   point of \\(S_n\\) must lie on at least one line.  \n   Using the maximal possible incidences per line we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{s\\,(n-1)}_{\\text{sunny lines}}+\n   \\underbrace{t\\,n}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny lines}}\n   \\;\\ge\\;\n   \\binom{n+1}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solving the inequality.**  \n   Substituting \\(t=n-s\\) into (1) gives\n   \\[\n   s\\,(n-1)+(n-s)n \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   n^2 - s \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   s \\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n   Hence the number of non\u2011sunny lines satisfies \\(t\\le 2\\), i.e. at most two lines can be\n   non\u2011sunny. Equivalently,\n   \\[\n   k=s\\ge n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Realising the three possibilities.**  \n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n   \\]\n   All are non\u2011sunny, but we can replace any one of them by a sunny line of slope\n   \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) that passes through the same set of points; thus we obtain a family of\n   \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) together with the diagonal line\n   \\(x+y=n\\).  The diagonal line is sunny, the horizontal ones are non\u2011sunny, giving exactly\n   one sunny line.\n\n   *\\(k=n-2\\).*  \n   Take the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) and the remaining \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n   of distinct slopes, each passing through a distinct point of the set\n   \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\).  These \\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) and contain exactly\n   \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n   In each case the lines are distinct and the covering condition is satisfied, showing that\n   the three values \\(k=n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2\\) are attainable.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (2) shows that no configuration can have fewer than \\(n-