nny} & n\\\\\n\\text{sunny} & n-1\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A combinatorial inequality\n\nSuppose we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of\n\\(S_n\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\ns:=|\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}|,\\qquad\nt:=n-s\n\\]\n\nbe the numbers of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines, respectively.  \nCounting incidences (ordered pairs \\((P,L)\\) with \\(P\\in S_n\\) and \\(P\\in L\\)) in two ways\ngives\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{s\\,(n-1)}_{\\text{contribution of sunny lines}}+\n\\underbrace{t\\,n}_{\\text{contribution of non\u2011sunny lines}}\n\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|\n\\stackrel{(3)}{=}\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t=n-s\\) into (7) yields\n\n\\[\ns\\,(n-1)+(n-s)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nn^{2}-s\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\Longleftrightarrow\ns\\le n-2.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the number of **non\u2011sunny** lines satisfies \\(t=n-s\\le 2\\); equivalently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=s\\ge n-2}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Realising the three admissible values\n\nWe now exhibit explicit configurations attaining each of the three values\n\\(k=n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (i)  \\(k=n\\)  (all lines sunny)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(-1\\), which is **not** allowed for a sunny line; therefore we\nmodify the construction.  Choose \\(n\\) distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and\nfor each \\(c\\) draw the line of that slope passing through the point \\((1,c-1)\\).\nBecause the points \\((1,c-1)\\) for \\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) are exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) on\nthe leftmost column, every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines.\nAll \\(n\\) lines 