   Let \\(k\\) satisfy  \n   \\[\n   n-2\\le k\\le n,\\qquad k\\neq n-1 .\n   \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n   Put \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines).  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   - If \\(m\\ge 1\\) take the horizontal line \\(H:\\;y=1\\).  \n   - If \\(m\\ge 2\\) take the vertical line \\(V:\\;x=1\\).  \n   - If \\(m=3\\) (the only possible case, because \\(m\\le 2\\) when \\(k\\ge n-2\\)) also take the line \\(D:\\;x-y=0\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   These lines are distinct, non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that has a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) or satisfies \\(x=y\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x + (n+2-t). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\frac{t-1}{t}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=t\\) gives \\(y=n+1-t\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t\\cap\\ell = (t,\\,n+1-t)\\in\\Delta_n . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) cover all points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) except \\((1,n)\\), which is covered by the vertical line \\(V\\) (present when \\(m\\ge 2\\); if \\(m=1\\) we replace \\(V\\) by the horizontal line \\(H\\) covering \\((1,n)\\) because \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(y=n\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  \n\n   *Covering the interior points.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n\n   - If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(V\\) (or on \\(H\\) when \\(V\\) is absent).  \n   - If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(H\\).  \n   - If \\(a=b\\) the point lies on \\(D\\) (present only when \\(m=3\\)).  \n   - Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\). Set \\(t=a\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).  \n     From (1) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}x + (n+2-t).\n     \\]\n\n     Evaluating at \\(x=t\\) gives the point \\((t,n+1-t)\\) on \\(\\ell\\).  \n     Because the