ave exhibited a concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\Delta_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\Delta_n\\) consists of the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\n\\]\n\nlying on the line \\(\\ell:x+y=n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (necessary number of sunny lines)\n\nA line that is **not** sunny is either horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nEach such line meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point of \\(\\Delta_n\\).  \nSince a sunny line also meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point, at most two points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) can be covered by non\u2011sunny lines.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines are required:\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-2\\qquad (n\\ge3). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (the case \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) (hence \\(k=n-1\\)).  \n\\(L\\) can cover at most one point of \\(\\Delta_n\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can each cover at most one point of \\(\\Delta_n\\).  \nThus every point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) must lie on a different line.  \n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) is not on \\(\\ell\\). It must belong to some line of the configuration.  \nIf it belongs to the unique non\u2011sunny line, then that line would be either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); each of these possibilities forces the line to be parallel to a forbidden direction, contradicting the requirement that the line be distinct from the sunny ones