on a different line.  \n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) is not on \\(\\ell\\). It must belong to some line of the configuration.  \nIf it belongs to the unique non\u2011sunny line, then that line would be either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\); each of these possibilities forces the line to be parallel to a forbidden direction, contradicting the requirement that the line be distinct from the sunny ones (which are all distinct).  \nIf \\((1,1)\\) belongs to a sunny line, that sunny line already meets \\(\\ell\\) at some point \\((t,n+1-t)\\) with \\(t\\ge2\\); a line cannot contain two distinct points of the same vertical line \\(x=1\\), so \\((1,1)\\) cannot be on a sunny line that also meets \\(\\ell\\) elsewhere.  \nHence no configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line exists, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (construction for all admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (3) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\).  \nSet \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines). Because of (3) we have \\(m\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n**Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nm & \\text{non\u2011sunny lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 & \\text{none}\\\\\n1 & H:\\;y=1\\\\\n2 & H:\\;y=1,\\; V:\\;x=1\\\\\n3 & H:\\;y=1,\\; V:\\;x=1,\\; D:\\;x-y=0\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and together cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that has a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) or satisfies \\(x=y\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x + (n+2-t). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\frac{t-1}{t}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus each \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \nEvaluating (4) at \\(x=t\\) gives \\(y=n+1-t\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nS_t\\cap\\ell = (t,\\,n+1-t)\\in\\Delta_n . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) cover all points of