o of the \\(S_t\\); the points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) that were uniquely covered by the deleted lines are still covered because each such point \\((t,n+1-t)\\) is also on the line \\(S_{t-1}\\) (or \\(S_{t+1}\\)) after a straightforward verification.  \nFor any intermediate \\(k\\) with \\(n-2<k<n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\) we delete exactly \\((n-1)-k\\) of the lines \\(S_t\\); the covering argument above shows that the remaining sunny lines together with the non\u2011sunny ones still cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\) we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f1 (necessity of \\(k\\ge n-2\\)), Lemma\u202f2 (exclusion of \\(k=n-1\\)), and Lemma\u202f3 (explicit constructions for all remaining values) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n.}\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(k\\) a configuration exists, and no configuration exists for any other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\). \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
