.  \n   That line covers at most one point of \\(\\Delta_n\\); the other \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can each cover at most one point of \\(\\Delta_n\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) would have to lie on a different line.  \n   Consider the point \\((1,1)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) (it is not on \\(\\ell\\)).  \n   If \\((1,1)\\) lies on the unique non\u2011sunny line, that line would be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   In each case the line also contains a point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) (because it meets \\(\\ell\\)), and the two points would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the assumption that the line is sunny (the remaining lines must be sunny).  \n   If \\((1,1)\\) lies on a sunny line, that sunny line already meets \\(\\ell\\) at some \\((t,n+1-t)\\) with \\(t\\ge 2\\); the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((t,n+1-t)\\) has slope \\(-1\\), which would make the line non\u2011sunny.  \n   Both possibilities are impossible, therefore a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line does not exist; i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) is excluded.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3 (Explicit constructions for the admissible values).**  \n   Define three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=1,\\qquad V:\\;x=1,\\qquad D:\\;x-y=0\\;( \\text{slope }1).\n   \\]\n\n   All three are respectively horizontal, vertical and parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define a sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x + (n+2-t). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\frac{t-1}{t}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=t\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t\\cap\\ell = (t,\\,n+1-t)\\in\\Delta_n . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the family \\(\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) (size \\(n-1\\)) covers every point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) except \\((1,n)\\).\n\n   *Covering \\((1,n)\\).*  \n   - If the vertical line \\(V\\) is present, \\((1,n)\\in V\\).  \n   - If \\(V\\) is absent we may replace it by the sunny line \\(y=-x+(n+1)\\), which passes through \\((1,n)\\) and is not parallel to any forbidden direction.\n\n   *Covering the interior points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).*  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) set \\(t=a\\).  \n   The column \\(x=t\\) contains the points \\((t,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\).  \n   The line \\(S_t\\) passes through the top point \\((t,n+1-t)\\) of this column.  \n   Because the slope of \\(S_t\\) is negative, all points of the same column lying strictly below the top point lie **below** the line \\(S_t\\).  \n   The next line \\(S_{t+1}\\) (when \\(t<n\\)) meets the same column at \\((t,n-t)\\), i.e. exactly one unit below the top point.  \n   Continuing this argument shows that every point of the column belongs to some line \\(S_{t'}\\) with \\(t'\\ge t\\).  \n   Hence every interior lattice point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by the sunny family \\(\\{S_t\\}\\).\n\n   With these ingredients we obtain configurations for the three admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n   *Case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) and add any additional sunny line not coinciding with them, e.g. \\(y=-x+(n+1)\\).  \n   By the discussion above all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered.\n\n   *Case \\(k=n-2\\) (exactly two non\u2011sunny lines).*  \n   Choose \\(H\\) and \\(V\\) as the two non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Keep the sunny lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid 3\\le t\\le n\\}\\) (size \\(n-2\\)).  \n   The point \\((1,n)\\) is covered by \\(V\\); the point \\((2,n-1)\\) of \\(\\Delta_n\\) is covered by \\(S_3\\) (since \\(S_3\\) meets the column \\(x=2\\) at \\(y=n-1\\)).  \n   All other points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) are covered by the remaining sunny lines as in (3).  \n   Interior points are covered exactly as in the general argument, because the deleted line \\(S_2\\) only affected the column \\(x=2\\) at its top point, which is now covered by \\(S_3\\).  \n   Thus we have a configuration with exactly two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n   *Case \\(k=n-3\\) (exactly three non\u2011sunny lines).*  \n   Use all three non\u2011sunny lines \\(H,V,D\\).  \n   Keep the sunny lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid 4\\le t\\le n\\}\\) (size \\(n-3\\)).  \n   The points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) of \\(\\Delta_n\\) are covered by \\(V\\); the point \\((3,n-2)\\) is covered by the diagonal line \\(D\\) when \\(3=n-2\\) (i.e. \\(n=5\\)); for all other \\(n\\) the point \\((3,n-2)\\) lies on \\(S_4\\).  \n   The remaining points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(S_t\\) with \\(t\\ge 4\\) as before.  \n   Interior points with \\(x\\le 3\\) are now covered by the non\u2011sunny lines \\(H,V,D\\); points with \\(x\\ge 4\\) are covered by the sunny family.  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly three non\u2011sunny lines exists, i.e. \\(k=n-3\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 forces \\(k\\ge n-3\\); Lemma\u202f2 excludes \\(k=n-1\\); Lemma\u202f3 provides explicit constructions for \\(k=n,\\;n-2,\\;n-3\\).  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{n,\\;n-2,\\;n-3\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=n,\\;n-2,\\;n-3.}\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n\\ell:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\qquad \n\\Delta_n=\\ell\\cap\\mathcal P_n=\\{(t,n+1-t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines, then \\(m\\le 3\\) and  \n\n\\[\nk=n-m\\ge n-3 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is of one of the three forms \\(y=c\\), \\(x=c\\) or \\(x+y=c\\).  \nEach such line meets \\(\\ell\\) in exactly one point of \\(\\Delta_n\\).  \nHence the \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines together can cover at most \\(m\\) points of \\(\\Delta_n\\).  \nEvery remaining point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) must be covered by a sunny line; a sunny line also meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point, so at least \\(n-m\\) sunny lines are needed.  \nSince there are only three possible non\u2011sunny directions, \\(m\\le 3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line (\\(m=1\\), i.e. \\(k=n-1\\)) does **not** exist.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume such a configuration exists.  \nThe unique non\u2011sunny line meets \\(\\ell\\) at some \\((t_0,n+1-t_0)\\in\\Delta_n\\) and therefore covers at most that one point of \\(\\Delta_n\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can each cover at most one point of \\(\\Delta_n\\); consequently every point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) must lie on a different line of the configuration.\n\nConsider the point \\((1,1)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) (it is not on \\(\\ell\\)).  \nIf \\((1,1)\\) lies on the unique non\u2011sunny line, that line is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nIn each case the line also contains a point of \\(\\Delta_n\\); the two points then determine a line of slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the fact that the remaining lines are required to be sunny (they cannot have slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nIf \\((1,1)\\) lies on a sunny line, that sunny line already meets \\(\\ell\\) at some \\((t,n+1-t)\\) with \\(t\\ge 2\\).  \nThe line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((t,n+1-t)\\) has slope \\(-1\\), which would make the line non\u2011sunny, again a contradiction.  \n\nThus no configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line can exist, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction)\n\nDefine three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1,\\qquad V:\\;x=1,\\qquad D:\\;x-y=0 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define a sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = -\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x + (n+2-t). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of \\(S_t\\).*  \nThe slope \\(-\\frac{t-1}{t}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \nSubstituting \\(x=t\\) in (4) gives  \n\n\\[\nS_t\\cap\\ell = (t,\\,n+1-t)\\in\\Delta_n . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) except \\((1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1 Covering the interior points (\\(a+b\\le n\\))\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).  \n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).  \n\n*If \\(a=b\\)*, the point lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\).  \n\n*Otherwise* \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a\\neq b\\).  \nSet \\(t=a\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).  \nThe column \\(x=t\\) contains the points \\((t,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\).  \nThe line \\(S_t\\) passes through the top point \\((t,n+1-t)\\) of this column.  \nBecause the slope of \\(S_t\\) is negative, the points of the same column with smaller \\(y\\) lie below the line.  \nThe next line \\(S_{t+1}\\) (when \\(t<n\\)) meets the column at \\((t,n-t)\\), i.e. exactly one unit below the top point.  \nProceeding inductively, the point \\((t,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(S_{t'}\\) where \\(t'\\) is the smallest integer \\(\\ge t\\) such that \\(n+1-t' \\ge b\\).  \nHence every interior point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by some line of \\(\\mathcal S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2 Realising the three admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\mathcal S\\) and add any additional sunny line not coinciding with them, for instance  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y=-x+(n+1).\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered by the argument of \u00a73.1, and the extra line does not affect the covering.\n\n*Case \\(k=n-2\\) (exactly two non\u2011sunny lines).*  \nChoose \\(H\\) and \\(V\\) as the two non\u2011sunny lines.  \nKeep the sunny lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid 3\\le t\\le n\\}\\) (size \\(n-2\\)).  \n\n- The point \\((1,n)\\) belongs to \\(V\\).  \n- The point \\((2,n-1)\\) of \\(\\Delta_n\\) is covered by \\(S_3\\) because, by (5) with \\(t=3\\), \\(S_3\\) meets the column \\(x=2\\) at \\(y=n-1\\).  \n- All other points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) are covered by the remaining sunny lines \\(S_t\\) with \\(t\\ge 3\\).  \n- Interior points are covered exactly as in \u00a73.1; the only column whose top point lost a line is \\(x=2\\), but that top point is now covered by \\(S_3\\).  \n\nThus we have a configuration with exactly two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=n-3\\) (exactly three non\u2011sunny lines).*  \nUse all three non\u2011sunny lines \\(H,V,D\\).  \nKeep the sunny lines \\(\\{S_t\\mid 4\\le t\\le n\\}\\) (size \\(n-3\\)).  \n\n- The points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) of \\(\\Delta_n\\) lie on \\(V\\).  \n- The point \\((3,n-2)\\) of \\(\\Delta_n\\) is covered either by \\(D\\) when \\(3=n-2\\) (i.e. \\(n=5\\)) or by \\(S_4\\) (by (5) with \\(t=4\\)).  \n- All remaining points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(S_t\\) with \\(t\\ge 4\\).  \n- Points with \\(x\\le 3\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\) are covered by the non\u2011sunny lines \\(H,V,D\\); points with \\(x\\ge 4\\) are covered by the sunny family as in \u00a73.1.  \n\nHence a configuration with exactly three non\u2011sunny lines exists, i.e. \\(k=n-3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(k\\ge n-3\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 eliminates the possibility \\(k=n-1\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 exhibits explicit configurations for \\(k=n,\\;n-2,\\;n-3\\).  \nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,n,\\;n-2,\\;n-3\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
