e 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Lemma 2 (Sunny\u2011Replacement Lemma).*  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at most \\(t\\) sunny lines. If \\(t<n\\) then there exists a line \\(\\ell\\) in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) parallel to one of the forbidden directions such that replacing \\(\\ell\\) by a suitably chosen sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) yields a new family \\(\\mathcal{F}'\\) that still satisfies the covering condition and now contains \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nUsing Lemma\u202f1 we obtain a configuration with \\(k=0\\). Applying Lemma\u202f2 repeatedly we obtain configurations with any admissible \\(k\\) (except possibly \\(k=1\\), because the first replacement may be forced to create two sunny lines simultaneously; this obstruction is the only gap in the argument).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering Lemma)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three forbidden directions:\n\n* horizontal: slope \\(0\\);\n* vertical: slope \\(\\infty\\);\n* diagonal \\(x+y=0\\): slope \\(-1\\).\n\nWe shall use only lines of these three directions.\n\n1. **Horizontal lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-2\\) take the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H_b:\\; y=b .\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(n-2\\) such lines.\n\n2. **Vertical line.**  \n   Take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V:\\; x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Diagonal 