\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n\\).  \n\n*If \\(b\\le n-2\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n\n*If \\(b=n-1\\)*, then \\(a\\le n+1-b =2\\). Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and the point must satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the only possibility is \\(a=1\\); thus \\((a,b)=(1,n-1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).  \n\n*If \\(b=n\\)*, then \\(a\\le n+1-b =1\\); the only admissible point would be \\((1,n)\\), but this violates \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence no point of \\(T_n\\) has \\(b=n\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny\u2011replacement)\n\n**Statement.**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition for \\(T_n\\). Suppose \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines with \\(t<n\\). Then there exists a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\) that is *not* sunny and a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\nstill satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Proof.**  \nBecause \\(t<n\\), at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is not sunny; choose any such line and denote it by \\(\\ell\\). By definition \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Write \\(\\ell\\) in the form  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; \\alpha x+\\beta y = \\gamma ,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((\\alpha,\\beta)\\) is one of \\((1,0)\\) (vertical), \\((0,1)\\) (horizontal) or \\((1,1)\\) (direction of \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) participates in the covering, there exists at least one point of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\); pick one and call it \\((a_0,b_0)\\).\n\nChoose a rational slope \\(m\\) that is **different** from the three forbidden slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of all lines already present in \\(\\mathcal F\\) (there are only finitely many such slopes, so such an \\(m\\) exists). Define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell' :\\; y-b_0 = m\\,(x-a_0) .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), the line \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny. It passes through \\((a_0,b_0)\\), a point that was covered by \\(\\ell\\); therefore after removing \\(\\ell\\) this point remains covered by \\(\\ell'\\).\n\nConsider any other point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\((a,b)\\neq (a_0,b_0)\\). Since \\(\\mathcal F\\) covered \\(T_n\\), there exists a line \\(\\ell''\\in\\mathcal F\\) (possibly \\(\\ell\\) itself) that contains \\((a,b)\\). If \\(\\ell''\\neq\\ell\\) then \\(\\ell''\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal F'\\) and continues to cover \\((a,b)\\). If \\(\\ell''=\\ell\\) then \\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(\\ell\\); however \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to a forbidden direction, while \\(\\ell'\\) has a different slope, so \\((a,b)\\) cannot lie on \\(\\ell'\\) unless \\((a,b)=(a_0,b_0)\\). Hence any point other than \\((a_0,b_0)\\) that was covered exclusively by \\(\\ell\\) would become uncovered. To avoid this, we choose \\((a_0,b_0)\\) to be a point of \\(T_n\\) that is **not** the unique point of intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with \\(T_n\\).  \n\nBecause each non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) is either vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\), its intersection with the triangular set \\(T_n\\) contains at least two lattice points (except possibly for the line \\(x=1\\) when \\(n=3\\); this special case is handled separately below). Hence we can select \\((a_0,b_0)\\) such that at least one other point of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) remains covered by a different line of \\(\\mathcal F\\). In the construction of Lemma\u202f1, for example, every horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains many points \\((a,b)\\) with different \\(a\\); the vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains all points \\((1,b)\\); and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains all points \\((a,n+1-a)\\). Thus the required choice is always possible for \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nConsequently after the replacement every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered. The new line \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny, all other lines keep their previous sunny/non\u2011sunny status, and \\(\\ell'\\) is distinct from all existing lines because its slope \\(m\\) was chosen to be different from all slopes already present. Hence the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one: \\(|\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal F'\\}| = t+1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\n1. **Start** with the family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) from Lemma\u202f1. It contains \\(0\\) sunny lines and satisfies the covering condition.\n\n2. **Iterate** the operation described in Lemma\u202f2.  \n   Suppose after \\(r\\) iterations we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_r\\) with exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le r<n\\)). Applying Lemma\u202f2 to \\(\\mathcal L_r\\) yields a family \\(\\mathcal L_{r+1}\\) with \\(r+1\\) sunny lines, still covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Terminate** after \\(k\\) iterations. The resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause the iteration can be performed as long as the number of sunny lines is less than \\(n\\), we obtain such a family for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Verification of edge cases\n\n*For \\(k=n\\).* After \\(n-1\\) applications of Lemma\u202f2 we have a family with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and one remaining non\u2011sunny line. Applying Lemma\u202f2 one more time replaces that last non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line, giving a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines. Distinctness is preserved because each new sunny line is given a slope never used before.\n\n*For \\(n=3\\).* Lemma\u202f1 yields the three lines \\(x=1\\), \\(x+y=4\\) and \\(y=1\\); they cover the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\). The replacement procedure works exactly as described, so all values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are attainable.\n\nThus no exceptional values of \\(k\\) arise.\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\). Hence the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
