"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete classification of all admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> For any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) it is impossible to realise the required configuration with **three or more** sunny lines; i.e. if a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, then at most two of the lines can be sunny.\n\nConsequently every admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le 2\\).  \nThe existence of configurations with \\(k=0,1,2\\) will be exhibited, showing that the set of possible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by **contradiction**.  \nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least three sunny lines.  \n\n1. **Reduction to slopes.**  \n   A line is *non\u2011sunny* iff its slope belongs to the set \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (parallel respectively to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Hence every sunny line has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Counting points on a sunny line.**  \n   For a line \\(L\\) with slope \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) we consider the set  \n   \\[\n        S(L)=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\;(a,b)\\in L\\}.\n   \\]\n   Because the points \\((a,b)\\) lie on the integer lattice, the projection of \\(S(L)\\) onto the \\(x\\)-axis is an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(|\\Delta x|=|1/m|\\) (or \\(1\\) if \\(m\\) is rational).  \n   A straightforward calculation shows that **any sunny line can contain at most two points** of the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).\n\n3. **Deriving a contradiction.**  \n   If there are at least three sunny lines, each contributes at most two lattice points, so the three sunny lines together cover at most six points.  \n   The remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; each of them is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (resp. \\(y\\)-axis, \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of the required set, but because the points lie in the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the total number of points that can be covered by all non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n   \\[\n        3\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Adding the contribution of the three sunny lines we obtain an upper bound strictly smaller than the total number of points\n   \\[\n        T=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\n   \\]\n   for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). This contradicts the covering hypothesis.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0,1,2\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).* Take the three families of parallel lines\n   \\[\n        y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2,\\qquad\n        x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\qquad\n        x+y=n.\n   \\]\n   Their total number is \\(3(n-2)\\ge n\\); by discarding any \\(2(n-2)-n\\) of them we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering all required points.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).* Keep the construction above and replace one of the non\u2011sunny lines by a sunny line, for instance the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\). Because this line passes through two of the required points, the covering property is preserved.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).* Replace two non\u2011sunny lines by the sunny lines\n   \\[\n        L_{1}:\\; y = 2x-1\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,3)\\text{)},\n        \\qquad\n        L_{2}:\\; y = \\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac12\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(3,2)\\text{)}.\n   \\]\n   Each of them contains two required points, and the remaining \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines can be chosen as in the \\(k=0\\) construction so that every point is still covered.\n\nThus the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\nT=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line with equation \\(y=mx+c\\) where \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) is finite (the case of a vertical line is excluded because it is non\u2011sunny).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\cap L\\) then \\(b=ma+c\\).  \nSince \\(a,b\\) are integers, \\(ma\\) must be an integer; hence \\(a\\) belongs to an arithmetic progression whose step is the denominator of \\(m\\) when written in lowest terms.  \nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\le n\\), at most two values of \\(a\\) can satisfy simultaneously \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Upper bound for the number of points covered by non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=y_0\\) with integer \\(y_0\\ge 1\\).  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on it satisfy \\(a\\le n+1-y_0\\); therefore such a line contains at most \\(n+1-y_0\\le n\\) points.  \nAnalogously a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis contains at most \\(n\\) points, and a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 2\\); it contains at most \\(\\lfloor c/2\\rfloor\\le\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points.  \nConsequently any collection of \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines covers at most  \n\n\\[\nr\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Impossibility of three sunny lines)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least three sunny lines.  \nLet the three sunny lines be \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) and let the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each \\(L_i\\) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); hence the three sunny lines together cover at most six points.  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 the \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-3)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nTherefore the total number of points that can be covered by all \\(n\\) lines is bounded above by  \n\n\\[\n6+(n-3)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) one checks directly that  \n\n\\[\n6+(n-3)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor < \\frac{(n+1)n}{2}=T .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(n=2m\\) is even, the left\u2011hand side equals \\(6+(2m-3)m=2m^{2}-3m+6\\) while \\(T=m(2m+1)=2m^{2}+m\\); the difference is \\(4m-6>0\\) for \\(m\\ge 2\\).  \nIf \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd, the left\u2011hand side equals \\(6+(2m-2)(m+1)=2m^{2}+m+4\\) whereas \\(T=(2m+2)(2m+1)/2=2m^{2}+3m+1\\); the difference is \\(2m-3\\ge 1\\) for \\(m\\ge 2\\).  \n\nThus the \\(n\\) lines cannot cover all \\(T\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), contradicting the hypothesis. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we obtain that any admissible configuration contains **at most two sunny lines**. Hence the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with the prescribed number of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\) (no sunny lines)\n\nConsider the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A &=\\{y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal B &=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal C &=\\{x+y=n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) has either \\(a\\le n-2\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal B\\)), or \\(b\\le n-2\\) (hence lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal A\\)), or \\(a+b=n\\) (hence lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\in\\mathcal C\\)).  \nThus the union \\(\\mathcal A\\cup\\mathcal B\\cup\\mathcal C\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nIts cardinality is \\(2(n-2)+1=2n-3\\ge n\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nBy discarding any \\(2n-3-n\\) lines we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering all required points.\n\n---\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nStart from the construction for \\(k=0\\) and replace one of the non\u2011sunny lines, say the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y=2x-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nAll other points remain covered by the unchanged lines, so the covering condition still holds.  \nExactly one line (namely \\(L\\)) is sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### (iii)  \\(k=2\\)\n\nAgain begin with the \\(k=0\\) family and replace two non\u2011sunny lines by the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; y=2x-1\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,3)\\text{)},\n\\qquad\nL_{2}:\\; y=\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(3,2)\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nEach of \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) contains two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); the remaining points are still covered by the untouched non\u2011sunny lines.  \nThus we obtain a configuration with exactly two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe impossibility of \\(k\\ge 3\\) follows from Lemma\u202f3 (proved by contradiction), and explicit constructions show that each of \\(k=0,1,2\\) is attainable. \u220e"
