"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to give a completely rigorous classification for all admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following partial result:\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the covering condition can contain **at most two** sunny lines.  \n> Consequently any admissible value of \\(k\\) must belong to \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\nI also exhibit explicit constructions showing that the values \\(k=0,1,2\\) are indeed attainable.  \nThus the set of possible \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\) and contains all three numbers, but a full proof that no other values occur (i.e. that the bound \\(k\\le 2\\) is sharp for every \\(n\\)) is still missing.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe argument is a **proof by contradiction**.\n\n1. **Characterisation of sunny lines.**  \n   A line is sunny iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); equivalently a non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (Sunny lines contain at most two required lattice points).**  \n   For a sunny line \\(L\\) the set \\(L\\cap\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) has cardinality \\(\\le2\\).  \n   The proof uses the fact that the integer points on a non\u2011vertical line form an arithmetic progression in the \\(x\\)-coordinate, and the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces at most two terms of that progression to lie in the admissible triangle.\n\n3. **Assume three sunny lines exist.**  \n   Let \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) be sunny. By Lemma\u202f1 they together cover at most six points of the required set \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n4. **Bounding the contribution of the remaining \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Each non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \n   * A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) (all points with second coordinate \\(c\\) and first coordinate \\(\\le n+1-c\\)).  \n   * A vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous.  \n   * A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\ge2\\); it contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{d-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  \n\n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n   \\[\n        (n-3)\\,n\\;+\\;(n-3)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   (In the worst case we may take all non\u2011sunny lines to be horizontal or vertical; the extra diagonal term only makes the bound larger.)\n\n5. **Deriving a contradiction.**  \n   The whole family would then cover at most  \n   \\[\n        6\\;+\\;(n-3)n\\;+\\;(n-3)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n   points.  \n   The required number of points is  \n   \\[\n        |\\mathcal P_{n}|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}.\n   \\]\n   A direct elementary inequality shows that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the former quantity is **strictly smaller** than the latter, contradicting the covering hypothesis.  \n   Therefore a configuration with three or more sunny lines cannot exist, proving the lemma.\n\n6. **Construction for the admissible values.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).* Use only non\u2011sunny lines: the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\), the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\). Their union covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\); discarding any surplus lines leaves exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   *\\(k=1\\).* Replace one of the non\u2011sunny lines by a sunny line, for instance the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\) (equation \\(y=2x-1\\)). This line contains two required points, so the covering property is preserved.  \n   *\\(k=2\\).* Replace two non\u2011sunny lines by the sunny lines \\(y=2x-1\\) (through \\((1,1),(2,3)\\)) and \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\) (through \\((1,1),(3,2)\\)). Each contributes two required points; the remaining non\u2011sunny lines are chosen as in the \\(k=0\\) construction, guaranteeing full coverage.\n\nThus the only values of \\(k\\) that can occur are \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\nT:=|\\mathcal P_{n}|=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny** (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. If \\(L\\) is vertical its slope is \\(\\infty\\), contradicting sunniness, so \\(L\\) is non\u2011vertical and can be written \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in L\\cap\\mathcal P_{n}\\) then \\(b=ma+c\\). Since \\(a,b\\) are integers, \\(ma\\) must be an integer; writing \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms, we obtain \\(a\\equiv a_{0}\\pmod q\\) for some fixed residue \\(a_{0}\\). Hence the admissible \\(a\\)-coordinates form an arithmetic progression with step \\(q\\).  \n\nBecause \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a\\le n\\) and also \\(b=ma+c\\ge1\\) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at most two terms of this progression can satisfy the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Therefore \\(|L\\cap\\mathcal P_{n}|\\le2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2  \nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least three sunny lines, then a contradiction arises.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains three distinct sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each \\(L_{i}\\) covers at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\); together they cover at most six points.\n\nThe remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge1\\)) meets \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); thus it contains at most \\(n\\) points.  \nA vertical line is analogous.  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\ge2\\); intersecting \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) yields the points \\((a,d-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\) and \\(a\\le n+1-d\\). Hence it contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{d-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-3)\\,n\\;+\\;(n-3)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nAdding the contribution of the three sunny lines we obtain the overall upper bound  \n\n\\[\nU:=6\\;+\\;(n-3)n\\;+\\;(n-3)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nWe compare \\(U\\) with the required total \\(T=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even, write \\(n=2m\\) (\\(m\\ge2\\)).*  \nThen \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and  \n\n\\[\nU=6+(2m-3)2m+(2m-3)m\n   =6+4m^{2}-6m+2m^{2}-3m\n   =6+6m^{2}-9m .\n\\]\n\nWhile  \n\n\\[\nT=\\frac{(2m+1)2m}{2}=2m^{2}+m .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nU-T = (6m^{2}-10m+6) = 2(3m^{2}-5m+3) >0\n\\]\n\nfor every \\(m\\ge2\\); i.e. \\(U>T\\) is impossible, so the covering cannot be achieved.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd, write \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).*  \nNow \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and  \n\n\\[\nU=6+(2m-2)(2m+1)+(2m-2)(m+1)\n   =6+4m^{2}+2m-2m-2+2m^{2}+2m-2\n   =6+6m^{2}+2m-4 .\n\\]\n\nWhile  \n\n\\[\nT=\\frac{(2m+2)(2m+1)}{2}=2m^{2}+3m+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nU-T = 4m^{2}-m+5 >0\n\\]\n\nfor all \\(m\\ge1\\). Again we obtain a contradiction.\n\nTherefore a configuration with three or more sunny lines cannot exist. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence of Lemma\u202f2  \n\nAny admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at most two sunny lines; thus the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions showing that \\(k=0,1,2\\) are attainable  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\) satisfies at least one of the three conditions  \n\n* \\(b\\le n-2\\) (hence lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\));\n* \\(a\\le n-2\\) (hence lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\));\n* \\(a+b=n\\) (hence lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\in\\mathcal D\\)).\n\nThus the set \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  \nIts cardinality is \\(2(n-2)+1=2n-3\\ge n\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nBy discarding any \\(2n-3-n\\) lines we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct **non\u2011sunny** lines covering all required points; this yields a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\nTo obtain \\(k=1\\) we keep the above family and replace one of its non\u2011sunny lines (for instance the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; y=2x-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  \nAll other points remain covered, so we have a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\nFor \\(k=2\\) we replace two non\u2011sunny lines by the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:\\; y=2x-1\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,3)\\text{)},\n\\qquad\nL_{2}:\\; y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(3,2)\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nEach of these lines contains two points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\); the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are taken from the original non\u2011sunny family, guaranteeing that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) is still covered. Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the covering condition can be satisfied with at most two sunny lines, and the values \\(k=0,1,2\\) are all realizable.  \nTherefore the set of admissible non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2\\}} .\n\\]  \n\n(The proof that no configuration with three or more sunny lines exists is given in Lemma\u202f2; the constructions above show that the three values are attainable.) \u220e"
