 \\]\n\n   where the integers \\(c_i\\) are distinct and satisfy \\(-\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\le c_i\\le \\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  \n   Each line \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the points of \\(S\\) with \\(a-b=c_i\\); the number of such points is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i = n+1-|c_i| \\ge 1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   After removing from \\(S\\) all points already covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, the remaining points lie on at most \\(n-k\\) horizontal, vertical or \\((-1)\\)-slope lines.  \n   Indeed, for each remaining integer \\(b\\) (resp. \\(a\\)) we take the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (resp. the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) if it still contains uncovered points; if a point \\((a,b)\\) is still uncovered and \\(a+b\\) is constant, we use the line \\(x+y = a+b\\).  \n   Because each such line contains at least one uncovered point, we need at most one line for each distinct value of \\(b\\), of \\(a\\) or of \\(a+b\\).  \n   The total number of distinct values that can appear is at most \\(n-k\\) (there are exactly \\(n\\) possible values of \\(b\\), of \\(a\\) and of \\(a+b\\) altogether, and we have already used \\(k\\) values for the sunny lines).  \n   Hence we can select \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines that together cover all the still uncovered points.\n\n   The resulting family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)\u2019s) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, and it covers every point of \\(S\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Since a construction exists for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and no value outside this interval can occur (obviously we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines among \\(n\\) lines), the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1   Counting the points of \\(S\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions in positive integers.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2   Non\u2011sunny lines and their capacity\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{v}:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); therefore it contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) contains exactly \\(n+1-a\\le n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) contains exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nThus **any** non\u2011sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3   No restriction on the number of sunny lines\n\nWe have to show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\nThe pigeonhole principle will be used only to guarantee that we never need more than \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines (see \u00a72.4).  \nThere is no lower bound on \\(k\\) coming from a counting argument: the total number of points \\(|S|\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), while the maximal number of points that can be covered by a single non\u2011sunny line is \\(n\\); with \\(n\\) lines we can always cover \\(|S|\\) even if all of them are non\u2011sunny. Consequently any \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is a priori admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4   Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4.1   The sunny lines\n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nc_{1},c_{2},\\dots ,c_{k}\\qquad\\text{with}\\qquad \n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le c_{i}\\le \n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2.4.1}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = x + c_{i}. \\tag{2.4.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{i}\\) is \\(1\\); therefore each \\(L_{i}\\) is **sunny** (it is not parallel to the forbidden directions).  \nThe line \\(L_{i}\\) contains precisely the points of \\(S\\) satisfying \\(a-b=c_{i}\\).  \nBecause of the bounds (2.4.1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a-b=c_{i}\\}\\,| = n+1-|c_{i}|\\ge 1 .\n\\tag{2.4.3}\n\\]\n\nThus every sunny line covers at least one point of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4.2   Removing the points already covered\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{0}=S\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_{i}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S_{0}\\) has a difference \\(a-b\\) that is **not** among the chosen numbers \\(c_{1},\\dots ,c_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4.3   Covering the remaining points with non\u2011sunny lines\n\nConsider the three families of non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* horizontal lines \\(H_{b}: y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\));\n* vertical lines \\(V_{a}: x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\));\n* diagonal lines \\(D_{c}: x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).\n\nFor each point \\((a,b)\\in S_{0}\\) at least one of the three lines \\(H_{b},V_{a},D_{a+b}\\) passes through it.  \nWe now select a **minimal** sub\u2011family \\(\\mathcal N\\) of non\u2011sunny lines that covers all points of \\(S_{0}\\).  \nBecause each line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) contains at least one point of \\(S_{0}\\), the number of lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\) does not exceed the number of distinct values taken by the triples \\((a,b,a+b)\\) on \\(S_{0}\\).\n\nObserve that the set of possible values of \\(b\\) is \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); similarly for \\(a\\) and for \\(a+b\\).  \nSince the \\(k\\) sunny lines have already exhausted exactly \\(k\\) distinct values of the difference \\(a-b\\), at most \\(n-k\\) distinct values remain among the three parameters \\((a,b,a+b)\\) when we restrict to \\(S_{0}\\).  \nConsequently we can choose at most \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines that together cover \\(S_{0}\\).\n\nFormally, we proceed as follows.\n\n* For each \\(b\\) such that there exists \\((a,b)\\in S_{0}\\) and such that no vertical or diagonal line has already been chosen for that point, add the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\) to \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n* After processing all possible \\(b\\), repeat the same procedure with the vertical lines \\(V_{a}\\) for the still uncovered points.\n* Finally, any point that is still uncovered must have a unique value of \\(a+b\\); we add the corresponding diagonal line \\(D_{a+b}\\).\n\nBecause each step removes at least one uncovered point, the process stops after at most \\(n-k\\) additions.  \nThus we obtain a set \\(\\mathcal N\\) of **non\u2011sunny** lines with  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\le n-k .\n\\tag{2.4.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4.4   Completing the family\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L = \\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nBy construction  \n\n* \\(|\\mathcal L| = k + |\\mathcal N| = n\\) (if \\(|\\mathcal N|<n-k\\) we may add arbitrary extra non\u2011sunny lines that are parallel to already used ones; this does not affect the covering property);\n* exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny;\n* every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct because the \\(L_{i}\\) have distinct intercepts \\(c_{i}\\) and the lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\) belong to three families with distinct defining constants.\n\nHence for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced a suitable configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5   Exhaustion of possibilities\n\n*If \\(k>n\\)*, we would need more sunny lines than the total number of lines, which is impossible.  \n*If \\(k<0\\)*, the number of sunny lines would be negative, also impossible.\n\nTherefore the admissible values are precisely  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
