L_0} L\\cap\\mathbb Z^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c : x+y=c\\qquad(c=3,4,\\dots ,n+1),\n\\]\n\nand add a single sunny line that passes through the remaining uncovered point\n\\((1,1)\\).  \nFor concreteness choose  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}} : y-1=2(x-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(2\\) and therefore is sunny.  \n\nThe set of points covered by the diagonal lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{c=3}^{n+1}\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=c,\\;a,b\\ge 1\\}\n   =T_n\\setminus\\{(1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\((1,1)\\in L_{\\text{sun}}\\), the union of the \\(n\\) lines\n\\(\\mathcal L_1:=\\{L_{\\text{sun}}\\}\\cup\\{L_c\\mid 3\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) equals \\(T_n\\).  \nExactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) is sunny, so \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_a :\\; y-1=\\frac{a}{a+1}\\,(x-a).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{a}{a+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), never undefined, and never \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny.  \nThe two points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a+1,2)\\) satisfy (1), so they belong to \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nBecause the equation of a line is linear, all points of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a+i-1,i)\\qquad(i\\ge 1)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nalso lie on \\(\\ell_a\\) as long as they satisfy the line equation, which they do by construction.\n\nNow let \\((x,y)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(y=1\\) then \\((x,1)=(a,1)\\) with \\(a=x\\) and belongs to \\(\\ell_x\\).  \nIf \\(y\\ge 2\\) set \\(a:=x-y+1\\).  \nSince \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a = x-y+1 \\le n,\n\\]\n\nso \\(a\\) is a valid index.  \nMoreover \\((x,y)=(a+y-1,y)\\) is of the form (2) with \\(i=y\\); hence \\((x,y)\\in\\ell_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  \nThe lines are distinct because they have distinct slopes \\(\\frac{a}{a+1}\\).  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n:=\\{\\ell_a\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for the three values  \n\n\\[\nk=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad k=n,\n\\]\n\nshowing that each of them can occur for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThe existence (or non\u2011existence) of configurations with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) has not been settled by the arguments presented here. \u220e"
