(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=3,4,\\dots ,n+1),\n\\]\n\nall of which are non\u2011sunny.  \nThese lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except \\((1,1)\\), because the only integer solution of \\(x+y=2\\) with \\(x,y\\ge1\\) is \\((1,1)\\).\n\nChoose any sunny line passing through \\((1,1)\\); for concreteness let  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}}:\\;y-1=2(x-1)\n\\]\n\n(which has slope \\(2\\) and therefore is sunny).  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\{L_{\\text{sun}}\\}\\cup\\{L_c\\mid 3\\le c\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) contains exactly one sunny line, the others are non\u2011sunny, and by construction its union contains all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. No configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nWe prove that any sunny line meets the set \\(T_n\\) in **at most one** lattice point.\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line and suppose that it contains two distinct points\n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\).  \nThen the direction vector of \\(L\\) is proportional to the integer vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v=(a_2-a_1,\\;b_2-b_1)\\neq(0,0).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause both points belong to \\(T_n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na_1+b_1\\le n+1,\\qquad a_2+b_2\\le n+1.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting gives  \n\n\\[\n(a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)= (a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)\\in\\{-n,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf the line \\(L\\) were parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) then its direction vector would satisfy \\(a_2-a_1=-(b_2-b_1)\\), i.e. the sum in (3) would be zero for every pair of points on the line.  \nSince \\(L\\) is sunny, its direction vector cannot satisfy \\(a_2-a_1=-(b_2-b_1)\\); therefore the integer\n\\((a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)\\) is **non\u2011zero**.\n\nConsequently the two points lie on two different level sets of the function\n\\((x,y)\\mapsto x+y\\).  \nBut a line that is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can intersect each level set\n\\(\\{x+y=c\\}\\) in at most one point.  \nHence a sunny line can contain at most one point of each set \\(\\{x+y=c\\}\\).\n\nSince the points of \\(T_n\\) are partitioned by the values of \\(c=a+b\\) into the\n\\(n\\) distinct level sets \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\), a sunny line can meet at most one of these level sets, i.e. at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nNow suppose we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) and containing \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines.  \nEach sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(T_n\\); therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe remaining \\(|T_n|-k\\) points must be covered by the other \\(n-k\\) lines.  \nBut each non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; such a line can lie on at most one of the level sets \\(\\{x+y=c\\}\\).  \nHence a non\u2011sunny line can cover **all** points of at most one level set, i.e. at most \\(c-1\\) points, where \\(c\\) is the corresponding constant.  \nIn particular, a single non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\), but to cover the \\(|T_n|-k\\) points left after the sunny lines we would need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|T_n|-k}{n}\\ge \\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k}{n}= \\frac{n+1}{2}-\\frac{k}{n}\n\\]\n\nnon\u2011sunny lines.  \nFor \\(k\\ge 2\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\) this lower bound exceeds \\(n-k\\), contradicting the assumption that only \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are available.\n\nTherefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines can satisfy the covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\), and we have proved that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  \nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\,1\\}} .\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an