y line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\) (step\u202f2).  \n   Starting from \\(S_{0}\\) we repeatedly pick a non\u2011sunny line that passes through at least one still uncovered point, add it to our family, and delete all points of \\(S_{0}\\) lying on that line.  \n   Because each chosen line removes at most \\(n\\) points, after at most \\((n-k)\\) selections all points of \\(S_{0}\\) are eliminated, by (2).  \n   Thus we obtain a set \\(\\mathcal N\\) of at most \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines that together cover \\(S_{0}\\).\n\n6. **Final family of \\(n\\) lines.**  \n   Let  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n   \\]\n   If \\(|\\mathcal N|<n-k\\) we add arbitrary extra non\u2011sunny lines (parallel to any already used one) so that \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).  \n   By construction  \n\n   * \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_{i}\\)\u2019s);\n   * every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n   * all lines are distinct.\n\n   Hence a configuration exists for the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\n7. **Exhaustion of possibilities.**  \n   Because we have exhibited a construction for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and obviously we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines among \\(n\\) lines, the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1   Number of points to be covered\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions in positive integers.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2   Non\u2011sunny lines contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\)\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to one of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{v}:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal* \\(y=b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); therefore at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Vertical* \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Diagonal* \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) contains exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most \\(n\\) points. \\(\\square\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3   Sunny lines that we shall use\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nc_{1},c_{2},\\dots ,c_{k}\\qquad\\text{such that}\\qquad \n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le c_{i}\\le \n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2.3.1}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\;y=x+c_{i}. \\tag{2.3.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{i}\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently none of the three forbidden directions is parallel to \\(L_{i}\\); each \\(L_{i}\\) is **sunny**.\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\) iff \\(a-b=c_{i}\\).  \nBecause of (2.3.1) we have \\(|c_{i}|\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\); therefore the number of solutions of \\(a-b=c_{i}\\) inside \\(S\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}=n+1-|c_{i}|\\ge n+1-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n      =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{2.3.3}\n\\]\n\nHence each sunny line covers at least \\(\\lceil (n+3)/2\\rceil\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4   Points that remain uncovered after the sunny lines\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{0}=S\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_{i}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.1) and (2.3.3),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|S_{0}|\n   &\\le \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil  \\\\\n   &\\le (n-k)\\,n . \\tag{2.4.1}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(The second inequality follows from the elementary estimate\n\\(\\displaystyle \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n      \\le (n-k)n\\), which is true for all \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5   Covering \\(S_{0}\\) with at most \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nWe apply a greedy procedure.\n\n*Step 1.*  If \\(S_{0}\\) is empty we are done.  \nOtherwise pick any point \\((a,b)\\in S_{0}\\).  \nChoose a non\u2011sunny line that passes through this point; for definiteness we may take the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (any of the three admissible directions works).  \nAdd this line to a set \\(\\mathcal N\\) and delete from \\(S_{0}\\) all points lying on it.\n\n*Step 2.*  Repeat Step\u202f1 with the new (smaller) set of uncovered points.\n\nEach iteration removes at least one point and, by Section\u202f2.2, removes **at most** \\(n\\) points.  \nConsequently after at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|S_{0}|}{n}\\le \\frac{(n-k)n}{n}=n-k\n\\]\n\niterations the set \\(S_{0}\\) becomes empty.  \nThus we obtain a collection \\(\\mathcal N\\) of non\u2011sunny lines with  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\le n-k . \\tag{2.5.1}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\) are distinct because each new line is chosen to contain a point that was not covered before.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6   The final family of \\(n\\) lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal N|<n-k\\) we adjoin arbitrary extra non\u2011sunny lines (for instance additional horizontal lines with unused \\(y\\)-coordinates); this does not affect the covering property.  \nNow \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\nProperties of \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n\n* Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{i}\\) have slope \\(1\\); hence precisely \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n* By construction every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n* All lines are distinct (the \\(L_{i}\\) have distinct intercepts, the lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\) were chosen successively to contain a previously uncovered point).\n\nThus for the given \\(k\\) we have produced a family satisfying the two requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7   Exhaustion of admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n*If \\(k>n\\)* we would need more sunny lines than the total number of lines, which is impossible.  \n*If \\(k<0\\)* the number of sunny lines would be negative, also impossible.\n\nSince we have exhibited a construction for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), the set of admissible values is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
