r \\(c\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL(c):\\;y=x+c . \\tag{2.3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L(c)\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(L(c)\\) is sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on \\(L(c)\\) iff \\(a-b=c\\).  \nConsequently, for two distinct integers \\(c\\neq c'\\),\n\n\\[\nL(c)\\cap S\\;\\cap\\;L(c')\\cap S=\\varnothing . \\tag{2.3.2}\n\\]\n\nHence the subsets of \\(S\\) cut out by different lines \\(L(c)\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\nFor a given \\(c\\) the conditions \\(a-b=c,\\;a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) are equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\max\\{1,1+c\\}\\le a\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1+c}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|L(c)\\cap S| = n+1-|c| . \\tag{2.3.3}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, if \\(|c|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) then  \n\n\\[\n|L(c)\\cap S|\\ge n+1-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n               =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{2.3.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4   Choice of the sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(k\\le n-1\\).*  \nThe interval  \n\n\\[\nI=\\Bigl[-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\;\n          \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr]\n\\]\n\ncontains at least \\(n-1\\) distinct integers; therefore we can select\n\\(k\\) distinct integers \\(c_{1},\\dots ,c_{k}\\in I\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}=L(c_{i})\\qquad (1\\le i\\le k).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny and, by (2.3.2), their intersections with \\(S\\) are disjoint.\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n\\).*  \nIf \\(n\\) is odd, the interval \\(I\\) already contains \\(n\\) integers, so we proceed as in Case\u202f1 with \\(k=n\\).  \nIf \\(n\\) is even, \\(I\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) integers. We take the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(L(c)\\) with \\(c\\in I\\) and add one further sunny line of a different slope, for instance  \n\n\\[\nL_{n}: y=2x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=2x\\) is sunny (its slope \\(2\\) is distinct from \\(0,\\,\\infty,\\,-1\\)) and does not coincide with any \\(L(c)\\).\n\nIn every sub\u2011case we have obtained exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, denoted \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5   Points of \\(S\\) still uncovered after the sunny lines\n\nBecause the \\(k\\) lines of slope\u202f1 are pairwise disjoint on \\(S\\),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_{i}\\cap S\\Bigr|\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{k}|L_{i}\\cap S|\n   \\ge k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\n\\]\n\nthe inequality being strict when the extra sunny line \\(y=2x\\) is present (it covers at most \\(n\\) points, which only strengthens the bound).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_{0}=S\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_{i}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n|S_{0}|\n   \\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\n        k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\le (n-k)\\,n . \\tag{2.5.1}\n\\]\n\nThe second inequality follows from the elementary estimate  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+3}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\le n^{2}-kn=(n-k)n,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds for all \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6   Covering the remaining points with non\u2011sunny lines\n\nWe now construct a family \\(\\mathcal N\\) of non\u2011sunny lines that covers \\(S_{0}\\).\n\nWhile \\(S_{0}\\neq\\varnothing\\) :\n\n* pick any point \\((a,b)\\in S_{0}\\);\n* choose a non\u2011sunny line that passes through \\((a,b)\\) (for definiteness the horizontal line \\(y=b\\));\n* add this line to \\(\\mathcal N\\) and delete from \\(S_{0}\\) all points of \\(S_{0}\\) lying on the chosen line.\n\nBy Section\u202f2.2 each added line removes at most \\(n\\) points from \\(S_{0}\\).  \nConsequently after at most  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|S_{0}|}{n}\\le\\frac{(n-k)n}{n}=n-k\n\\]\n\niterations the set \\(S_{0}\\) becomes empty.  \nThus we obtain a collection \\(\\mathcal N\\) of at most \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_{0}\\).\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal N|<n-k\\) we may append arbitrary extra non\u2011sunny lines (for instance additional horizontal lines with unused \\(y\\)-coordinates) so that the total number of non\u2011sunny lines equals exactly \\(n-k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7   The final family of \\(n\\) lines\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nBy construction  \n\n* \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\\);\n* exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are sunny;\n* every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* all lines are distinct (the sunny lines have distinct equations, and the non\u2011sunny lines were chosen successively to contain a previously uncovered point).\n\nHence for the given integer \\(k\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem and having precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8   Exhaustion of admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nObviously we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines among \\(n\\) lines, so no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
