\n\nAll \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny, and by (2.3.2) the subsets \\(L_{i}\\cap S\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5   Points of \\(S\\) that remain uncovered after the sunny lines\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nS_{0}=S\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_{i}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (2.3.3),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}L_{i}\\cap S\\Bigr|\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|c_{i}|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\ge k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(n-1)}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =k .\n\\]\n\nTherefore, by (2.1),\n\n\\[\n|S_{0}|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|c_{i}|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2-k .\n\\tag{2.5.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6   Covering the remaining points with non\u2011sunny lines\n\nRecall from Section\u202f2.2 that any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nWe construct a family \\(\\mathcal N\\) of non\u2011sunny lines as follows.\n\nWhile \\(S_{0}\\neq\\varnothing\\):\n\n* pick any point \\((a,b)\\in S_{0}\\);\n* take the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which is non\u2011sunny);\n* add this line to \\(\\mathcal N\\) and delete from \\(S_{0}\\) all points lying on it.\n\nEach iteration removes at most \\(n\\) points, because a horizontal line meets \\(S\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (Section\u202f2.2).  \nConsequently after at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|S_{0}|}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{2.6.1}\n\\]\n\niterations the set \\(S_{0}\\) becomes empty.\n\nFrom (2.5.1) we have \\(|S_{0}|\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2-k\\).  \nWe now prove the crucial inequality\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{\\frac{n(n+1)}2-k}{\\,n\\,}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le n-k\n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3,\\;0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2.6.2}\n\\]\n\n**Proof of (2.6.2).**  \nWrite the left\u2011hand side as  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}-\\frac{k}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), the term \\(\\frac{k}{n}\\) lies in \\([0,1]\\